; Nolang doc catalog: en
; Source-comment translations, keyed by the anchor an author wrote in a
; trailing `[label](#anchor)` line. This file holds TRANSLATIONS ONLY: an
; anchor that is absent falls back to the comment text in the .no source, so
; untranslated blocks degrade to the author's language instead of shipping a
; copy of it here. Every key must still exist in some .no source, or the gate
; rejects it as an orphan.
{
  'aes': `
aes — AES block cipher core (AES-128 and AES-256)

Provides S-box, GF(2^8) arithmetic, round functions and key expansion; the common base for the entire AES family.
Working modes are not here; they are in aes-cbc.no / aes-ctr.no / aes-gcm.no in the same directory.

Layered usage (applies at the mode layer): expand the key once, then encrypt/decrypt block by block
ek []byte = aes.key-expand(key [16]byte)       ; 176 bytes, AES-128
ek []byte = aes.key-expand-256(key [32]byte)   ; 240 bytes, AES-256
out [16]byte = aes.enc-block(block [16]byte, ek)
out [16]byte = aes.dec-block(block [16]byte, ek)

Single-block usage (with built-in key expansion, equivalent to an ECB single block):
out [16]byte = aes.enc-128(block [16]byte, key [16]byte)
out [16]byte = aes.dec-128(block [16]byte, key [16]byte)
out [16]byte = aes.enc-256(block [16]byte, key [32]byte)
out [16]byte = aes.dec-256(block [16]byte, key [32]byte)

note: the round count of enc-block / dec-block is derived from the ek length (nr = ek.len() / 16 - 1),
so AES-128 (10 rounds) and AES-256 (14 rounds) share the same round loop.
`
  'aes-cbc': `
aes-cbc — AES-CBC encryption/decryption mode (AES-128 and AES-256)

Implements CBC (Cipher Block Chaining) mode on top of the AES block cipher.
CBC encryption: C_i = AES_enc(P_i ⊕ C_{i-1}), C_0 = IV
CBC decryption: P_i = AES_dec(C_i) ⊕ C_{i-1}, C_0 = IV

Key length is not a file dimension: the same CBC logic serves both AES-128 and AES-256;
the only difference is whether the entry point uses aes.key-expand or aes.key-expand-256.

usage:
iv [16]byte
key [16]byte
padded = aes-cbc.pkcs7-pad(plain)
cipher = aes-cbc.enc-128(padded, key, iv)
plain  = aes-cbc.dec-128(cipher, key, iv)
n      = aes-cbc.pkcs7-unpad(plain)
; for AES-256 use key [32]byte with enc-256 / dec-256
`
  'aes-cbc.aes-block-size': 'AES block size'
  'aes-cbc.cbc-dec': `
CBC decryption core (round keys expanded once by the caller)
Each block runs AES-ECB decryption first, then XORs with the previous ciphertext segment; padding is not verified.
in: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 16)
iv: initial vector, must be the same as used for encryption
ek: round keys expanded by aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256
returns out: decrypted plaintext, same length as in (still carrying PKCS#7 padding)
`
  'aes-cbc.cbc-enc': `
CBC encryption core (round keys expanded once by the caller)
Each block XORs with the previous ciphertext segment first, then runs AES-ECB; no padding is applied, trailing bytes short of 16 are discarded.
in: input data (length must be a multiple of 16)
iv: initial vector; must never be reused under the same key
ek: round keys expanded by aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256
returns out: encrypted ciphertext, same length as in
`
  'aes-cbc.dec-128': `
AES-128-CBC decryption
in: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 16)
key: 128-bit key, must match the one used for encryption
iv: initial vector, must match the one used for encryption
returns out: decryption result (still contains PKCS#7 padding, remove it with pkcs7-unpad)
`
  'aes-cbc.dec-256': `
AES-256-CBC decryption
in: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 16)
key: 256-bit key, must match the one used for encryption
iv: initial vector, must match the one used for encryption
returns out: decryption result (still contains PKCS#7 padding, remove it with pkcs7-unpad)
`
  'aes-cbc.enc-128': `
AES-128-CBC encryption
Expands the 16-byte key then runs cbc-enc; no padding is applied, the plaintext length must be padded to a multiple of 16 by the caller.
in: input data (length must be a multiple of 16)
key: 128-bit key
iv: initial vector, should be random and unique per message
returns out: encrypted ciphertext, same length as in
`
  'aes-cbc.enc-256': `
AES-256-CBC encryption
Uses a 32-byte (14-round) key; all other behavior is the same as enc-128.
in: input data (length must be a multiple of 16)
key: 256-bit key
iv: initial vector, should be random and unique per message
returns out: encrypted ciphertext, same length as in
`
  'aes-cbc.pkcs7-pad': `
PKCS#7 padding
Always appends 1-16 bytes each valued n (when the plaintext is already a multiple of 16, a full 16-byte block is appended).
in: input data
returns out: padded data (length is a multiple of 16 and strictly greater than the original)
`
  'aes-cbc.pkcs7-unpad': `
PKCS#7 unpadding
Reads the last byte as the padding length and verifies that the trailing pad-len bytes all equal that value.
in: decrypted data (with PKCS#7 padding; length 0 is treated as a padding error)
out-n: valid length after unpadding; -1 when the padding value is invalid, out of range, or fails verification
`
  'aes-ctr': `
aes-ctr — AES-CTR counter mode (AES-128 and AES-256)

CTR mode turns a block cipher into a stream cipher.
The counter is incremented and encrypted, and the resulting keystream is XORed with the plaintext; encryption and decryption are the same operation.

Key length is not a file-level dimension: the same CTR logic serves both AES-128 and AES-256,
the only difference is whether the entry point uses aes.key-expand or aes.key-expand-256.

usage:
iv [16]byte
key [16]byte
cipher = aes-ctr.crypt-128(plain, key, iv)   ; encrypt
plain  = aes-ctr.crypt-128(cipher, key, iv)  ; decrypt (same function)
; AES-256 instead uses key [32]byte and crypt-256
`
  'aes-ctr.crypt-128': `
AES-128-CTR encrypt/decrypt (symmetric operation)
Calling twice with the same key and the same iv (nonce) restores the plaintext; never reuse a (key, iv) combination.
in: input data (any length)
key: 128-bit key
iv: initial counter value (nonce), must be unique
returns out: output with the same length as in
`
  'aes-ctr.crypt-256': `
AES-256-CTR encrypt/decrypt (symmetric operation)
Same behavior as crypt-128, except the key is expanded to 32 bytes (14 rounds).
in: input data (any length)
key: 256-bit key
iv: initial counter value (nonce), must be unique
returns out: output with the same length as in
`
  'aes-ctr.ctr-crypt': `
CTR encryption core (round keys expanded once by the caller)
Each counter value is encrypted then XORed with the plaintext, so any length works and one function serves both encryption and decryption.
in: input data (any length; the trailing partial block only XORs the first n bytes)
iv: initial counter value; must never be reused under the same key
ek: round keys expanded by aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256
returns out: output with the same length as in (encryption or decryption result)
`
  'aes-ctr.ctr-inc': `
Counter increment (big-endian: the low 32 bits are added first, carries propagate to higher bytes)
cb: 16-byte counter block, modified in place; only the last 4 bytes (indices 12-15) are touched
`
  'aes-gcm': `
aes-gcm — AES-GCM AEAD encryption/decryption (NIST SP 800-38D, AES-128 and AES-256)

GCM = AES-CTR (counter mode) + GHASH (GF(2^128) authentication).
counter increment logic reuses aes-ctr.ctr-inc; key length is not a file dimension,
the only difference is whether the entry uses aes.key-expand or aes.key-expand-256.

usage:
key [16]byte
iv [12]byte
aad []byte = 'additional data'
plain []byte = 'plaintext'
sealed = aes-gcm.seal-128(key, iv, aad, plain)   ; ciphertext + 16-byte tag
opened = aes-gcm.open-128(key, iv, aad, sealed)  ; plaintext, length 0 on authentication failure
; for AES-256 use key [32]byte and seal-256 / open-256
`
  'aes-gcm.ctr-xor': `
Generate the keystream with AES-CTR and XOR it with the data
icb: initial counter block J0+1
data: data to XOR with the keystream, its length is the output length (may be empty)
ek: result of aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256 (the expanded round keys)
returns out: data XORed with the keystream, same length as data
`
  'aes-gcm.gcm-h': `
Compute the hash subkey H = AES-enc(0^128)
ek: AES expanded key (round-key schedule)
h: hash subkey (AES-enc(0^128))
`
  'aes-gcm.gcm-j0': `
Build J0 = iv || 0x00000001 from a 12-byte IV
iv: 12-byte nonce
j0: pre-counter block (iv concatenated with 0x00000001)
`
  'aes-gcm.gcm-open': `
GCM decryption core
iv: 12-byte IV
aad: additional authenticated data
sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
ek: result of aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256
returns plain: plaintext (length 0 when authentication fails)
`
  'aes-gcm.gcm-seal': `
GCM encryption core
iv: 12-byte IV (nonce)
aad: additional authenticated data
plain: plaintext
ek: result of aes.key-expand / aes.key-expand-256
returns sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
`
  'aes-gcm.ghash': `
Compute GHASH
h: hash subkey (AES-enc(0^128))
aad: additional authenticated data
cipher: ciphertext
returns out: GHASH value
`
  'aes-gcm.ghash-mul': `
GF(2^128) multiplication (used by GHASH)
X = X * H mod P, where P = x^128 + x^7 + x^2 + x + 1
x: multiplicand (128-bit block of big-endian bits)
h: hash subkey
returns out: product X * H mod P
`
  'aes-gcm.open-128': `
AES-128-GCM decryption
key: 16-byte key
iv: IV
aad: additional authenticated data
sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
returns plain: plaintext (length 0 when authentication fails)
`
  'aes-gcm.open-256': `
AES-256-GCM decryption
key: 32-byte key
iv: IV
aad: additional authenticated data
sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
returns plain: plaintext (length 0 when authentication fails)
`
  'aes-gcm.seal-128': `
AES-128-GCM encryption
key: 16-byte key
iv: IV (nonce)
aad: additional authenticated data
plain: plaintext
returns sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag (length = plain.len() + 16)
`
  'aes-gcm.seal-256': `
AES-256-GCM encryption
key: 32-byte key
iv: IV (nonce)
aad: additional authenticated data
plain: plaintext
returns sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
`
  'aes.add-round-key': `
XOR the state with the round key
state: 16-byte state (XORed in place with the first 16 bytes of the round key)
rk: round-key array
`
  'aes.add-round-key-at': `
XOR the state with the round key starting at byte off
state: 16-byte state (XORed in place)
rk: round-key array
off: starting byte offset of this round's key within rk (usually 16×round index)
`
  'aes.dec-128': `
Decrypt one 16-byte block (AES-128)
in: 16-byte ciphertext block
key: 16-byte key (AES-128)
out: 16-byte plaintext block
`
  'aes.dec-256': `
Decrypt one 16-byte block (AES-256)
in: 16-byte ciphertext block
key: 32-byte key (AES-256)
out: 16-byte plaintext block
`
  'aes.dec-block': `
Decrypt one 16-byte block with pre-expanded round keys
in: input block
ek: round keys (result of key-expand / key-expand-256)
returns out: decrypted block
`
  'aes.enc-128': `
Encrypt one 16-byte block (AES-128)
in: 16-byte plaintext block
key: 16-byte key (AES-128)
out: 16-byte ciphertext block
`
  'aes.enc-256': `
Encrypt one 16-byte block (AES-256)
in: 16-byte plaintext block
key: 32-byte key (AES-256)
out: 16-byte ciphertext block
`
  'aes.enc-block': `
Encrypt one 16-byte block with pre-expanded round keys
in: input block
ek: round keys (result of key-expand / key-expand-256)
returns out: encrypted block
`
  'aes.gf-mul2': `
Multiply by x (the polynomial x) in GF(2^8)
x: byte value 0..255
r: result of x·{02} in GF(2^8) (reduced by 0x11b on left-shift overflow)
`
  'aes.gf-mul3': `
Multiply by {03} in GF(2^8) (i.e. x+1)
x: byte value in 0..255
r: result of x·{03}
`
  'aes.inv-mix-col': `
Inverse mix of a single column (using 9, 11, 13, 14)
b0: byte 0 of the column
b1: byte 1 of the column
b2: byte 2 of the column
b3: byte 3 of the column
d0: byte 0 after inverse mix ({14}·b0 ⊕ {11}·b1 ⊕ {13}·b2 ⊕ {09}·b3)
d1: byte 1 after inverse mix ({09}·b0 ⊕ {14}·b1 ⊕ {11}·b2 ⊕ {13}·b3)
d2: byte 2 after inverse mix ({13}·b0 ⊕ {09}·b1 ⊕ {14}·b2 ⊕ {11}·b3)
d3: byte 3 after inverse mix ({11}·b0 ⊕ {13}·b1 ⊕ {09}·b2 ⊕ {14}·b3)
`
  'aes.inv-mix-columns': `
Inverse-mix all columns of the state (coefficients {14,11,13,09}, used in decryption rounds)
state: 16-byte state (each 4 consecutive bytes form a column, inverse-mixed in place column by column)
`
  'aes.inv-shift-rows': `
Inverse shift rows (right rotation)
state: 16-byte state matrix (row i is cyclically shifted right by i bytes, in place, used for decryption)
`
  'aes.inv-sub-bytes': `
Substitute using the inverse S-box
state: 16-byte state (the first 16 elements are replaced one by one via the inverse S-box)
n: reserved parameter (the implementation always processes 16 bytes; unused)
`
  'aes.key-expand': `
Expand a 16-byte key into 176 bytes of round keys (AES-128, 11 round-key words)
key: 128-bit key
returns ek: 176-byte round keys, fed directly to enc-block / dec-block
`
  'aes.key-expand-256': `
Expand a 32-byte key into 240 bytes of round keys (AES-256, 15 round-key words)
key: 256-bit key
returns ek: 240-byte round keys
`
  'aes.mix-col': `
Mix a single column (4 bytes)
b0: byte 0 of the column
b1: byte 1 of the column
b2: byte 2 of the column
b3: byte 3 of the column
d0: byte 0 after mixing ({02}·b0 ⊕ {03}·b1 ⊕ b2 ⊕ b3)
d1: byte 1 after mixing (b0 ⊕ {02}·b1 ⊕ {03}·b2 ⊕ b3)
d2: byte 2 after mixing (b0 ⊕ b1 ⊕ {02}·b2 ⊕ {03}·b3)
d3: byte 3 after mixing ({03}·b0 ⊕ b1 ⊕ b2 ⊕ {02}·b3)
`
  'aes.mix-columns': `
Mix all columns of the state
state: 16-byte state (each 4 consecutive bytes form a column, mixed in place column by column)
`
  'aes.rcon-val': `
rcon: round constant
round: round number (1..14)
r: the round constant x^(round-1) for this round (i.e. 1, 2, 4, …, 0x6b, 0xd8, 0xab, 0x4d; other round numbers return 0)
`
  'aes.rot-word': `
Rotate a 32-bit word left by 8 bits (RotWord: the most significant byte moves to the low end)
w: 32-bit word (interpreted in big-endian byte order)
r: rotated 32-bit word
`
  'aes.shift-rows': `
Shift rows (row i is rotated left by i bytes)
state: 16-byte state matrix (each 4 consecutive bytes form a row, row i is cyclically shifted left by i bytes, in place)
`
  'aes.sub-bytes': `
Substitute every byte of the state using the S-box
state: 16-byte state (the first 16 elements are replaced one by one via the S-box)
n: reserved parameter (the implementation always processes 16 bytes; unused)
`
  'aes.sub-word': `
substitutes each of the 4 bytes of a 32-bit word via the S-box (SubWord)
w: 32-bit word
r: 32-bit word reassembled after substitution
`
  'argon2': `
argon2 — Argon2id memory-hard key derivation function (RFC 9106)

Argon2 is the winner of the Password Hashing Competition.
Argon2id is a hybrid mode, resisting both time and space attacks.

Uses Blake2b as the underlying hash function.

usage:
# std/crypto/argon2
password []byte = 'password'
salt []byte = 'somesalt'
// parameter: time_cost=3, memory_cost=4096, parallelism=1, output_len=32
derived = id(password, salt, 3, 4096, 1, 32)
`
  'argon2.argon2-block-size': 'block size (1024 bytes = 128 × 8 bytes)'
  'argon2.argon2-id': 'Argon2 type'
  'argon2.argon2-mask': '64-bit all-ones mask'
  'argon2.argon2-version': 'Argon2 version'
  'argon2.blake-g': `
Blake2b G function (the core mixing step of the Argon2 compression function)
Modifies the 64-bit words at indices a/b/c/d of the v array in place (pairwise add, XOR, rotate).
v: 16-word workspace (Argon2 block), modified in place
a: index a (0..15)
b: index b
c: index c
d: index d
x: reserved parameter (unused in this implementation; the multipliers needed for mixing are computed on the fly from the low 32 bits of v[a]/v[b])
y: reserved parameter (unused in this implementation)
No return value: the result stays in v[a]/v[b]/v[c]/v[d]
`
  'argon2.compress': `
Argon2 compression function G(X, Y)
First writes X XOR Y into scratch r, runs one blake-g round over each 16-word row and column of r,
then XORs the result back with the original r into out.
x: 1024-byte block X
y: 1024-byte block Y
returns out: 1024-byte compression result
`
  'argon2.h-prime': `
Variable-length Blake2b (H'): Argon2 uses it to stretch a block digest into an arbitrary-length output
input: input data (usually a 64-byte Blake2b digest)
out-len: desired output byte length; <= 64 needs a single Blake2b pass
out: the derived result, exactly out-len bytes
`
  'argon2.id': `
Argon2id key derivation (RFC 9106, Argon2id v=0x13)
First computes the block count m (rounded down to a multiple of 4*parallelism, at least 8*parallelism),
initializes h0, fills blocks lane by lane, runs time-cost mixing rounds, then extracts out-len bytes.
password: password (UTF-8 byte sequence, may be empty)
salt: salt, at least 8 bytes and unique per account
time-cost: time cost (number of passes, e.g. 3); larger is slower and safer
memory-cost: memory cost in KB (e.g. 65536 = 64MB); too small is raised to 8*parallelism blocks
parallelism: degree of parallelism (e.g. 1); must be >= 1 and sets the rounding granularity of the block count
out-len: output key length in bytes (commonly 32)
returns out: the derived key, exactly out-len bytes long
`
  'argon2.rotr': `
rotr64: 64-bit cyclic right shift
x: the 64-bit word to rotate
n: shift amount (1-63; 0 or 64 leaves the shift undefined)
r: (x >> n) | (x << (64-n)), keeping only the low 64 bits
`
  'args': `
args — command-line argument parsing

relies on os.args() / os.arg() for low-level access.
provides higher-level interfaces such as flag detection, option reading, and positional arguments.
`
  'args.count': `
number of arguments, including the program name
delegates to the os.args() builtin, which returns the whole argv vector; the length of that vector is the count
The returned count includes argv[0] (the program name), so the minimum is 1 with no extra arguments.
n: total number of argv elements (command-line argument count + 1)
`
  'args.get': `
Get the i-th raw argument (no flag / positional parsing at all)
Delegates to the os.arg(i) builtin (reads main's argv[i]).
i: index, 0 is the program name, 1 onward are the user-supplied arguments
val: ok=argv[i], nil=index out of range (negative or ≥ argument count)
`
  'args.get-option': `
reads an option value (e.g. --output=xxx)
walks argv[1..] comparing each, finding the parameter that starts with key followed immediately by '=' (0x3D),
Returns the remainder after '=' (a byte slice, may be an empty string). Takes only the first match.
key: option key (with leading - or --, e.g. '--output')
val: ok=the value after '=' (may be ''), nil=no parameter matches key=...
`
  'args.get-positional': `
Get the idx-th positional argument (0-based), skipping all flags along the way
Scan argv[1..]: parameters starting with '-' are always skipped; if it is the single-dash form (like -o),
it also skips the one following parameter not starting with '-' (treated as the option's value);
double-dash forms (like --output=xxx) only skip themselves. Other parameters are counted in order of appearance.
idx: positional argument ordinal, 0 is the first non-flag argument
val: ok=the idx-th positional argument, nil=not enough positional arguments
`
  'args.has-flag': `
reports whether a flag is present (e.g. --verbose, -v)
Compare each argv[1..] element against flag character by character exactly (not substring matching, and no --k=v expansion).
An empty flag string never matches.
flag: the full flag string to compare (with leading - or --)
yes: true=argv contains an element exactly equal to flag, false=absent
`
  'args.program': `
program name (parameter 0, i.e. argv[0] verbatim)
name: ok=argv[0] (usually the executable path, may include directories), nil=argv is empty
`
  'arr': `
arr — array operations

provides generic operations for fixed-size arrays.
generic parameters are inferred with single letters a-z.

a slice is a view, not a distinct type: slicing an arr (e.g. a[1..3]) produces a []t (vec) view,
sharing arr's underlying memory without copying data.
therefore all vec methods (len, push, pop, contains, reverse, clone, fill, to-arr, etc.)
apply to arr slice views. Modifying elements through a slice affects the original arr.
`
  'arr-stack': `
arr-stack — generic stack (backed by a fixed-size array)

LIFO (last-in-first-out) data structure, using a [n]t fixed-size array as underlying storage.
top points to the next write position (also equal to the current element count).

generic design: value operations (push/pop/peek) are defined on [n]t;
generic parameters n (array size) and t (element type) are inferred automatically by the compiler.
stack state (cap/top) is stored separately in the arr-stack struct.

differences from stack:
- arr-stack uses generic [n]t, supporting any element type (i64/u8/byte, etc.)
- stack supports only []i64
- arr-stack's pop/peek return a ?t option, stack returns (val, ok)

usage:
buf [128]i64
s = buf.arr-stack-init()
ok = buf.arr-stack-push(s, 42)
ok = buf.arr-stack-push(s, 7)
val = buf.arr-stack-pop(s)      // val = ok (last-in-first-out)
val = buf.arr-stack-pop(s)      // val = ok
val = buf.arr-stack-pop(s)      // val = nil (stack empty)
sz = s.size()
yes = s.empty()

// option unwrapping
val = buf.arr-stack-pop(s)
val: {
nil -> print('empty')
-> print(it)          // it = the popped value
}
`
  'arr-stack.arr-stack': `
arr-stack: stack state (no buffer; the buffer is provided by the [n]t receiver)
cap: capacity (= array length n)
top: stack top index (next write position, also equal to the element count)
`
  'arr-stack.arr-stack.clear': 'Clears the stack'
  'arr-stack.arr-stack.empty': `
Reports whether the stack is empty
Returns yes: true = empty, false = not empty
yes: true=top <= 0 (nothing pushed yet or already cleared)
`
  'arr-stack.arr-stack.full': `
Reports whether the stack is full
Returns yes: true = full, false = not full
yes: true=top >= cap (another push would fail or overflow, pop first)
`
  'arr-stack.arr-stack.size': `
Returns the number of elements on the stack
Returns sz: the element count
sz: current stack depth (the top pointer), excluding emptied slots already popped
`
  'arr.arr': '`[n]t` is a bare aggregate `[N x T]` at the LLVM level: elements are inline, no len/cap fields, no heap pointer.\n`.len` does not read memory; the compiler replaces it directly with the compile-time constant n (see [n]t.len below).\ntherefore only one pseudo-field, len, is declared here; there is no cap and no data.\nfor cases needing runtime length/capacity, use a slice `[]t` instead (see the vec declaration in std/vec.no).'
  'arr.arr-stack-init': `
[n]t.arr-stack-init: initialises the stack state
cap is set to the array size n automatically
returns s: the initialised stack state
`
  'arr.arr-stack-peek': `
[n]t.arr-stack-peek: looks at the top element without removing it
s: the stack state
returns val: ok = the top value, nil = the stack is empty
`
  'arr.arr-stack-pop': `
[n]t.arr-stack-pop: pops the element at the top of the stack
s: the stack state
returns val: ok = the popped value, nil = the stack is empty
`
  'arr.arr-stack-push': `
[n]t.arr-stack-push: pushes an element onto the top of the stack
s: the stack state
val: the value to push
returns ok: true = success, false = the stack is full
`
  'arr.asc': '[n]t.asc: sorts ascending in place (insertion sort)'
  'arr.at': `
