  'async': `
async — coroutines / async tasks and cancellation primitives (D10)

⚠️ Deprecated (hand-written primitives): run / awy / cancel / cancelled are deprecated,
do not hand-write them in application code. Manually managing task handles is unsafe (not awaiting leaks the argument buffer,
awaiting a copied handle twice crashes), and cooperative cancellation cannot forcibly interrupt long blocking calls;
use "coroutine groups" instead (a bare block { ... } in statement position). This file is kept for low-level reference only.

nolang provides a cooperative, single-threaded, stackless async coroutine model:
- `awy f-async(args)`  synchronously waits for an -async function to finish (may suspend the current task within a coroutine).
- `run f-async(args)`  starts an -async function as a background task, immediately returning an opaque
task handle (underlying i8*), without blocking the current coroutine.
- `awy h`              waits for background task h to finish and returns its result.

The real "cancellation" primitives (previously missing) are as follows:
- `cancel(h)`        cancels task h: sets the %task.cancelled flag.
When the event loop schedules h, async_wrapper checks the flag at entry,
and if set, skips the target function and marks done=true (graceful abort),
so the waiter (awy h) can return immediately.
- `cancelled()`      cooperative self-cancellation check: returns whether the currently executing task has been cancelled.
Async functions (e.g. Agent Loop) should call it at the start of every step,
and return early if it returns true, thereby "exiting promptly after external cancellation".

note (limits of cooperative cancellation):
Long blocking calls (e.g. a single LLM network request) cannot be forcibly interrupted. After cancel sets the flag,
the task actually stops only at the "next cooperative checkpoint" (a cancelled() call, or the event loop's next scheduling).
Cancellation is therefore "prompt" rather than "instantaneous" - an inherent property of the cooperative scheduling model.

usage example:
// start a background task and cancel it later
h = run worker-async(...)
...
cancel(h)        ; request cancellation
r = awy h              ; wait (returns immediately if cancelled, r is the zero value)

// cooperative check inside an async function
worker-async = (n i64) (r i64) {
loop i in 0..n {
if cancelled() { return 0 }   ; exit early if cancelled
r = r + step(i)
}
r
}
`
  'async.cancel': `
⚠️ deprecated: a hand-rolled cancel is deprecated; use a coroutine group (a bare block { ... }) instead.
cancel: cancels a background task started by run()
task is the opaque handle run() returned (i8*). Returns void.
task: a pointer
`
  'async.cancelled': `
⚠️ deprecated: a hand-rolled cancelled is deprecated; use a coroutine group (a bare block { ... }) instead.
cancelled: reports whether the async task currently executing has been cancelled
returns bool. Only meaningful inside an async task (an -async function scheduled by the event loop).
cancelled: true=the current task has been asked to cancel and should release resources and return soon; false=not cancelled
`
  'base32': `
base32 — Base32 encoding/decoding (RFC 4648)

Encodes byte data as a Base32 string (A-Z, 2-7).
Every 5 bytes are encoded as 8 characters, padding the remainder with '='.

usage:
encoded = encode(data)
decoded = decode(encoded)
`
  'base32.base32-alphabet': 'Base32 alphabet'
  'base32.decode': `
Base32 decode (the result is truncated according to the number of trailing '=' padding characters; characters outside the alphabet are treated as 0 without an error)
Note: leftover characters whose length is not a multiple of 8 are ignored.
in: Base32-encoded string (usually ASCII)
out: decoded raw bytes, length already reduced by the bytes taken by padding
`
  'base32.encode': `
Base32 encode (RFC 4648 standard alphabet, 5 bits per group, padded with '=' to a multiple of 8)
data: input bytes to encode
out: encoded string, length is a multiple of 8 (empty input yields empty string)
`
  'base64': `
encoding/base64 — Base64 encoding/decoding (RFC 4648)

provides encoding/decoding for both the standard and URL-safe alphabets.
`
  'base64.b64-val': `
Converts a single Base64 character to its value
The padding character '=' returns 0; characters outside the alphabet return -1
c: the character code point to look up
table: the 64-character alphabet; the matching index is found by linear scan
v: 0..63 is an alphabet index, -1 is an invalid character
`
  'base64.base64-std': 'standard Base64 alphabet'
  'base64.base64-url': 'URL-safe Base64 alphabet'
  'base64.decode': `
Decodes Base64 using the given alphabet
s: Base64 string (should be a multiple of 4; a trailing partial group is ignored)
table: the same 64-character alphabet as used when encoding
out: ok=decoded data (shortened per the '=' padding count), err=input contains characters outside the alphabet
`
  'base64.encode': `
Encodes Base64 using the given alphabet
data: the input bytes to encode
table: the 64-character alphabet (standard is A-Za-z0-9+/); URL-safe alphabets use -_ instead of +/
out: encoded string, length a multiple of 4, padded with '=' as needed
`
  'base64.encode-std': `
Encodes using the standard alphabet (A-Za-z0-9+/)
data: the input bytes to encode
out: standard Base64 string ('=' padded)
`
  'base64.encode-url': `
Encodes using the URL-safe alphabet (A-Za-z0-9-_)
data: the input bytes to encode
out: URL-safe Base64 string ('=' padding retained)
`
  'base64.pad': 'padding character \'=\''
  'bigint': `
bigint — arbitrary-precision integer

Represented as a base 2^32 little-endian []i64 slice.
Each i64 element stores one 32-bit limb, with the high limb at the leading position.
Negative numbers are marked by the sign field (sign-magnitude representation).

Shares the same underlying []i64 representation with crypto/rsa.no;
this module provides a full big-number arithmetic library.
`
  'bigint._add': `
a + b (operator overload; written ?bigint, the compiler treats it as bigint)
route C: union formal parameter `(b big)` + type-destructuring match. The bigint arm delegates to `add-bigint`, the i64 arm
Delegates to `_add-i64`. The core is not inlined inside the type arm: measured, doing this on a named output inside a match arm's BlockStatement
For formal parameter `c`, doing `c = expr` then `c.sign = ...` does not write the field mutation back (the sign is lost, e.g. -100+7
wrongly yielding 93); after delegating to a normal method the `c.sign` mutation semantics return to normal (same rationale as _mul delegating to mul-bigint).
b: right operand (bigint or i64, the arm is chosen statically by the actual type)
c: the sum self + b (?bigint is required by the operator-overload signature; the addition is always ok)
`
  'bigint._add-i64': `
c = self + v (small-integer addition)
v: a small integer addend
c: self + v (v lifted via from-i64, then bigint addition)
`
  'bigint._div': `
a / b (operator overload; division by zero returns zero per div-mod; the route-C i64 arm goes through _div-i64)
b: divisor (bigint or i64)
q: truncated quotient (division by zero returns 0 instead of erroring, consistent with div-mod)
`
  'bigint._div-i64': `
q = self / v (small-integer division)
v: a small integer divisor (absolute value taken for per-limb long division)
q: quotient of self / v (sign follows self, independent of v's sign)
`
  'bigint._mod': `
a % b (operator overload; division by zero returns zero per div-mod; the route-C i64 arm goes through _mod-i64,
Its result is ?i64; unpack first (return 0 on failure, aligning with div-mod returning zero on divide-by-zero) then lift via from-i64
bigint to align with the output parameter r)
b: divisor (bigint or i64)
r: remainder (sign follows the dividend self; division by zero returns 0)
`
  'bigint._mod-i64': `
r = self % v (modulo on small integers)
Returns ?i64: by D6's unified option rule `cur % r-v` is already ?i64 (fails when the divisor is 0),
declaring the result parameter plain would flatten it and cause silent unwrapping, so it is declared ?i64 faithfully.
v: a small integer divisor (the option fails when 0)
r: the non-negative remainder of |self| % |v|
`
  'bigint._mul': `
a * b (operator overload; route C same as _add, both arms delegate to concrete methods)
the bigint arm delegates to mul-bigint and the i64 arm to _mul-i64. The core is not inlined inside the type arm because
heavy loops / `with-len` / `#{overflow=wrap}` nested inside a match arm's BlockStatement
's lowering boundary crashes (measured segfault); after delegating to a plain method, the `.` receiver and the output
formal-parameter semantics return to normal, taking the same shape as the working _add i64 arm.
b: the multiplier (bigint or i64)
c: the product self × b
`
  'bigint._mul-i64': `
self * v (small-integer multiplication)
v: a small integer multiplier (may be negative; |v| ≥ 2^32 splits automatically into two 32-bit limbs)
c: self × v (the sign flips with the sign of v)
`
  'bigint._sub': `
a - b (operator overload; delegation strategy is the same as _add)
b: the subtrahend (bigint or i64)
c: the difference self - b
`
  'bigint.abs-add': `
|self| + |b| (signs ignored)
b: the addend bigint
c: |self| + |b| (signs not handled, for _add to compose internally)
`
  'bigint.abs-cmp': `
Compares |self| with |b| (1 = greater, 0 = equal, -1 = smaller)
b: the bigint to compare
r: 1=|self| greater, 0=absolute values equal, -1=|self| smaller (signs ignored)
`
  'bigint.abs-sub': `
|self| - |b| (assumes |self| >= |b|, signs ignored)
b: the subtrahend bigint (precondition: |self| >= |b|)
c: |self| - |b| (the result is never negative; unreliable when the precondition fails)
`
  'bigint.add-bigint': `
self + b: the big⊕big core (an ordinary concrete method, not operator overloading; delegated by _add)
Naming the actual argument: the s in `v.abs-sub(s)` must first be captured with `s = .` (a bare `.` as a method argument has an existing lowering gap).
b: right operand bigint
c: the sum self + b (sign composed, for delegation from the _add bigint arm)
`
  'bigint.base': '2^32'
  'bigint.big': `
the "union parameter" type for operator overloading (route C). Mixed operands like `a + 1` are
statically dispatched by `_add = (b big)` + type-destructuring match on b's concrete type; a right-side i64 scalar takes the
i64 arm (via the existing `_*-i64` private methods), bigint takes the same-type arm (original big⊕big core).
private convention: small-integer fast paths such as `_add-i64` / `_mul-i64` / `_div-i64` / `_mod-i64` are for
internal use of this module only; other modules must use operator overloading (`+ - * / %`, i.e. `_add`/`_sub`/`_mul`/`_div`/`_mod`).
`
  'bigint.bigint': `
Type definition
bigint: arbitrary-precision integer
`
  'bigint.cmp': `
Compares self with b (1 = greater, 0 = equal, -1 = smaller)
b: the bigint to compare
r: 1=self greater, 0=equal, -1=self smaller (compare sign first, then absolute value)
`
  'bigint.copy': `
Deep-copy a bigint
⚠️ Not deprecated (unlike str/txt.copy): assignment `b = a` only copies the {sign,limbs,len} descriptor,
shares the same limbs buffer with a (measured: after a.limbs[0] = 99, b.limbs[0] is 99 too),
it has no deep-copy semantics. An independent snapshot must use this method.
out: a new bigint fully independent of self (limbs copied one by one, mutually unaffected)
`
  'bigint.div-mod': `
self / b, returning both the quotient and the remainder
b: divisor (when 0, both quotient and remainder return a zero bigint)
q: the quotient (truncating division)
r: remainder (sign follows the dividend; equals self itself when |self| < |b|)
`
  'bigint.eq': `
Is self == b?
b: the comparison target
r: whether sign and absolute value are all equal
`
  'bigint.from-i64': `
Builds a bigint from an i64
v: the original i64 value (may be negative)
out: the equivalent bigint (sign-magnitude representation)
`
  'bigint.from-u64': `
Builds a bigint from an unsigned i64 (always positive)
v: an unsigned integer value (always treated as non-negative)
out: non-negative bigint (sign always 1)
`
  'bigint.gcd': `
g = gcd(self, b) (Euclidean algorithm)
b: the other integer
g: the greatest common divisor (always positive; 0 when both are zero)
`
  'bigint.is-neg': `
Whether the value is negative
r: whether the sign field is -1 (zero counts as non-negative)
`
  'bigint.is-pos': `
Whether the value is positive
r: whether it is positive and non-zero
`
  'bigint.is-zero': `
Whether the value is zero
r: whether the value is exactly 0
`
  'bigint.lcm': `
l = lcm(self, b)
b: the other integer
l: least common multiple = self/gcd × b (0 when either is zero)
`
  'bigint.mask': '32-bit mask'
  'bigint.mod-pow': `
r = self^exp mod modulus
exp: the exponent bigint (scanned bit by bit, arbitrary precision supported)
modulus: modulus bigint (returns 0 directly when it is 0)
r: self^exp mod modulus (square-and-multiply modular exponentiation, the core of RSA signing/encryption)
`
  'bigint.mul-bigint': `
The bigint core of |self| * |b| (a plain concrete method, not operator overloading; for _mul to delegate to)
b: the multiplier bigint (its absolute value feeds the core multiplication)
c: |self| × |b| (the sign is composed by the delegating _mul from both operands)
`
  'bigint.normalize': 'Strips leading zeros and guarantees len >= 1'
  'bigint.one': `
Builds a bigint holding one
out: a bigint equal to 1
`
  'bigint.pow': `
c = self^n (n >= 0, a non-negative integer power)
n: non-negative exponent (returns 1 when n=0)
c: self^n (square-and-multiply fast exponentiation, O(log n) multiplications)
`
  'bigint.shl': `
c = a << n
a: the bigint being shifted
n: left shift amount
c: a × 2^n (equivalent to repeated ×2)
`
  'bigint.shl1': 'self = self << 1'
  'bigint.shr': `
c = a >> n
a: the bigint being shifted
n: right shift amount
c: a / 2^n (floor, equivalent to repeated ÷2)
`
  'bigint.shr1': 'self = self >> 1'
  'bigint.sub-bigint': `
The big-minus-big core of self - b (a plain concrete method; _sub delegates here)
b: the subtrahend bigint
c: the difference self - b (for delegation from the _sub bigint arm)
`
  'bigint.to-hex': `
Formats a bigint as a lowercase hexadecimal string
returns out: hexadecimal string (lowercase, no 0x prefix, negatives start with '-')
`
  'bigint.to-i64': `
Why it is needed: fast paths for small integers like `_mod-i64` / `_div-i64` are private to this module (see
The private convention above). External modules receive the operator results (`% / * + -`, whose ranges are naturally bounded by the operands
a bigint within i64 range; to get the i64 back you must go through this public outlet instead of detouring into private methods.
v: ok=an i64 within range, err=out of i64 range (high bits never silently truncated)
`
  'bigint.to-str': `
Formats a bigint as a decimal string
returns out: decimal string (negatives start with '-', zero is "0")
`
  'bigint.zero': `
Builds a zero bigint
out: a bigint equal to 0 (sign=1, limbs=[0])
`
  'blake2': `
b — BLAKE2b hash algorithm (RFC 7693)

BLAKE2b is optimized for 64-bit platforms, with a maximum output of 64 bytes.
block size 128 bytes, 12 rounds, using 64-bit words.

usage:
# std/crypto/b
hash = b(data)           // [64]byte, default output 64 bytes
hash32 = b-256(data)     // [32]byte, output 32 bytes
hex = b-hex(data)        // 128-character hex string
`
  'blake2.b': `
Compute the BLAKE2b hash (64-byte output)
data: input bytes to hash
returns hash: the 64-byte BLAKE2b-512 hash value
`
  'blake2.b-256': `
Compute the BLAKE2b hash (32-byte output)
data: input bytes to hash
returns hash: the BLAKE2b hash truncated to 32 bytes
`
  'blake2.b-256-hex': `
Compute BLAKE2b-256 and return a hex string
data: input bytes to hash
returns hex: the 64-character hexadecimal string of the BLAKE2b-256 hash
`
  'blake2.b-compress': `
Compression function
h: hash state (8 u64 words, updated in place)
block: a single 128-byte input block
t: cumulative byte count processed so far (counter, XORed into v[12..13])
last: whether this is the final block (1 sets the final flag, 0 does not)
`
  'blake2.b-g': `
G function (the mixing function)
v: working vector where mixing happens (updated in place; []u64 for BLAKE2b)
a: index in v of the first mixed element
b: index in v of the second mixed element
c: index in v of the third mixed element
d: index in v of the fourth mixed element
x: first message word (added into a in the first round)
y: second message word (added into a in the second round)
`
  'blake2.b-hex': `
Compute BLAKE2b and return a hex string
data: input bytes to hash
returns hex: the 128-character hexadecimal string of the BLAKE2b-512 hash
`
  'blake2.b-state': `
History: earlier compilers had a silent dropped-write MIR bug with "array slice + option-form indexed write `h[i]: { ok -> it = ... }`",
worked around then with with-len(8) plus a copy loop. The bug disappeared after
512d63b6 (narrow-slot loads fix): array slices with option writes are now correct,
so the plain array-slice syntax is used and no manual copy is needed.
h: freshly copied BLAKE2b initial state (8 u64 words, isolated from the array buffer)
`
  'blake2.blake2b-iv': `
BLAKE2b initial vector (IV, = SHA-512 IV).
IV[0] = 0x6a09e667f3bcc908 is the original value; the parameter block (0x01010000 | out-len) is
XORed into h[0] by b()/b-256() itself; the parameter block must not be pre-merged into the IV.
`
  'blake2.blake2b-mask': '64-bit all-ones mask'
  'blake2.blake2b-sigma': 'permutation table (sigma)'
  'blake2.blake2s-iv': `
BLAKE2s initialization vector (IV) —— same as SHA-256
note: previously misused the ChaCha "expand 32-byte k" constant (0x61707865...), making all BLAKE2s hashes wrong.
`
  'blake2.blake2s-sigma': 'BLAKE2s permutation table (same as BLAKE2b)'
  'blake2.rotr32': `
32-bit cyclic right shift
x: the 32-bit value to rotate right
n: number of bits to rotate right
r: the result after the cyclic right shift
`
  'blake2.rotr64': `
64-bit cyclic right shift
x: the 64-bit value to rotate right
n: number of bits to rotate right
r: the result after the cyclic right shift (64-bit mask applied)
`
  'blake2.s': `
Compute the BLAKE2s hash (32-byte output)
data: input bytes to hash
returns hash: the 32-byte BLAKE2s hash value
`
  'blake2.s-compress': `
BLAKE2s compression function
h: hash state (8 u32 words, updated in place)
block: a single 64-byte input block
t: cumulative byte count processed so far (counter)
last: whether this is the final block (1 sets the final flag, 0 does not)
`
  'blake2.s-g': `
G function
v: working vector where mixing happens (updated in place; []u32 for BLAKE2s)
a: index in v of the first mixed element
b: index in v of the second mixed element
c: index in v of the third mixed element
d: index in v of the fourth mixed element
x: first message word (u32)
y: second message word (u32)
`
  'blake2.s-hex': `
Compute BLAKE2s and return a hex string
data: input bytes to hash
returns hex: the 64-character hexadecimal string of the BLAKE2s hash
`
  'blake2.s-state': `
Same as above, BLAKE2s version (BLAKE2s uses 32-bit words, hence []u32 rather than []u64)
h: freshly copied BLAKE2s initial state (8 u32 words)
`
  'bool': `
bool — boolean type standard library

provides common operations for the bool type.
underlying representation: i1 (1 byte, 0 or 1)
`
  'bool.to-str': `
converts a bool to the string "true" or "false"
out: true → 'true', false → 'false' (length 4 or 5)
`
  'bufio': `
bufio — buffered I/O

provides buffered read/write and line-reading for os.read / os.write.
`
  'bufio.reader': `
reader: buffered reader state
fd: opened file descriptor, consumed by fs.read
buf: buffer provided by the caller (capacity sets the per-read upper limit)
pos: index of the next byte to take from buf
avail: number of bytes filled in buf but not yet consumed
`
  'bufio.reader.close': `
closes the reader (closes only the fd, does not free buf; buf is managed by the caller's scope)
No parameters and no return value; a failed close (e.g. fd already closed) reports no error
`
  'bufio.reader.fill': `
refills the buffer
When there is unconsumed data (avail > 0) reuse it directly; otherwise read once from the fd and reset pos/avail.
ok: true=new data is available in the buffer; false=EOF, read error, or invalid fd position
`
  'bufio.reader.init': `
creates a buffered reader
fd: an open, readable file descriptor
buf: dedicated buffer; its length may be 0, fill() reads using buf.cap
r: the initialized reader (pos=0, avail=0, no eager read)
`
  'bufio.reader.read-byte': `
reads one byte
b: ok=the next byte, nil=EOF or read failure
`
  'bufio.reader.read-line': `
reads a line (stops at '\n'; the newline is not part of the result)
returns the remainder when the line ends without a newline; returns nil when the file is empty and nothing was read this call.
line: ok=the line text (without '\n'), nil=EOF with no leftover content
`
  'byte': `
byte — byte operations

Provides conversion between int64 and []byte.

usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-be()       // out = [0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x12, 0x34]
out = v.to-bytes-le()       // out = [0x34, 0x12, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x00]

data = [0x12, 0x34]
v = data.to-i64-be()        // v = 0x1234
v = data.to-i64-le()        // v = 0x3412

To convert between [n]byte and []byte, use arr.to-vec() / []byte.to-arr()
`
  'byte.hex-lower': 'lowercase hex character table'
  'byte.hex-upper': 'Uppercase hexadecimal character table'
  'bytes.index': `
[]byte.index: searches a []byte for the substring target
returns the first occurrence position, or -1 if not found
`
  'bytes.index-from': `
[]byte.index-from: searches a []byte for the substring target starting at from
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
`
  'bytes.len': `
[]byte.len: returns the length of the byte slice
byte is a distinct type rather than an alias of u8, so []byte.len is not instantiated automatically from []t.len.
`
  'bytes.slice': `
[]byte.slice: takes the sub-slice [start, end)
Returns a new slice
`
  'bytes.to-bytes-str': `
[]byte.to-bytes-str: 1:1 byte-wise copy (keeps the original []byte.to-str semantics).
For scenarios where buf is already a legal UTF-8 byte stream (reading files / sub-process output); no per-codepoint re-encoding is needed.
this function is equivalent to the old []byte.to-str.
`
  'bytes.to-chars': `
⚠️ Compiler limitation: both the legacy and MIR backends currently have incomplete support for []char (i32 elements) slices
(unlike []byte / []i64, []char's len/cap/stride have latent bugs in codegen,
the []char built this way is corrupted out-of-bounds at runtime), so this method is not yet usable.
if you only need the final byte→code-point→UTF-8 string result, just use b.to-str()—
[]byte.to-str already inlines this semantics (each byte is re-encoded as a 0~255 code point in UTF-8), no []char needed.
`
  'bytes.to-hex': '[]byte.to-hex: encodes a []byte as an uppercase hexadecimal string'
  'bytes.to-hex-lower': '[]byte.to-hex-lower: encodes a []byte as a lowercase hexadecimal string'
  'bytes.to-i64-be': '[]byte.to-i64-be: converts big-endian bytes to an int64 (1 to 8 bytes)'
  'bytes.to-i64-le': '[]byte.to-i64-le: converts little-endian bytes to an int64 (1 to 8 bytes)'
  'bytes.to-str': `
⚠️ Semantics change (2026-09-06, at the requester's demand): previously this method was a "1:1 byte-by-byte copy", which was
the contract that buf.to-str() (reads files / subprocess output, buf is already valid UTF-8) depends on.
After switching to "byte→codepoint→UTF-8 re-encode", a legal multi-byte UTF-8 byte stream gets double-encoded and corrupted
(e.g. 0xC3 0xA9 read from disk would be treated as code points 0xC3 and 0xA9 and each re-encoded). To copy verbatim
legal UTF-8 byte streams (file / child-process reading scenarios); switch to the variant that keeps 1:1 copy semantics
b.to-bytes-str() (see below), or use the underlying slice directly.
`
  'bytes.to-txt': `
[]byte.to-txt: converts []byte to txt (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns out: the fixed 256-byte txt struct
`
  'bzip2': `
bzip2 — BZIP2 compression and decompression (pure Nolang implementation)

Implements the BZIP2 decompression algorithm entirely in Nolang,
including the inverse Burrows-Wheeler Transform (BWT), inverse Move-To-Front (MTF),
Huffman decoding, and Run-Length Encoding (RLE) decoding.

supports:
- BZIP2 decompression: full decoding of the .bz2 format

usage:
data = fs.read-file('example.bz2')  ; read the bz2 file as []byte
decompressed = bzip2.decompress(data)

algorithm reference: official bzip2 format specification
https://sourceware.org/bzip2/
`
  'bzip2.bz-bit-reader': `
bz-bit-reader: bzip2 bit reader (MSB-first)
data: raw byte buffer to decode
pos: byte index of the next byte to load into bit-buf
bit-buf: bit buffer; only the unconsumed low bit-cnt bits remain
bit-cnt: number of bits still readable in bit-buf (0 means the next byte must be loaded)
`
  'bzip2.bz-bit-reader.read-bit': `
Read one bit (MSB-first); out of range returns 0
bit: the bit read (0 or 1); after bytes are exhausted it always returns 0 without an error
`
  'bzip2.bz-bit-reader.read-bits': `
Read n bits (MSB-first)
n: number of bits to read consecutively
v: the assembled integer; bits read first go into the high positions
`
  'bzip2.bz-huffman': `
bz-huffman: bzip2-style Huffman decode table (three arrays: base / limit / perm)
limit: maximum code value acceptable for each code length (increasing)
base: starting offset of each code length in perm
perm: symbol table sorted by code length and lexicographic order
min-len: shortest code length; 0 means the table is empty and decoding fails immediately
max-len: longest code length
`
  'bzip2.bz-huffman.build': `
Build a Huffman code table from a code-length array
First counts how many codes of each length exist, then accumulates limit / base, finally fills perm.
lengths: code length of each symbol (0 means the symbol is unused)
alpha-size: alphabet size (upper bound of valid symbols, <= 258)
`
  'bzip2.bz-huffman.decode': `
Decode one symbol
Reads min-len bits first, then extends bit by bit until falling inside the limit range; returns -1 when out of range or the code is too long.
br: bit source (may read 1 extra bit per round)
sym: decoded symbol value, -1 = illegal code or empty table
`
  'bzip2.bz-huffman.decode-symbol': `
Decode one symbol (fixed version: takes the symbol directly via perm[zvec - base[zn]])
Differs from decode only in the final index arithmetic; the rest of the decoding flow is the same; the bzip2 main flow uses this version.
br: bit source (may read 1 extra bit per round)
sym: decoded symbol value, -1 = illegal code or empty table
`
  'bzip2.compress': `
compress data in bzip2 format
data: raw data ([]byte)
out: bzip2-compressed data ([]byte)
note: currently a basic implementation using stored blocks;
full BWT + MTF + Huffman compression is left for a later implementation.
`
  'bzip2.decompress': `
decompresses bzip2-format data
data: bzip2 compressed data ([]byte)
out: decompressed data ([]byte)
`
  'chacha20-poly1305': `
chacha20-poly1305 — ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD encryption (RFC 8439)

ChaCha20 is a stream cipher with a 256-bit key; Poly1305 is a 128-bit MAC.
combined into ChaCha20-Poly1305 AEAD, providing confidentiality and integrity.

usage:
# std/crypto/chacha20-poly1305
key: 256-bit key
nonce: 96-bit nonce
aad: additional authenticated data
plain: plaintext
sealed = chacha20-poly1305-seal(key, nonce, aad, plain)
opened = chacha20-poly1305-open(key, nonce, aad, sealed)
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.cc-mask32': '32-bit all-ones mask'
  'chacha20-poly1305.cc-sigma0': `
ChaCha20 constant "expand 32-byte k"
each 32-bit little-endian value
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.chacha20-block': `
Generate one 64-byte keystream block
key: 256-bit key
counter: block counter
nonce: 96-bit nonce
returns out: keystream
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.chacha20-poly1305-open': `
ChaCha20-Poly1305 decryption
key: key
nonce: nonce
aad: additional authenticated data
sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
returns plain: plaintext (length 0 when authentication fails)
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.chacha20-poly1305-seal': `
ChaCha20-Poly1305 encryption
key: key
nonce: nonce
aad: additional authenticated data
plain: plaintext
returns sealed: ciphertext + 16-byte tag
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.chacha20-xor': `
ChaCha20 stream encrypt/decrypt
key: key
counter: initial counter
nonce: nonce
data: input data
returns out: data after XOR
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.poly1305': `
Compute the Poly1305 MAC
key: one-time key (first 16 bytes are r, last 16 bytes are s)
data: input data
returns tag: 16-byte authentication tag
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.poly1305-key-gen': `
Generate the Poly1305 one-time key
key: ChaCha20 key
nonce: nonce
returns otk: one-time Poly1305 key
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.poly1305-reduce': `
Reduce x into [0, 2^130-5)
Fold-and-reduce using 2^130 ≡ 5 (mod p), relying only on verified bigint operations
Does not use bigint.mod — its div-mod has an unrestored normalize-shift bug for multi-limb divisors
x: large integer to reduce (Poly1305 running product, may far exceed 130 bits)
out: reduced value, within the range [0, 2^130-5)
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.qr': `
quarter-round: the ChaCha20 quarter round
state: ChaCha20's 16-word u32 state array (updated in place)
a: word index of accumulator a (start of the additive operations)
b: word index of accumulator b (provides the addend; later XORed and cyclically left-shifted by 8/7 bits in the round)
c: word index of accumulator c (addend of the second addition)
d: word index of accumulator d (XORed then cyclically left-shifted by 16/12 bits)
`
  'chacha20-poly1305.rotl32': `
32-bit cyclic left shift
x: the 32-bit unsigned value to rotate left
n: shift amount in bits (the caller guarantees it is below 32; no modulo inside the function)
r: result of the cyclic left shift (masked to 32 bits)
`
  'char': `
char — character operations

char is essentially i32 (Unicode code point / rune).
Provides character classification, case conversion, UTF-8 encoding, and other methods.

usage:
c = "A"                  // double quotes = char literal (single-character rune)
yes = c.is-upper()       // true
u = c.to-lower()         // "a"
s = c.to-str()           // s = 'A'(single quotes = str)
`
  'char.is-alnum': `
reports whether the character is a letter or a digit
result: true when the receiver is a letter or a digit
`
  'char.is-alpha': `
reports whether the character is a letter (A-Z a-z)
an alias of is-letter
result: true when the receiver is an English letter (same semantics as is-letter)
`
  'char.is-digit': `
reports whether the character is a digit (0-9)
result: true when the receiver is a digit character (0-9)
`
  'char.is-letter': `
reports whether the character is a letter (A-Z a-z)
result: true when the receiver is an English letter (A-Z or a-z)
`
  'char.is-lower': `
reports whether the character is a lowercase letter (a-z)
result: true when the receiver is a lower-case letter (a-z)
`
  'char.is-space': `
reports whether the character is whitespace (space, tab, newline, cr)
result: true when the receiver is a whitespace character (space/Tab/newline/carriage return)
`
  'char.is-upper': `
reports whether the character is an uppercase letter (A-Z)
result: true when the receiver is an upper-case letter (A-Z)
`
  'char.to-bytes': `
converts to the UTF-8 encoded byte array
out: the 1~4 byte sequence of the code point's UTF-8 encoding
`
  'char.to-lower': `
converts to lower case (ASCII only)
result: the character converted upper→lower (non A-Z returned unchanged)
`
  'char.to-str': `
converts to the UTF-8 encoded string
s: a single-character UTF-8 string built from that code point
`
  'char.to-txt': `
converts to a UTF-8 encoded txt (the fixed 256-byte structure)
Reuses char.to-bytes to obtain the 1 to 4 byte UTF-8 sequence, then copies it into the txt buffer byte by byte.
Consistent with str.to-txt / []byte.to-txt it returns a plain txt (converting a single code point cannot fail, so no ?txt).
With it, txt.to-upper / to-lower and friends can be rewritten as "take a char -> char.to-lower -> char.to-txt",
no longer depending on indexed writes through txt[i], which returns a char.
out: txt loaded with the code point's UTF-8 sequence (remaining bytes zero-filled)
`
  'char.to-upper': `
converts to upper case (ASCII only)
result: the character converted lower→upper (non a-z returned unchanged)
`
  'chars.to-str': `
⚠️ Note: as of 2026-09-06 the nolang compiler's support for the []char slice type is incomplete; this method is not yet usable:
- legacy backend: []char with-cap/push mishandles the len/cap units (element count vs byte count),
makes .len() report too many elements, and []char.to-str then reads out of bounds and produces garbage (measured: a single push(72)
also outputs "H"+garbage). []char literals and index reads themselves have correct i32 stride, but what push constructs
the slice len field is corrupted.
- MIR=3 gate: the compiler supports neither the []char element type nor the new []t.to-chars generic method,
directly reports "unknown callee []t.to-chars" and cannot compile.
Do not rely on to-chars / []char.to-str until the compiler fixes []char slice support.
`
  'client': `
client — high-level TCP client

Wraps conn, symmetric to listener, providing conveniences such as connection-state tracking, reconnect, and request-response mode.
Uses the conn struct from net.no and the net-* built-in functions underneath.

usage:
c = client{}
ok = c.connect('127.0.0.1', 8080)
n = c.send('hello')
n = c.recv(buf, 1024)
c.close()

// request-response mode
response = c.request('PING')              // returns ?str

// reconnect
ok = c.reconnect()

// convenience function
c = client-create('127.0.0.1', 8080)          // returns ?client
`
  'client.client': `
client: high-level TCP client
c: underlying TCP connection (net.conn struct)
host: target host (recorded for reconnect)
port: target port number
connected: connection state
`
  'client.client-create': `
Create a client and connect
returns c: ok = client, nil = failure
host: target server address
port: target server port
`
  'client.client.close': 'Close the connection and reset state'
  'client.client.connect': `
Connect to the given host and port
Records host/port for later reconnect
host: target server address
port: target server port
ok: true=TCP connection established, false=dialing failed
`
  'client.client.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
yes: true=currently in the connected state
`
  'client.client.reconnect': `
Reconnect using the recorded host/port
Closes the old connection first (avoiding fd leaks), then dials again
ok: true=re-dialing with the previously recorded host/port succeeded (returns false directly if never recorded)
`
  'client.client.recv': `
Receive data into a buffer
buf: receive buffer (str)
n: maximum number of bytes to receive
read-n: number of bytes actually received (-1=not connected or error, 0=connection closed)
`
  'client.client.recv-line': `
Receive one line of data (at most NET-BUF-SIZE bytes)
returns line: ok = received content, nil = failure or connection closed
`
  'client.client.request': `
Request-response mode
Sends data then receives the whole response until the connection closes
returns response: ok = response content, nil = failure
data: the request content to send
`
  'client.client.send': `
Send string data
data: byte-content string to send
written: number of bytes actually sent (-1=not connected or underlying error)
`
  'client.client.send-line': `
Send one line (appends the newline \n)
line: line text to send (a newline is appended automatically)
written: number of bytes actually sent (including the newline; -1=not connected or error)
`
  'cookie': `
cookie — HTTP Cookie handling

Provides Cookie parsing, construction, and management (Cookie Jar).
Supports Set-Cookie header parsing, Cookie header construction, Cookie storage and retrieval.
Supports HMAC-SHA256-based signed Cookies to prevent tampering.

usage:
// parse the Set-Cookie header
c = parse-set('sessionid=abc123; Path=/; HttpOnly; Secure')
c.valid -> {
print(c.name)        // 'sessionid'
print(c.value)       // 'abc123'
print(c.path)        // '/'
print(c.http-only)   // true
print(c.secure)      // true
`
  'cookie.build-header': `
Build a Cookie request header
names: array of cookie names (fixed [16]str, first count entries used)
values: matching array of cookie values (fixed [16]str)
count: number of cookie pairs to emit
returns out: concatenated Cookie request header value (e.g. name1=val1; name2=val2)
`
  'cookie.build-set': `
Build a Set-Cookie header value
c: the cookie struct to serialize
returns out: the assembled Set-Cookie header value string
`
  'cookie.cookie': `
cookie: a single Cookie
valid: whether it is valid
name: Cookie name
value: Cookie value
domain: domain
path: path
expires: expiration time (Unix milliseconds, 0=session Cookie)
max-age: maximum lifetime in seconds (-1=not set)
secure: whether HTTPS only
http-only: whether JS access is forbidden
same-site: SameSite policy ('Strict', 'Lax', 'None')
`
  'cookie.cookie-jar-max': 'Cookie Jar maximum capacity'
  'cookie.cookie-sign': `
Sign a cookie value with HMAC-SHA256
Note: the original name sign collided with the num.sign method symbol (cross-module
calls resolved to signum and silently returned 1), hence the cookie- prefix
(matching the url- prefix convention in url.no).
name: cookie name (forms the name=value pair fed into the HMAC)
value: raw cookie value to sign
key: HMAC-SHA256 signing key
returns signed: the signed value (format: value.hex-hmac)
`
  'cookie.cookie-verify': `
Verify a signed cookie
name: cookie name (must match the one used when signing, otherwise the recomputed signature differs)
signed-value: signed value to verify (format: value.hex-hmac)
key: HMAC-SHA256 signing key
returns ok: true when the signature matches (constant-time comparison)
returns value: the original cookie value with the signature stripped (empty when verification fails)
`
  'cookie.jar': `
jar: Cookie store
names: Cookie name array
values: Cookie value array
domains: domain array
paths: path array
count: number of stored Cookies
`
  'cookie.jar.build-header': `
Build a Cookie request header with matching cookies
domain: request target domain (selects cookies matching that domain)
path: request target path (selects cookies whose path prefix matches)
returns out: header string joining all cookies that match the domain/path criteria
`
  'cookie.jar.clear': 'Clear the cookie jar'
  'cookie.jar.count-of': `
Get the cookie count
returns n: number of cookies currently stored in the jar
`
  'cookie.jar.delete': `
Delete a cookie
name: cookie name to delete
domain: owning domain (must match exactly)
path: owning path (must match exactly; deletion requires all of name/domain/path to match)
`
  'cookie.jar.get': `
Get a cookie value
name: cookie name to look up
domain: request target domain (matched exactly, as a subdomain, or via an empty domain)
path: request target path (prefix-matched; '/' matches everything)
returns val: ok = matching cookie value, nil = not found
`
  'cookie.jar.get-verified': `
Get and verify a signed cookie
name: cookie name to look up
domain: request target domain
path: request target path
key: signing key used for verification
returns val: ok = original value with the signature stripped and verified, nil = not found or verification failed
`
  'cookie.jar.parse-response': `
Parse cookies from an HTTP response's Set-Cookie header and store them
set-cookie-str: the response's Set-Cookie header value (a single entry)
domain: response source domain (used as the attribution domain of the stored cookie)
path: response source path (used as the attribution path of the stored cookie)
`
  'cookie.jar.set': `
Set a cookie
A cookie with the same name (+ same domain + same path) is overwritten in place; no new slot is allocated.
name: cookie name
value: cookie value (unsigned raw text)
domain: owning domain, used to match request targets
path: owning path prefix
max-age: maximum lifetime in seconds (-1 = unset, lifetime decided at session level)
`
  'cookie.jar.set-signed': `
Set a signed cookie
Stores the result of cookie-sign(name, value, key); reading it back requires verifying with the same key.
name: cookie name (also feeds into the HMAC)
value: cookie value before signing (signed automatically before being stored in the jar)
domain: owning domain
path: owning path prefix
max-age: maximum lifetime in seconds (-1 = unset)
key: HMAC-SHA256 signing key
`
  'cookie.parse-header': `
Parse a Cookie request header
cookie-header: the request's Cookie header value (e.g. name1=val1; name2=val2)
names: output array filled with cookie names (fixed [16]str)
values: output array filled with cookie values paired with names (fixed [16]str)
returns count: number of cookie pairs actually parsed (<= 16)
`
  'cookie.parse-set': `
Parse a Set-Cookie header value
set-cookie-str: a single Set-Cookie header value (e.g. name=value; Path=/; HttpOnly)
returns c: the parsed cookie struct (fields include name/value/domain/path/max-age/secure)
`
  'crc-16': `
crc16 — CRC-16 cyclic redundancy check (CCITT variant)

uses the CRC-16-CCITT polynomial 0x1021, initial value 0xFFFF.

usage:
h = new(data)
`
  'crc-16.hex': `
Compute CRC-16 and return a 4-digit lowercase hex string
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
hex: fixed 4-character hexadecimal representation (big-endian, no 0x prefix)
`
  'crc-16.new': `
Compute the CRC-16-CCITT checksum (polynomial 0x1021, initial value 0xFFFF)
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
crc: 16-bit checksum (0..65535)
`
  'crc-32': `
crc32 — CRC32 cyclic redundancy check

Uses the standard CRC-32 (IEEE 802.3) polynomial 0xEDB88320.

usage:
h = new('hello', 5)
`
  'crc-32.new': `
Compute the CRC-32 (IEEE 802.3, reflected polynomial 0xEDB88320) checksum
s: input byte buffer
n: number of bytes actually included (may be less than the length of s); only s[0..n) is scanned
crc: 32-bit checksum (final xorout 0xFFFFFFFF applied), range 0..4294967295
`
  'crc-64': `
crc64 — CRC-64 cyclic redundancy check (ECMA-182 variant)

Uses the CRC-64-ECMA polynomial 0x42F0E1EBA9EA3693.
Reflected input and output (LSB first), initial value 0, no final XOR.

usage:
h = new(data)
`
  'crc-64.hex': `
Compute CRC-64 and return a 16-digit lowercase hex string
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
hex: fixed 16-character hexadecimal representation (big-endian, no 0x prefix)
`
  'crc-64.new': `
Compute the CRC-64/ECMA-182 checksum (reflected polynomial 0xC96C5795D7870F42, all-ones initial value)
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
crc: 64-bit checksum (final xorout applied)
`
  'csv': `
csv — CSV file parsing

Provides reading and generation of CSV format.
Supports standard CSV format (RFC 4180): comma-separated, double-quote escaping.

usage:
use std/encoding/csv
line = 'a,b,c'
fields = csv.parse-line(line)           // max-fields defaults to 1024
fields = csv.parse-line(line, 3)        // limits to at most 3 fields
`
  'csv.encode-field': `
Encode one field in CSV format
if it contains commas, double quotes, or newlines, it is wrapped in double quotes and escaped
field: the raw field text to encode
returns out: text ready to write into CSV; quoted with double quotes and " doubled as "" where needed
`
  'csv.parse-field': `
Parses one CSV field
Parse starting at s[pos], handling double quotes and commas; "" inside double quotes is restored to a single ",
A comma outside quotes / the line end is a field boundary.
s: the CSV text
pos: start position (byte index), typically the previous entry's new-pos
returns field: the parsed field content (outer quotes removed, escapes restored)
returns new-pos: the byte position after the field ends (skipping the separating comma)
`
  'csv.parse-line': `
Parses one line of CSV text
Call parse-field repeatedly until end of line or the field-count limit; surplus capacity is cut off with truncate
s: the single line to parse (should not include the trailing newline)
max-fields: maximum number of fields to parse (default 1024); content beyond the limit is discarded
returns fields: the parsed field list, length ≤ max-fields
`
  'deque': `
deque — double-ended queue

implements a double-ended queue (Double-Ended Queue) using []i64.
supports pushing and popping elements from both front and back.
uses a circular buffer internally.

usage:
use std/deque
buf [128]i64
d = deque { buf: buf, cap: 128, head: 0, tail: 0 }
d.push-back(42)
d.push-back(7)
val = d.pop-front()
val: {
nil -> print('empty')
-> print(it)
}
`
  'deque.deque': `
deque: double-ended queue state
buf: ring buffer, capacity = cap (provided and kept alive by the caller)
cap: queue capacity limit (equal to buf length)
head: front virtual index; actual storage index is head mod cap
tail: back virtual index; tail - head = number of elements
`
  'deque.deque.clear': `
Clear the double-ended queue (tail returns to head; buf is not freed and head is not reset)
No parameters and no return value; size() is 0 after clearing
`
  'deque.deque.empty': `
Reports whether the deque is empty
yes: true means tail <= head (there is no element to take)
`
  'deque.deque.peek-back': `
Peeks at the back element without removing it
val: ok=the current back element, nil=the queue is empty
`
  'deque.deque.peek-front': `
Peeks at the front element without removing it
val: ok=the current front element, nil=the queue is empty
`
  'deque.deque.pop-back': `
Pops an element from the back
val: ok=the original back element (removed), nil=the queue is empty
`
  'deque.deque.pop-front': `
Pops an element from the front
val: ok=the original front element (removed), nil=the queue is empty
`
  'deque.deque.push-back': `
Pushes an element onto the back
When the queue is full (element count >= cap) this push is simply ignored, with no error reported.
val: the value to place at the back
`
  'deque.deque.push-front': `
Pushes an element onto the front
When the queue is full (element count >= cap) this push is simply ignored, with no error reported.
val: the value to place at the front
`
  'deque.deque.size': `
returns the current element count of the deque
sz: tail - head (never goes negative while elements exist)
`
  'des': `
des — DES encryption/decryption (ECB mode)

Encrypts/decrypts 8-byte blocks using DES.
The 56-bit key is stored in 8 bytes (the 8th bit is a parity bit and is ignored).

usage:
key [8]byte
out  = des-enc(plain, 8, key)   // encrypt
out  = des-dec(cipher, 8, key)  // decrypt
`
  'des.des-block': `
DES single-block processing (generic)
Reads the first 8 bytes → IP permutation → 16 Feistel rounds → FP permutation → writes back the 8-byte result.
in: input data, only the first n bytes are taken (usually 8)
n: number of bytes to process (usually 8)
ks: 16 subkeys produced by des-key-schedule
encrypt: 1 = encrypt (subkeys forward), any other value = decrypt (subkeys reversed)
returns out: 8-byte result output
`
  'des.des-dec': `
Decrypt one 8-byte block
Same as des-enc but uses the 16 subkeys in reverse order (des-block(encrypt=0)).
in: input ciphertext, only the first 8 bytes are taken
n: number of bytes to process (usually 8)
key: 8-byte DES key, must match the one used for encryption
returns out: 8-byte plaintext
`
  'des.des-enc': `
Encrypt one 8-byte block
Internally expands the key schedule first, then calls des-block(encrypt=1).
in: input plaintext, only the first 8 bytes are taken
n: number of bytes to process (usually 8)
key: 8-byte DES key
returns out: 8-byte ciphertext
`
  'des.des-key-schedule': `
Produce 16 48-bit subkeys
Compresses the 64-bit key to 56 bits via PC-1, splits into C/D halves, cyclically left-shifts each round per the round table,
then selects 48 bits via PC-2; all 16 groups are written into ks in big-endian order (6 bytes each).
key: 8-byte DES key (parity bits included, not checked)
returns ks: sequence of 16 × 6-byte subkeys (group i at ks[i*6 .. i*6+6))
`
  'des.e-tbl': 'E: expansion permutation (32→48 bits)'
  'des.f': `
DES round function F(R, K)
Expands the 32-bit half R to 48 bits via E, XORs with the 48-bit subkey K, passes through 8 S-boxes,
then applies the P permutation for a 32-bit output.
r: right half (low 32 bits significant)
k: 48-bit subkey (low 48 bits significant)
returns out: result of F (low 32 bits significant)
`
  'des.fp-tbl': 'FP: final permutation (inverse of IP)'
  'des.ip-tbl': 'IP: initial permutation (64 bits)'
  'des.p-tbl': 'P: permutation (32 bits)'
  'des.pc1-c': `
PC1: initial key permutation (64→56 bits)
C half (28 bits)
`
  'des.pc1-d': 'D half (28 bits)'
  'des.pc2-tbl': 'PC2: key compression permutation (56→48 bits)'
  'des.permute': `
Extract and reorder bits according to a permutation table
tbl uses the standard DES 1-indexed MSB bit numbering (1..width); the output is MSB-first (table entry 0 → highest bit).
input: value to permute (only the low width bits are taken)
tbl: permutation table, each element a 1-indexed source bit position
n: number of bits participating (equals the effective length of tbl)
width: input bit width (sets the bit-position base, e.g. 64 / 56 / 48 / 32)
r: permutation result, the first table entry occupies the highest bit
`
  'des.sbox-lookup': `
Look up an S-box
input is a 6-bit value (MSB-first), b1 b2 b3 b4 b5 b6; row = b1*2 + b6, col = b2b3b4b5.
input: 6-bit S-box input (only the low 6 bits are taken)
box: 64-entry S-box table (4 rows × 16 columns stored flat)
r: the 4-bit output from the lookup
`
  'des.shift-tbl': 'left-shift table (16 rounds)'
  'dns': `
dns — DNS resolver (pure Nolang implementation)

Implements DNS queries over UDP (port 53), supporting A record (IPv4) resolution.
Does not depend on the C library's getaddrinfo; constructs and parses DNS packets directly.

usage:
// resolve a domain name
ip = resolve('example.com')           // returns ?str (IP address)
ip: {
nil -> print('resolution failed')
-> print(ip)                         // e.g. '93.184.216.34'
}

// specify a DNS server
ip = resolve-from('8.8.8.8', 'example.com')  // returns ?str
`
  'dns.build-query': `
Build a DNS query packet
domain: domain to query ('.'-separated, each label written as length + label)
qtype: query type (DNS-TYPE-A = 1 means an IPv4 address)
returns query: query packet (12-byte header + question section); trailing spare space is not truncated
returns qlen: actual useful length of the packet (only the first qlen bytes should be sent)
`
  'dns.dial': `
Resolve a domain name and establish a TCP connection
Tries net-dial first (it already supports hostname resolution internally); only on failure falls back to DNS to obtain an IP and retries once.
domain: domain name (or an IP directly)
port: target port
returns fd: connected socket descriptor, -1 = both approaches failed
`
  'dns.dns-backup-server': 'backup DNS server (Cloudflare)'
  'dns.dns-class-in': 'DNS class'
  'dns.dns-default-server': 'default DNS server (Google Public DNS)'
  'dns.dns-max-packet': 'maximum DNS packet size'
  'dns.dns-port': 'DNS port'
  'dns.dns-rcode-no-error': 'DNS response codes'
  'dns.dns-timeout': 'DNS query timeout (milliseconds)'
  'dns.dns-type-a': 'DNS record type'
  'dns.parse-name': `
Parse a domain name from a response packet (supports compression pointers)
Reads length + label segments until a zero length; when the top two bits are 11 (a compression pointer) the read position jumps to the target,
and jumps may chain; fields like CLASS are not validated. Malformed packets (out of range or illegal label length) return next-pos = -1.
data: DNS packet
offset: starting position of the name (byte index)
returns name: parsed domain name, labels joined by '.'
returns next-pos: position after this name (with compression pointers: the position after the pointer itself)
`
  'dns.parse-response': `
Parse a DNS response packet and return the IP of the first A record
First checks RCODE (non-zero gives up immediately) and ANCOUNT = 0 (gives up), skips the question section, then reads answer records one by one;
only accepts records with TYPE = A and RDLENGTH = 4 (CLASS is not checked).
data: DNS response packet (at least the 12-byte header)
returns ip: ok = dotted-decimal IP of the first A record, nil = no A record or an illegal packet
`
  'dns.record': `
record: DNS record
rtype: record type (1=A, 5=CNAME, 28=AAAA)
name: domain name
value: record value (for A records, the IP address)
ttl: time to live
`
  'dns.resolve': `
Resolve a domain name using the default DNS server
Tries DNS-DEFAULT-SERVER first, then DNS-BACKUP-SERVER on failure.
domain: domain name to resolve
returns ip: ok = resolved IPv4 address, nil = both servers failed
`
  'dns.resolve-all': `
Resolve a domain name, returning the first A record
Difference from resolve: no backup server is tried — a single query goes straight to DNS-DEFAULT-SERVER (same behavior as resolve's first round).
domain: domain name to resolve
returns ip: ok = resolved IPv4 address, nil = resolution failed
`
  'dns.resolve-from': `
Resolve a domain name using a specified DNS server
Uses UDP, waits at most DNS-TIMEOUT milliseconds per query, no retries.
server: DNS server IPv4 address (e.g. '8.8.8.8', no port)
domain: domain name to resolve
returns ip: ok = resolved IPv4 address, nil = packet build failed, socket/send failed, timeout, or no A record
`
  'dom': `
dom — XML / HTML DOM parsing and manipulation

full DOM tree model (node pool + index handles), supporting:
- strict XML 1.0 parsing: elements / attributes / text / comments / CDATA / processing instructions / DOCTYPE,
predefined and numeric entity decoding, tag matching and single-root-element checks; parse failure returns err.
- lenient HTML parsing: case-insensitive tags, void elements, raw-text/RCDATA elements
(script/style/textarea/title, etc.), auto-closing of unmatched end tags and fault-tolerant ignoring,
common implicit closing rules (p/li/dd/dt/option/td/th/tr/table sections), HTML4 named entity table.
- DOM operations: parent/child/sibling traversal, attribute add/remove/query, text-content reading,
getElementById / getElementsByTagName / getElementsByClassName,
node creation and insert/remove/replace, outer/inner serialization (XML and HTML modes).

nodes are identified by pool index i64 (0 = document root node); indices are valid for the dom lifetime.

usage:
d = dom.parse-xml('<root a="1">text<!--c--></root>')
d: {
ok -> {
doc = it
root, found = doc.document-element()
root: {
ok -> {
v, has = doc.get-attribute(it, 'a')
print(doc.text-content(it))
}
}
}
err -> print(it)
}

h = dom.parse-html('<ul><li>a<li>b</ul>')
h: {
ok -> {
items = it.get-elements-by-tag-name('li')
print(items.len())
}
}
`
  'dom.append-child': `
Appends (or moves) a child node to the end of parent's child list
parent: index of the target parent node
child: index of the child node to attach
`
  'dom.attribute-count': `
The number of attributes
idx: element node index
n: number of attributes
`
  'dom.attribute-name': `
The i-th attribute name in document order, or nil when out of range
idx: element node index
i: attribute ordinal (0-based)
nm: attribute name (nil=out of range)
`
  'dom.attribute-value': `
The i-th attribute value, or nil when out of range
idx: element node index
i: attribute ordinal (0-based)
val: the attribute value (nil=out of range)
`
  'dom.blob-has': `
Looks up the whole word name in a space-padded word-table blob
blob: word-list string padded with spaces on both sides
name: the word to look up
yes: whether the word list contains that whole word
`
  'dom.child-at': `
The i-th child node, or nil when out of range
idx: the parent node index
i: child node ordinal (0-based)
out: child node index (nil=out of range)
`
  'dom.child-count': `
The number of child nodes
idx: node index
n: number of child nodes
`
  'dom.class-has': `
Whether the class value (whitespace-separated words) contains the word cls
val: the class attribute value (multiple words separated by whitespace)
cls: the word to match
yes: whether it contains that word
`
  'dom.cp-append': `
Appends the code point cp to out as UTF-8 (returns the new string)
out: the existing string buffer
cp: the Unicode code point to append
res: a new string with the UTF-8 encoding appended
`
  'dom.create-comment': `
Creates a comment node
data: the comment content
idx: index of the new comment node
`
  'dom.create-element': `
Creates a detached element node (not yet attached)
name: tag name (lower-cased in HTML mode)
idx: index of the new element node
`
  'dom.create-text-node': `
Creates a text node
data: the text content
idx: index of the new text node
`
  'dom.decode-entities': `
decodes entities in s[from..to) (named entities looked up in DOM-ENTITY-BLOB, numeric ones &#NN;/&#xHH;)
html=true: unknown/unclosed entities are kept verbatim; html=false: unknown entities are treated as errors
returns out=decoded result, ok=whether it succeeded (invalid entities give ok=false in XML mode)
s: the source string
from: the start byte index of the fragment to decode (inclusive)
to: ending byte index (exclusive)
html: lenient HTML mode (true=unknown entities kept verbatim)
out: the decoded string (nil=entity not found)
`
  'dom.document-element': `
The root element (the first ELEMENT child)
idx: root element index (nil=absent)
`
  'dom.dom': `
dom: document handle (node pool + document root index + format)
format: 0=XML, 1=HTML (affects serialization of void elements and case handling)
pool is stored as a pointer field with #{inline=false}; dom deep-copies the pool when passed by value
`
  'dom.dom-byte-space': 'Common byte values'
  'dom.dom-closers-of': `
The list of open tags that must be closed first when opening tag t (empty string=no rule)
t: the newly opened tag name (lower case)
blob: the open-tag word list that should be implicitly closed first (empty = no rule)
`
  'dom.dom-entity-blob': 'Complete HTML4 named entity table (\'name:codepoint\' space-separated) + XML predefined entities'
  'dom.dom-kind-document': 'Node types (aligned with W3C nodeType numbers)'
  'dom.dom-max-depth': 'maximum nesting depth for parsing (guards against stack overflow from deeply nested input)'
  'dom.dom-node': `
dom-node: DOM node (pure scalar fields, safe to store in a heap vec)
the tree structure is an indexed doubly linked list: parent/prev/next/first/last
attr-first/attr-last/attr-count: attribute edge-list linked list (edge indices stored in pool.an/av/ax)
`
  'dom.dom-pblock-blob': 'Open <p> that is auto-closed when a new block-level tag starts'
  'dom.dom-pool': `
dom-pool: node pool (heap vec, no capacity limit)
nodes: parallel node arrays; names/vals: strings indexed by node idx
element→tag name, pi→target, doctype→raw internal text, text/cdata/comment→value
an/av/ax: attribute edge table (name, value, next sibling edge index; -1=end of list)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.alloc': `
Allocates a new node (with parallel names/vals slots) and returns its index
k: node type (DOM-KIND-* constant)
idx: pool index of the newly allocated node
`
  'dom.dom-pool.append': `
Appends child to the end of parent's child list (an existing parent is detached first)
parent: parent node index
child: index of the node to append at the end of the child list
`
  'dom.dom-pool.attr-edge-at': `
Returns the edge index of the node's i-th attribute in document order; -1 = out of range
node: index of the target element node
i: attribute ordinal (0-based, in document order)
e: the edge-table index of the i-th attribute; -1=out of range
`
  'dom.dom-pool.collect-class': `
Collect indices of elements in the node subtree whose class attribute contains the word cls
node: index of the root node whose subtree is searched
cls: the class word to match
out: list of indices of elements containing that class word
`
  'dom.dom-pool.collect-tag': `
Collect indices of elements in the node subtree whose tag name is want (all elements when star=true)
node: index of the root node whose subtree is searched
want: the target label name
star: match every element (want is treated as '*')
out: list of matched element node indices
`
  'dom.dom-pool.detach': `
Detaches child from its parent, keeping the child list consistent
child: index of the node to detach from its parent
`
  'dom.dom-pool.err-pos-or': `
gets the failure position: prefers .errpos (start of the most recently attempted element), otherwise falls back to dflt
dflt: fallback value (caller's scan position)
v: the larger of the two positions
`
  'dom.dom-pool.find-attr': `
Finds the edge index of the node's name attribute; -1 means it does not exist
node: index of the target element node
name: attribute name
e: the attribute edge-table index that hit; -1=absent
`
  'dom.dom-pool.find-id-node': `
Recursively search the node subtree for an element whose id attribute equals id, -1=not found
node: index of the root node whose subtree is searched
id: the target id value
idx: index of the matched element node; -1=not found
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-add-text': `
Decodes s[from..to) by HTML entity rules and appends it to parent as a text node
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
from: the start of the text fragment (inclusive)
to: end of the text slice (exclusive)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-bogus-comment': `
Any other content starting with '<!' up to '>' is kept as a comment
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of '<!'
np: position after '>' (runs to EOF when there is no '>')
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-attr': `
parses HTML attributes (unquoted values allowed); on duplicate attributes the first wins
node: index of the element node carrying the attributes
s: the source string
p: start position of the attribute name
np: address after this attribute (nil=illegal attribute name)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-close-name': `
Parses '</name' and returns the position after '>' plus the lowercase tag name
s: the source string
p: position of '</'
np: position after '>'
nm: closing tag name (already lower-cased)
ok: whether a tag name was read (false=unnamed ones are skipped as comments)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-comment': `
Parses <!--...-->, tolerating EOF
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of the comment opener '<!--'
np: position after '-->' (runs to EOF when unclosed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-doc': `
Parses a whole HTML document (never fails) and returns the document root index
s: HTML source text
root: index of the document root node
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-open': `
parses a start tag and appends the element to cur
returns np=next position, el=element index, mode:
0=invalid '<' (treated as text), 1=normal element (the caller pushes it), 2=void/self-closing, 3=raw-text with content consumed
s: the source string
p: position of the opening '<'
cur: index of the current insertion-position node
np: byte position after the start tag
el: index of the new element node (-1=invalid '<' treated as text)
mode: 0=invalid text 1=normal element (pushed) 2=void/self-closing 3=raw-text already consumed
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-parse-pi': `
'<?...>' (HTML treats this as a bogus comment; here it is kept as a PI node)
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of the processing-instruction opener '<?'
np: position after '>' (runs to EOF when unclosed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.html-set-attr-once': `
Sets the attribute only when it does not already exist (HTML duplicate attributes keep the first)
node: index of the target element node
name: attribute name (already lower-cased)
val: the attribute value
`
  'dom.dom-pool.init': 'Initialises the node pool (the heap vec must be initialised before pushing safely)'
  'dom.dom-pool.insert-before': `
Insert node before ref in parent's children (appended at the end when ref is invalid or not a child of parent)
parent: index of the target parent node
node: index of the node to insert
ref: reference node index (node is inserted before it; appended to the end when invalid)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.parse-doctype-node': `
parses <!DOCTYPE ...> (internal subset and quotes included), shared by XML/HTML
Content is stored verbatim (entities not decoded); the name is the first word after '!'
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of '<' in '<!DOCTYPE'
np: position after '>' (nil=not closed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.remove-attr': `
Removes an attribute; returns whether it was found
node: index of the target element node
name: attribute name to remove
yes: whether it was found and removed
`
  'dom.dom-pool.ser': `
Serialises a single node (outer)
idx: index of the node to serialize
html: HTML output mode (affects void elements and empty-element closing)
out: the outer serialization string of that node
`
  'dom.dom-pool.ser-children': `
Serialises all child nodes of idx
idx: index of the parent node to serialize
html: HTML output mode (affects void elements and empty-element closing)
out: serialization result of the child node (inner)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.set-attr': `
Sets an attribute: an existing one is updated, otherwise it is appended in document order
node: index of the target element node
name: attribute name
val: the attribute value
`
  'dom.dom-pool.text-of': `
Recursively collects the text content (TEXT / CDATA) of idx's subtree
idx: index of the subtree root node
out: text of all TEXT/CDATA concatenated within the subtree
`
  'dom.dom-pool.valid': `
Whether the node index is valid
idx: the node-pool index to validate
yes: whether the index is non-negative and within the pool length
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-add-text': `
Decodes s[from..to) and appends it to parent as a text node
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
from: the start of the text fragment (inclusive)
to: end of the text slice (exclusive)
ok: whether the append succeeded (false=entity decoding failed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-attr': `
parses one attribute (when xml=true values must be quoted and duplicate attributes are errors)
returns the next position and a success flag
node: index of the element node carrying the attributes
s: the source string
p: start position of the attribute name
xml: strict XML mode (values must be quoted, duplicate attributes are an error)
np: address after this attribute (nil=parse failure)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-cdata': `
Parses <![CDATA[...]]> and appends it to parent
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of the CDATA opener '<![CDATA['
np: position after ']]>' (nil=not closed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-close': `
Parses the end tag </name>, which must match the tag name of node
node: index of the element node to close
s: the source string
p: position of '</'
np: position after '>' (nil=name mismatch or format error)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-comment': `
Parses <!--...-->, appends it to parent, and returns the next position
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of the comment opener '<!--'
np: position after '-->' (nil=comment not closed)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-doc': `
Parses a whole XML document and returns the document root index
s: XML source text
root: index of the document root node
failpos: the byte position where parsing failed (-1=success)
ok: whether the whole document parsed successfully
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-element': `
Parses one element (including its children) and appends it to parent
p points at '<'; depth is the current nesting depth
s: the source string
parent: parent node index of the attach target
p: position of the opening '<'
depth: the current nesting depth (fails when exceeding DOM-MAX-DEPTH)
np: position after the element's end tag (nil=parse failure)
`
  'dom.dom-pool.xml-parse-pi': `
Parses <?target data?> and appends it to parent
parent: parent node index of the attach target
s: the source string
p: position of the processing-instruction opener '<?'
np: position after '?>' (nil=not closed or no target name)
`
  'dom.dom-rawtext-blob': 'HTML raw-text / RCDATA elements: content runs until the matching end tag, with no further lexical analysis'
  'dom.dom-rcdata-blob': 'RCDATA set (content requires entity decoding): title / textarea; other raw-text is not decoded'
  'dom.dom-strip-bom': `
strip-bom: removes a leading UTF-8 BOM and returns the start offset
s: the source string
p: start offset after the BOM (0=no BOM, 3=BOM stripped)
`
  'dom.dom-void-blob': 'HTML void elements (padded with spaces on both sides for whole-word lookup)'
  'dom.dom.last-child': `
Get the last child
idx: node index
c: last child node index (-1=none)
`
  'dom.dom.next-sibling': `
get the next sibling
idx: node index
c: .nextSibling node index (-1=none)
`
  'dom.dom.prev-sibling': `
Get the previous sibling
idx: node index
c: previous sibling node index (-1=none)
`
  'dom.esc-attr': `
Escapes &, <, > and the double quote in an attribute value
s: the original attribute value
out: escaped attribute value
`
  'dom.esc-text': `
Escapes &, < and > in text
s: the original text
out: escaped text
`
  'dom.find-entity': `
Looks up a named entity's code point in DOM-ENTITY-BLOB; -1 = not found
name: entity name (without & and ;)
cp: the corresponding Unicode code point; -1=not found
`
  'dom.first-child': `
dom.first-child / last-child / next-sibling / prev-sibling
idx: node index
c: index of the first child node (-1=none)
`
  'dom.get-attribute': `
Reads an attribute value (nil = absent; in HTML mode the name is case-insensitive)
idx: element node index
name: attribute name
val: the attribute value (nil=absent)
`
  'dom.get-element-by-id': `
Finds an element by its id attribute
id: the target id value
idx: matched element index (nil=not found)
`
  'dom.get-elements-by-class-name': `
Collects every element in the document whose class contains the given word
cls: the class word to match
out: list of matched element indices
`
  'dom.get-elements-by-tag-name': `
Collects every element with the given tag name; '*' returns all elements
name: tag name ('*'=all)
out: list of matched element indices
`
  'dom.has-attribute': `
Whether an attribute exists
idx: element node index
name: attribute name
yes: whether that attribute exists
`
  'dom.html-close-match': `
Reports whether s[pos..] starts the end-tag name for name (case-insensitive; it must be
followed by '>', '/', '=', or whitespace)
s: the source string
pos: starting byte position of the tag name (after '</')
name: tag name to match
yes: whether it is the closing tag name for that label
`
  'dom.html-peek-name': `
Peeks at the tag name (lowercase) after the '<' at s[p]; returns an empty string when illegal
s: the source string
p: byte position of '<'
nm: tag name (already lower-cased; empty string=illegal tag)
`
  'dom.inner-html': `
Serialises all child nodes of the node
idx: node index
out: serialized string of the child node (inner)
`
  'dom.insert-child-before': `
Inserts child before ref in parent
parent: index of the target parent node
child: index of the child node to insert
ref: reference child node index (child is inserted before it)
`
  'dom.is-html': `
Whether this is HTML mode (affects void-element serialisation and name case)
yes: whether the document format is HTML
`
  'dom.is-name-byte': `
XML/HTML tag and attribute name characters (ASCII subset + UTF-8 high bytes allowed)
c: the byte value to test
yes: whether it can serve as a name character of a tag/attribute
`
  'dom.kind': `
The node type (a DOM-KIND-* constant; -1 = illegal index)
idx: node index
k: node type (DOM-KIND-*; -1=invalid index)
`
  'dom.name': `
The element tag name, PI target, or DOCTYPE name
idx: node index
nm: tag name / PI target / DOCTYPE name
`
  'dom.new-document': `
Creates an empty document
Usage:
d = dom.new-document(true)   // an HTML document
html: whether to create an HTML-flavored document
d: the newly created empty document
`
  'dom.outer-html': `
Serialises a single node including itself
idx: node index
out: the outer serialization string of that node
`
  'dom.parent': `
The parent node index (-1 = none)
idx: node index
p: parent node index (-1=no parent)
`
  'dom.parse-html': `
Parses HTML leniently (never fails) and returns the document
source: HTML source text
out: the parsed document (always ok in lenient mode)
`
  'dom.parse-numeric-entity': `
parses the decimal/hex code point in &#...;; returns -1 if invalid
s[pos] starts the numeric part (may begin with 'x'/'X' for hex), end is the ';' position
s: the source string
pos: start position of the numeric part (after &#)
end: the position of the closing semicolon ';'
cp: the parsed code point; -1=invalid
`
  'dom.parse-xml': `
Parses XML strictly; returns err (including the error position) on failure
source: XML source text
out: the parsed document (err carries the error address)
`
  'dom.remove-attribute': `
Removes an attribute; returns whether it was found
idx: element node index
name: attribute name to remove
yes: whether it was found and removed
`
  'dom.remove-child': `
Detaches child from parent
parent: parent node index
child: index of the child node to detach
`
  'dom.replace-child': `
Replaces old with new in parent
parent: parent node index
new: index of the replacement node
old: index of the replaced node
`
  'dom.set-attribute': `
Sets an attribute (an existing one is overwritten)
idx: element node index
name: attribute name
val: the attribute value
`
  'dom.set-node-value': `
Sets the node value
idx: node index
v: the new value
`
  'dom.set-text-content': `
Clears the children and replaces them with a single text node
idx: the target node index
text: the new text content (empty string=just clear)
`
  'dom.text-content': `
The subtree's text content (TEXT / CDATA concatenated recursively)
idx: index of the subtree root node
out: the concatenated text content
`
  'dom.to-string': `
Serialises the whole document (the innerHTML of the document)
out: serialized string of the whole document
`
  'dom.value': `
The node value (text/cdata/comment content, PI data, or the raw DOCTYPE content)
idx: node index
v: the node value content
`
  'dom.xml-read-name': `
Reads a name (a run of name characters) from p and returns the end position
s: the source string
p: start byte position of the name
np: position where the name ends (nil=the first character is already not a name character)
`
  'dom.xml-skip-ws': `
Skips XML whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF)
s: the source string
p: starting byte position
np: position of the first non-space byte after skipping whitespace
`
  'ecdsa': `
ecdsa — ECDSA digital signature (NIST P-256 / secp256r1)

Implements the ECDSA signature algorithm based on the NIST P-256 elliptic curve.
# std/fs
Curve: y² = x³ - 3x + b (mod p)

Uses 8×32-bit limbs to represent 256-bit big integers.
Jacobian coordinates for point operations.

usage:
# std/crypto/ecdsa
priv [32]byte = ecdsa-generate(seed)
pub [65]byte = ecdsa-publickey(priv)
sig [64]byte = ecdsa-sign(priv, msg)
ok = ecdsa-verify(pub, msg, sig)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-add': `
256-bit addition (without modular reduction)
returns sum [32]byte and carry i64
a: addend (little-endian 32 bytes)
b: addend (little-endian 32 bytes)
sum: a + b (not reduced; the overflow is carried in carry)
carry: top carry bit (0 or 1)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-addmod': `
Modular addition (a + b) mod m
a, b < m (m is a 256-bit modulus, typically m ≈ 2^256)
Key point: a + b may be >= 2^256 and produce a carry. The true value is then
(a + b) = 2^256 + sum_low, and (a+b) mod m = (sum_low + (2^256 mod m)) mod m.
Using only bn-mod(sum_low, m) would drop the 2^256 mod m term and give a wrong result.
Here (a+b) is treated as a number of at most 33 bytes (carry included), placed in a 64-byte
space, and shares the reduce-by-subtracting-m<<(k*8)-from-the-top logic with bn-mulmod.
a: addend (little-endian 32 bytes, must be < m)
b: addend (little-endian 32 bytes, must be < m)
m: modulus (256-bit little-endian 32 bytes)
out: (a + b) mod m result (includes the 2^256 carry term, 32 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-cmp': `
Compare two 256-bit numbers (little-endian)
returns: -1 if a < b, 0 if a == b, 1 if a > b
a: first 256-bit operand (little-endian 32 bytes)
b: second 256-bit operand (little-endian 32 bytes)
r: comparison result (a<b → -1, a==b → 0, a>b → 1; compared from the top byte 31 down to the first difference)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-invmod': `
Modular inverse a^(-1) mod m, via the extended Euclidean algorithm
Simplified version: uses Fermat's little theorem a^(m-2) mod m (only when m is prime)
a: value to invert (little-endian 32 bytes, must be coprime with m)
m: modulus (this simplified version uses Fermat's little theorem, so m must be prime; little-endian 32 bytes)
out: the modular multiplicative inverse a^(-1) mod m of a (32 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-mod': `
Modular reduction a mod m (repeated subtraction)
a: dividend (little-endian 32 bytes)
m: divisor/modulus (little-endian 32 bytes)
out: remainder of a mod m (via repeated subtraction, 32 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-mulmod': `
Modular multiplication (a * b) mod m, via schoolbook multiplication
a: multiplicand (little-endian 32 bytes)
b: multiplier (little-endian 32 bytes)
m: modulus (little-endian 32 bytes)
out: (a * b) mod m result (32 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-sub': `
256-bit subtraction a - b (without modular reduction)
returns diff [32]byte and borrow i64
a: minuend (little-endian 32 bytes)
b: subtrahend (little-endian 32 bytes)
diff: a - b (not reduced; the borrow is reflected in borrow)
borrow: whether a borrow occurred from the high end (0 or 1)
`
  'ecdsa.bn-submod': `
Modular subtraction (a - b) mod m
a: minuend (little-endian 32 bytes, must be < m)
b: subtrahend (little-endian 32 bytes, must be < m)
m: modulus (little-endian 32 bytes)
out: (a - b) mod m result (m is added back when negative, 32 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.ec-bytes-to-point': `
Convert the uncompressed encoding to Jacobian coordinates
in: 04 || x || y (big-endian)
returns out: 96 bytes
`
  'ecdsa.ec-get-basepoint': `
Get the Jacobian coordinates of the base point G
g: Jacobian coordinates of the base point G (96 bytes: X‖Y‖Z, each 32 bytes little-endian)
`
  'ecdsa.ec-point-add': `
Point addition R = P + Q (P in Jacobian, Q in Jacobian)
returns r: 96 bytes
p: addend point (Jacobian coordinates, 96 bytes X‖Y‖Z little-endian)
q: other addend point (Jacobian coordinates, 96 bytes)
`
  'ecdsa.ec-point-double': `
Point doubling R = 2*P (Jacobian coordinates)
p: input point (X, Y, Z), 32 bytes each
returns r: output point (X, Y, Z), 96 bytes
`
  'ecdsa.ec-point-to-bytes': `
Convert Jacobian coordinates to the uncompressed encoding
p: Jacobian point (96 bytes)
returns out: 04 || x || y
`
  'ecdsa.ec-scalar-mult': `
Scalar multiplication R = k * P
k: scalar
p: point (96 bytes)
returns r: 96 bytes
`
  'ecdsa.ecdh-p256': `
P-256 (secp256r1) ECDH shared secret
priv: our private key (big-endian)
peer: peer's uncompressed public key (04 || x || y)
returns shared: shared secret (the x coordinate of the point R, 32 bytes big-endian); empty = invalid input
`
  'ecdsa.ecdsa-publickey': `
Compute the public key from the private key
priv: private key (big-endian)
returns pub: uncompressed public key (04 || x || y)
`
  'ecdsa.ecdsa-sign': `
ECDSA signing
priv: private key (big-endian)
msg: plaintext to sign (SHA-256 is computed internally before signing; not a pre-hashed input)
returns sig: signature (r || s, 32 bytes each, big-endian)
`
  'ecdsa.ecdsa-verify': `
ECDSA verification
pub: public key
msg: plaintext to verify (SHA-256 is computed internally and must match the signing side)
sig: signature (r || s)
returns ok: 1 = valid, 0 = invalid
`
  'ecdsa.p256-b': 'curve parameter b'
  'ecdsa.p256-gx': 'Base point Gx (big-endian; ec-get-basepoint converts it to internal little-endian via Gx[31-i])'
  'ecdsa.p256-gy': 'base point Gy (big-endian)'
  'ecdsa.p256-n': 'Group order n'
  'ecdsa.p256-p': `
Prime p = 2^256 - 2^224 + 2^192 + 2^96 - 1
32 bytes, little-endian
Prime field p = 2^256 - 2^224 + 2^192 + 2^96 - 1 (little-endian order: byte[0] is the least significant byte,
consistent with the storage convention of the bn-* engine and P256-N)
`
  'ed25519': `
ed25519 — Ed25519 signature algorithm (RFC 8032)

EdDSA signature scheme based on the twisted Edwards curve.
curve: -x² + y² = 1 + d*x²*y² (mod 2^255-19)
base point B, group order L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493

Field element (GF(2^255-19)) arithmetic reuses x25519.no, but always through its "return-value form"
fe-*-v wrapper functions to obtain results—cross-module calls do not pass "in-place writes to slice parameters"
back to the caller; only "return values" do, so all intermediate values in this module are assembled via return values.

Points are represented as []i64 (length 40) in extended coordinates (X,Y,Z,T), 10 limbs each,
at offsets 0/10/20/30. Coordinate reads can use slice views (read-only, cross-module safe),
writes go through this module's set-coord (in-place writes to same-module parameters do return).

usage:
# std/crypto/ed25519
pub [32]byte = ed25519-publickey(seed)
sig [64]byte = ed25519-sign(seed, msg)
ok = ed25519-verify(pub, msg, sig)
`
  'ed25519.ed-d': `
Return the field element for the curve constant d
h: field element of the curve constant d (10 limbs, little-endian)
`
  'ed25519.ed-d-bytes': 'Curve parameter d = -121665/121666 mod p (little-endian, 32 bytes)'
  'ed25519.ed-exp-bytes': 'square-root exponent (p-5)/8 = 2^252 - 3 (little-endian 32 bytes)'
  'ed25519.ed-gx-bytes': 'x coordinate of base point B (little-endian 32 bytes)'
  'ed25519.ed-gy-bytes': `
y coordinate of base point B = 4/5 mod p (little-endian 32 bytes)
0x6666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666666658
`
  'ed25519.ed-l': 'group order L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493 (little-endian 32 bytes)'
  'ed25519.ed-sqm1': `
Return the field element for sqrt(-1)
h: field element of sqrt(-1) mod p (10 limbs)
`
  'ed25519.ed-sqm1-bytes': 'sqrt(-1) mod p = 2^((p-1)/4) (little-endian 32 bytes), used for the square-root correction in point decompression'
  'ed25519.ed25519-publickey': `
Compute the public key from the seed
seed: 32-byte private key seed
pub: public key derived from the seed (32-byte compressed point)
`
  'ed25519.ed25519-sign': `
Sign, returning R || S (64 bytes)
seed: 32-byte private key seed
msg: message to sign
sig: signature R ‖ S (64 bytes)
`
  'ed25519.ed25519-verify': `
Verify a signature, returning ok (1 = valid, 0 = invalid)
pub: signer's public key (32-byte compressed point)
msg: original message to verify
sig: signature to verify R ‖ S (64 bytes)
ok: 1 = signature valid, 0 = invalid
`
  'ed25519.fe-pow': `
Return base^e (e is a little-endian 32-byte exponent, square-and-multiply)
base: base field element (10 limbs)
e: exponent (32 bytes little-endian)
out: resulting field element base^e (10 limbs)
`
  'ed25519.ge-add': `
R = P + Q (add-2008-hwcd, complete addition for a = -1)
r: output point R = P + Q (40-limb extended coordinates)
p: addend point (40-limb extended coordinates)
q: other addend point (40-limb extended coordinates)
`
  'ed25519.ge-basepoint': `
Return the extended coordinates of the base point B (length 40)
bp: extended coordinates of the base point B (point buffer of length 40)
`
  'ed25519.ge-pack': `
Compress a point to 32 bytes (little-endian encoding of y, top bit is the sign of x)
p: point to compress (40-limb extended coordinates)
out: compressed 32 bytes (little-endian encoding of y, top bit is the sign of x)
`
  'ed25519.ge-scalar-mult': `
R = scalar * P (double-and-add, over bits 254..0)
scalar: []byte (little-endian, length >= 32)
r: output point R = scalar * P (40-limb extended coordinates)
p: base point to multiply (40-limb extended coordinates)
`
  'ed25519.ge-unpack': `
Decompress a point from 32 bytes. Returns ok (1/0) and p (point of length 40)
s: compressed point, 32 bytes (little-endian encoding of y, top bit is the sign of x)
ok: 1 = decompression succeeded (a valid y coordinate), 0 = non-square remainder / invalid
p: decompressed point (40-limb extended coordinates; contents are not guaranteed valid when ok = 0)
`
  'ed25519.is-zero-arr': `
Return 1 if all 32 bytes are 0, otherwise 0
a: 32-byte array to test
z: 1 = all 32 bytes are 0, 0 = some byte is nonzero
`
  'ed25519.pcopy': `
Copy a whole point dst = src (40 limbs)
dst: destination point (40 limbs)
src: source point (40 limbs)
`
  'ed25519.sc-ge-l': `
Compare acc (32 bytes): return 1 when acc >= L, otherwise 0
acc: byte-wise scalar to compare (length 32, little-endian)
ge: 1 = acc >= L (group order), 0 = acc < L
`
  'ed25519.sc-muladd': `
Return (a*b + c) mod L (a, b, c each [32]byte)
a: multiplier scalar (32 bytes little-endian)
b: multiplier scalar (32 bytes little-endian)
c: addend scalar (32 bytes little-endian)
s: resulting scalar (a * b + c) mod L (32 bytes)
`
  'ed25519.sc-reduce': `
Reduce s (little-endian integer, any length) mod L, returning [32]byte
Bit-by-bit long division: acc always stays < L; after acc = acc*2 + bit, subtract L when acc >= L
s: little-endian scalar byte string to reduce (any length)
out: the 32-byte result of s mod L
`
  'ed25519.set-coord': `
Write the 10-limb field element src into point p at offset off
p: target point buffer (40 limbs: X‖Y‖Z‖T, each 10 limbs concatenated)
off: starting limb offset within p to write at
src: field element to write (10 limbs)
`
  'embed': `
embed: a compile-time embedded file system

Initialized at compile time by #{embed='dir/path'}.
Embeds all files in a directory into the executable, enabling single-binary distribution.

usage:
#{embed='../frontend/dist'}
DIST fs.embed

data = DIST.read('index.html')
ok = DIST.exists('css/style.css')
`
  'embed.embed': `
embed: embedded file system
count: number of files
paths: all paths concatenated (separated by \0)
pathStarts: start offset of each path in paths
pathLens: length of each path
blob: all file contents concatenated
dataStarts: start offset of each file in blob
dataLens: length of each file
`
  'embed.exists': `
embed.exists: check whether an embedded file exists
Same linear path-matching logic as read, but does not take the content.
path: file path (relative to the embed directory)
yes: true = an exactly identical path exists in the embed manifest
`
  'embed.read': `
embed.read: read an embedded file (linear path match, then slice from the blob)
path: file path (relative to the embed directory, e.g. 'index.html'; may contain path separators)
data: ok = file content (a slice over the blob, not copied), nil = path not present
Note: the return value points into the embed region; do not modify its content.
`
  'enter': `
enter — scope-entry hook (interface declaration)

Once a type provides `T.enter = () { … }`, the compiler inserts `var.enter()` right after a variable of that type is declared (entering scope):
the injection is performed by injectEnterLeave in src/build/transpiler_inject.go,
keyed by the **method name**, with no coupling to this declaration.

The decision is based on the "resolved effective type", not on whether the source has an annotation — `no fmt` removes
redundant annotations where "annotated type == inferred type"; if only annotations count, `a T = T{…}` after going through fmt would no longer
enter the lifecycle, and formatting would silently change semantics. Hence `a = T{…}` and `a = make-t()` also trigger.

note: enter runs immediately after the declaration, **before** any field assignment — when you need already-filled
fields, do not rely on enter; use an explicit initialization method instead.

This file only registers the interface itself, for documentation and LSP navigation; no type in the std corpus currently implements it
(example/sqlite-driver and example/mysql-driver do).
`
  'env': `
env — environment variable utilities

Provides high-level operations for reading, setting, and looking up environment variables.
Underneath it relies on the os module's get-env and set-env calls.

usage:
use std/env
val = env.get('HOME')
val: {
nil -> print('HOME not set')
-> print(it)
}
ok = env.set('MY_VAR', 'value')
val = env.lookup('PATH')
val: {
nil -> print('not found')
-> print(it)
}
`
  'env.get': `
reads an environment variable
key: environment variable name (case-sensitive, e.g. 'HOME' / 'PATH')
val: ok=the variable's value (possibly an empty string), nil=the variable is unset, err=read failure
`
  'env.get-with-default': `
reads an environment variable, returning a default when it is unset
key: environment variable name
default-str: the fallback used when the variable is unset or reading fails
val: the variable's value; equals default-str when unset (nil/err)
`
  'env.is-set': `
reports whether an environment variable is set
key: environment variable name
yes: true=read ok (including an empty-string value), false=unset or read failed
`
  'env.lookup': `
Get an environment variable's value (a synonym alias of get, with identical behavior)
key: environment variable name
val: ok=the variable's value, nil=the variable is unset, err=read failure
`
  'env.set': `
sets an environment variable
overwrites an existing variable of the same name; affects only the current process and its later child processes.
key: environment variable name, must not be empty
val: the value to write (an empty string amounts to setting it empty)
ok: true=write succeeded, false=the underlying set-env failed (e.g. empty key or length exceeded)
`
  'env.unset': `
removes an environment variable (sets it to the empty string)
Note: the implementation writes an empty string rather than a true unset; the variable still exists but its value is empty.
key: environment variable name
ok: true=empty value written successfully, false=the underlying set-env failed
`
  'err': `
err — error handling

Provides structured error types and utility functions.
The err struct wraps an error code, message and location (file / line / col); the code enum defines common error categories.

usage:
e = err.new('disk full', code.io)
yes = e.is(err.code.io)
s = e.format()     ; => 'code.io: disk full' (with error code and location)
`
  'err._errno-to-code': `
maps a C errno onto the code enum (module-private, used only by from-errno)
errno: the C errno value to map (e.g. 2=ENOENT, 13=EACCES)
ec: the corresponding code enum value (unlisted errnos all fall to code.unknown)
`
  'err.code': `
returns the error code
returns ec: the code
`
  'err.code-2': `
code: error category
index-out: index out of bounds (i of `a[i]` / `s[i]` is not within `[0, len)`). D11 decision: out-of-bounds reads
return err (not nil) — nil is reserved for "the value truly does not exist" (map missing key, `?T` slot empty),
whereas out-of-bounds is a **program error**, so err is given to distinguish it from an empty value.
`
  'err.code.to-name': `
returns the variant name of an error code, without the code. prefix
returns s: the variant name (e.g. 'ok' / 'not-found' / 'io')
usage:
n = code.io.to-name()     ; => 'io'
`
  'err.err': `
err: structured error
code: error code (code enum value)
msg: human-readable error description text
file / line / col: location where the error occurred (auto-injected by the compiler at the construction point; leave empty string and 0 when constructing manually)
`
  'err.format': `
renders the error as a full information string
returns s: 'code.<variant>: <msg>', with ' at <file>:<line>:<col>' appended when a position is known
usage:
s = err.new('disk full', code.io).format()   ; => 'code.io: disk full'
e = err('no such file', code.not-found)      ; the compiler injects the position
t = e.format()                               ; => 'code.not-found: no such file at main.no:12:3'
Use err.msg() for the bare message, err.code() for the code alone
`
  'err.from-errno': `
builds an error from a C errno
errno: a standard C error code (e.g. 13 = EACCES)
e: the error instance (msg from os.strerror(errno), code mapped by _errno-to-code, no position info)
`
  'err.is': `
tests whether the error code matches
ec: the code to match
returns yes: whether it matched
`
  'err.msg': `
returns the error message
returns msg: the message text carried by the error object; empty for non-error values
`
  'err.new': `
builds an error
msg: description text written into the error instance
ec: the error code (a code enum value such as code.io; defaults to code.unknown)
e: the constructed error instance (position fields left empty)
`
  'err.new-at': `
builds an error with a position (the caller supplies the position explicitly)
msg: description text written into the error instance
ec: the error code to attach (a code enum value)
file: the source file name where the error occurred
line: the line number where the error occurred
col: the line number where the error occurred
e: the error instance carrying full position information
`
  'float': `
float — float union type and float methods

float = f32 | f64 (floating-point union type alias).
this module carries the methods of the float type: div, is-nan, is-inf.
see std/int.no for the int union type; see std/num.no for the num union type;
see the number module for concrete-type operations (range constants, type conversion, f64/f32.to-str, bitwise operations, etc.).
`
  'float.div': `
floating-point division, returns the quotient (a ÷ b)
follows IEEE-754: dividing by 0.0 yields ±inf (0.0/0.0 yields NaN), no error thrown and no truncation.
b: divisor; its type must match the receiver (both f32 or both f64)
q: the quotient, typed like the receiver (f32 in yields f32, f64 in yields f64)
`
  'float.is-inf': `
Determine whether it is infinite.
Note: do not write `. + . == .` — it also holds for ±0.0 (0+0==0), which would misjudge zero as infinity.
Adding the nonzero condition makes it the necessary and sufficient condition for infinity (for finite nonzero x, x+x!=x; NaN comparisons are always false).
yes: true=the receiver is +inf or -inf; NaN, ±0.0, and finite numbers are all false
`
  'float.is-nan': `
reports whether the value is NaN
Judged via the IEEE-754 property "NaN is not equal to itself", so any NaN (including different payloads) yields true.
yes: true=the receiver is NaN, false=finite number or ±inf
`
  'fmt': 'fmt — formatted input/output standard library\n\nprint/eprint/format/sprintf are compiler builtins (ForwardFunc).\n⚠️ printf/eprintf are removed: names remain in the symbol table (calls still resolve, LSP still completes),\nbut any call is a hard compile error [printf-depr], guiding migration to print/io.out and eprint/io.err.\nThis file provides formatting helper functions implemented in pure Nolang, supporting a subset of the Python format spec:\nfmt-parse-spec  — parse the spec string\nfmt-apply-spec  — apply width/alignment/fill\nfmt-uint-base   — convert an unsigned integer to a string in the given base\nfmt-int         — signed integer formatting\nfmt-uint        — unsigned integer formatting\nfmt-f64         — floating-point formatting\nfmt-str         — string formatting\nfmt-bool        — boolean formatting\n\nSpec string syntax (Python format spec subset):\n[[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type]\nalign:  <(left) >(right) =(fill after sign) ^(center)\nsign:   + -(space)\ngrouping: ,(comma) _(underscore)\ntype:   b o d x X p e E f F g G s t v\n\nImplementation notes (compiler limitation workaround):\n1. Assigning a scalar i64 output parameter inside a conditional block has no effect (compiler bug),\nso fmt-parse-spec computes with local -v variables and assigns to output params at the top level.\n2. with-cap() infers vec rather than str by default when there is no type annotation,\nso local buffers must be explicitly type-annotated.\n3. Direct str subscript writes (`s[i] = v`) have been globally disabled since 2026-11-11 (even the with-cap base\nis no longer exempt); byte-by-byte construction always uses `x []byte = with-cap-len(n, n)` +\n`x[i] = b` + `x.to-bytes-str()`.'
  'fmt.eprintf': `
removed (the name is kept so parsing still works) — calling it is a compile error; use eprint (adds a newline) or io.err (no newline) instead
s: format string (this function was removed; calling it is a compile error)
`
  'fmt.fmt-apply-spec': `
applies width, alignment and the fill character
align: 60 = left, 62 = right, 94 = centre, 0/61 = right (the default)
s: the content string to apply width and alignment to
fill: ASCII value of the fill character
width: the target minimum width (strings shorter than this are padded)
out: the output string after padding and alignment
`
  'fmt.fmt-bool': `
boolean formatting
supports width, align, fill and type (t)
b: the bool value to format
spec: format specification string
out: formatted boolean string
`
  'fmt.fmt-f64': `
floating-point formatting
supports type (f/e/g/t), precision, sign, zero, width, align and fill
x: the float value to format
spec: format specification string
out: formatted float string
`
  'fmt.fmt-group-digits': `
⚠️ must be applied AFTER zero padding: f'{1234:08,d}' is 0001,234 (pad to 8 digits first, then group).
Grouping first and padding after produces the wrong 0001234,.
digits: the pure digit string to group (no sign or radix prefix)
sep: ASCII value of the thousands separator character (44=comma, 95=underscore)
out: string with a separator inserted every 3 digits
`
  'fmt.fmt-int': `
signed integer formatting
supports type (c/d/t/none), sign (+/-/space), zero, width, align, fill and digit grouping (,/_)
n: the signed integer value to format
spec: format specification string
out: formatted integer string
`
  'fmt.fmt-parse-spec': `
parses a format spec string
returns fill/align/sign/alt/zero/width/grouping/precision/typ
align/sign/grouping/typ are character ASCII values; alt/zero are 0/1; precision = -1 means unspecified
spec: the format specification string to parse
fill: ASCII value of the fill character (default 32, a space)
align: ASCII value of the alignment character (60=left, 62=right, 94=center, 0=default)
sign: ASCII value of the sign character (43=+, 45=-, 32=space, 0=unspecified)
alt: whether the alternative form (# prefix) is enabled, 1=yes 0=no
zero: whether zero padding is enabled (0 prefix), 1=yes 0=no
width: minimum field width (0=unspecified)
grouping: ASCII value of the thousands separator character (44=comma, 95=underscore, 0=unspecified)
precision: precision (digits after the decimal point; -1=unspecified)
typ: ASCII value of the type character (0=unspecified)
`
  'fmt.fmt-str': `
string formatting
supports precision (truncation), width, align, fill and type (t/v)
s: the string value to format
spec: format specification string
out: string after truncation and width alignment
`
  'fmt.fmt-uint': `
unsigned integer formatting
supports type (b/o/d/x/X/t), alt (# prefix), zero, width, align, fill and digit grouping (,/_)
u: the unsigned integer value to format
spec: format specification string
out: formatted unsigned integer string
`
  'fmt.fmt-uint-base': `
converts a u64 to a string in the given base
base: 2/8/10/16; upper: whether hex is uppercase
u: the unsigned integer value to convert
upper: whether hex digits are printed in upper case
out: digit string converted in the given radix
`
  'fmt.printf': `
removed (the name is kept so parsing still works) — calling it is a compile error; use print (adds a newline) or io.out (no newline) instead
s: format string (this function was removed; calling it is a compile error)
`
  'fmt.sprintf': `
deprecated but still usable; use format instead
s: format specification string (syntax same as format)
out: formatted output string
`
  'fnv': `
fnv — FNV-1a hash function (32-bit and 64-bit)

FNV (Fowler-Noll-Vo) is a fast non-cryptographic hash function.
FNV-1a: hash = (hash XOR byte) * prime

usage:
h32 = fnv-1a-32(data)
h64 = fnv-1a-64(data)
`
  'fnv-1a-32': `
fnv-1a — FNV-1a non-cryptographic hash (32bit)

fast, simple non-cryptographic hash, suited for scenarios such as hash tables.

usage:
h = fnv-1a-32('hello', 5)
`
  'fnv-1a-32.fnv-1a-32': `
Compute the 32-bit FNV-1a hash (offset basis 2166136261, prime 16777619)
s: input byte buffer
n: number of bytes actually included in the computation; only s[0..n) is scanned
h: 32-bit hash value (& 0xFFFFFFFF applied after each multiply), range 0..4294967295
`
  'fnv.fnv-1a-32': `
Compute the FNV-1a 32-bit hash (offset basis 2166136261, prime 16777619)
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
hash: 32-bit hash value (& 0xFFFFFFFF applied after each multiply)
`
  'fnv.fnv-1a-64': `
Compute the FNV-1a 64-bit hash (offset basis 14695981039346656037, prime 1099511628211)
data: input bytes, all included in the computation
hash: 64-bit hash value (each multiply is masked back to 64 bits)
`
  'fnv.fnv32-offset': 'FNV-1a-32 parameter'
  'fnv.fnv64-offset': 'FNV-1a-64 parameters'
  'fs': `
fs — filesystem utilities

Wraps an open file in the file struct, and a path in the path struct.
Contains low-level system calls for file operations, read/write, and information queries.

usage:
f = open(FILE-PATH, {
mode: write
perm: perm-644
excl: false
truncate: false
append: false
})
f.write('hello'.to-bytes(), 5)         // method call
f.close()

line = STDIN.read-line()              // read a line from standard input, returns ?str
n = STDOUT.write('hello'.to-bytes(), 5)  // write to standard output
n = STDERR.write('error'.to-bytes(), 5)  // write to standard error

p = path{
p: '/tmp'
}
yes = p.exists()
`
  'fs.close': `
closes a file descriptor
fd: the file descriptor to close
ok: 0=success, -1=failure (closing twice also fails)
`
  'fs.close-dir': `
close-dir: closes a directory handle
dirp: directory handle returned by open-dir (cannot be read after closing)
ok: true=directory stream buffer released
`
  'fs.copy-file': `
copies a file's contents
src: source file path
dst: the destination file path
ok: true=copy completed
`
  'fs.dir-entries': `
usage:
names = dir-entries('/path/to/dir')
i <- [0..names.len()): {
print(names[i])
}
dirpath: the directory path
entries: child name list ('.'/'..' excluded; empty when the directory cannot be opened)
`
  'fs.exists': `
reports whether a path exists (following symlinks)
returns true on success, false on failure
p: the path to check
yes: true=the path exists (a symlink follows its target)
`
  'fs.fd': `
standard file descriptor
fd is a newtype alias for a file descriptor (underlying i64), mutually exclusive with i64 in the type system,
preventing fd from being misused as an arbitrary integer (port number, PID, etc.).
integer literals can be assigned to fd (e.g. STDIN-FD fd = 0), and fd can be compared with integer literals.
`
  'fs.file': `
file: opened file
fd=-1 means not opened; path is the source path
`
  'fs.file-mode': 'file-mode: file open mode (pre-combined)'
  'fs.file-opts': `
file-opts: open() options
mode: file mode (file-mode enum)
perm: permissions (file-perm enum)
excl: exclusive creation (same as O_EXCL)
truncate: truncate to 0 length (same as O_TRUNC)
append: append writes (same as O_APPEND)
`
  'fs.file-perm': 'file-perm: file permissions (pre-combined)'
  'fs.file-size': `
gets the file size (same as stat-size)
p: file path
size: ok=byte size, nil=fetch failed
`
  'fs.file.append': `
appends data to the end of the file
A simplified implementation: read the existing content, then write it back merged
data: the text to append (only the first n bytes are taken)
n: number of bytes to append
success: true=both the existing content and the new data have been written back (appending is achieved by rewriting the whole file)
`
  'fs.file.close': `
closes the file
After success fd is reset to -1
The standard files (STDIN/STDOUT/STDERR) are not closed
ok: true=closed successfully (the fd is reset to -1 afterwards; false for standard streams and repeat closes)
`
  'fs.file.copy-to': `
copies this file to dst-path
returns true = success, false = failure
dst-path: the destination file path (an existing file is overwritten)
ok: true=copy completed
`
  'fs.file.is-open': `
checks whether the file is open
yes: true=fd >= 0, i.e. the file is still open
`
  'fs.file.read': `
reads at most n bytes from the file into buf
buf: read buffer (reserve at least n bytes)
n: maximum bytes to read this round
read-n: bytes actually read (0=EOF, -1=error or not opened)
`
  'fs.file.read-bytes': `
reads the whole file into a []byte (the Rust hybrid strategy)
returns content:
ok([]byte) - success, with the file content
nil        - the file is empty
err        - the file is not open or the read failed
content: ok=file content, nil=file empty, err=not opened or read failure
`
  'fs.file.read-line': `
reads one line from the file (at most 4096 bytes)
returns line: ok = the line, nil = EOF or error
line: ok=a line read (without the newline), nil=EOF, over 4096 bytes, or error
`
  'fs.file.read-str': `
reads the whole file into a string
returns content:
ok(str) - success, with the file content
nil     - the file is empty
err     - the file is not open or the read failed
content: ok=file content, nil=file empty, err=not opened or read failure
`
  'fs.file.size': `
gets the size of the open file (through fstat(fd), eliminating TOCTOU)
sz: the file's current size fetched via fstat (bytes)
`
  'fs.file.write': `
writes the first n bytes of data to the file
data: data to write (only the first n bytes are taken)
n: number of bytes to write
written: actual bytes written (-1=error or not open)
`
  'fs.file.write-bytes': `
writes a []byte to the file (overwriting it entirely)
data: the byte array to write
returns ok:
ok(true)  - success
err       - the file is not open or the write failed
data: the bytes to write (whole-file overwrite)
