  'noml.doc.stringify': `
serializes the whole document (document-level annotations are emitted above the root node)
out: NOML text (empty string when root is invalid)
`
  'noml.doc.stringify-node': `
Serializes any subtree
idx: subtree root index
out: NOML text (empty string when the index is invalid)
`
  'noml.hex-char': `
Convert a value 0~15 to its hexadecimal character byte
d: 0~15
c: the corresponding ASCII byte value
`
  'noml.hex-digit': `
Convert a hexadecimal digit character to its value
c: byte value
d: 0~15; -1 for non-hex characters
`
  'noml.is-ident-char': `
Whether it may appear after the first char of a bare key (lowercase letter, digit, underscore, hyphen)
c: byte value
yes: true = usable as a trailing character
`
  'noml.is-ident-key': `
Whether it can be written as a bare key
k: key name
yes: true = writable as a bare key (otherwise serialization adds single quotes)
`
  'noml.is-ident-start': `
Whether it can start a bare key (underscore or lowercase letter; digits cannot start one)
c: byte value
yes: true = usable as the first character
`
  'noml.kind-nil': 'kind: node kind (constants rather than an enum, consistent with the json module)'
  'noml.new': `
Create an empty document (root node = -1, i.e. empty)
Usage:
d = noml.new()
o = d.new-obj()
d.put(o, 'name', d.new-str('nolang'))
d.set-root(o)
d: a new document with an initialized pool and root = -1
`
  'noml.parse': `
Parse NOML text
Usage:
r = noml.parse('{\nname: \'nolang\'\n}')
r: {
ok -> print(it.stringify())
err -> print(it)
}
s: full NOML text (leading and trailing whitespace is skipped)
result: ok = parsed document, err (message with line number) = syntax error
`
  'noml.pool': `
pool: node pool (heap vec, no capacity limit)
nodes: parallel node array
strs: string value of each node (same index as nodes)
anns: raw `#{...}` annotation text of each node (empty string = no annotation, same index as nodes)
ec/ek/en: edge table — child node index, key name, next sibling edge index
err: message of the most recent parse failure (with line number), read out by parse
`
  'noml.pool.add-child': `
Append a child node to a parent node (variable-length children implemented as a singly linked edge list)
node-idx: parent node index
child-idx: child node index
key: key name on the edge (used for object children; pass an empty string for arrays)
`
  'noml.pool.alloc': `
allocates a new node (kind=nil, no children)
idx: new node index
`
  'noml.pool.ann': `
get the raw `#{...}` annotation text of a node (excluding `#{` and `}`)
idx: node index
v: ok=raw annotation text, nil=the node has no annotation
`
  'noml.pool.ann-literal': `
Read an annotation value literal: unquotes single-quoted strings, otherwise returns the raw text
body: raw annotation text
pos: start position of the value
v: ok=value, nil=invalid format
`
  'noml.pool.ann-value': `
Get the value of a key in an annotation (unquotes single-quoted strings, otherwise returns the raw text)
idx: node index
key: annotation key (e.g. 'type')
v: ok=value, nil=key does not exist
`
  'noml.pool.at': `
gets an array element index by position
idx: array node index
i: element index (0-based)
child: ok=element node index, nil=not an array or index out of bounds
`
  'noml.pool.at-line-start': `
whether a position is at line start (only whitespace before it)
s: source text
pos: byte offset to check
yes: true=no non-whitespace character has appeared on this line yet
`
  'noml.pool.decode-escape': `
Decodes one escape sequence (`\\'` `\\\\` `\\/` `\\n` `\\r` `\\t` `\\uXXXX`)
s: source text
pos: position of the backslash
val: the decoded string (a single code point)
next: position after the escape sequence
ok: true=decode succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.fail': `
Record a parse error with a line number and return false
s: source text
pos: error position
msg: message (without a position prefix)
ok: always false (so `ok = .fail(...)` is convenient)
`
  'noml.pool.fmt-key': `
Produces a key literal (adds quotes when it does not fit bare-key rules)
k: key name
out: NOML key literal
`
  'noml.pool.fmt-num': `
produces a numeric literal (integer values without a decimal point)
when within ±1e15 and recoverable after truncation (i.to-f64() == n), outputs the integer form;
otherwise keeps the result of n.to-str().
n: value to serialize
out: numeric literal (e.g. '42' or '3.14')
`
  'noml.pool.get': `
Gets a child node index by key
idx: object node index
key: key name
child: ok=child node index, nil=not an object or key does not exist
`
  'noml.pool.get-bool': `
Gets a boolean value by key
idx: object node index
key: key name
v: ok=boolean value, nil=key does not exist or value is not a boolean
`
  'noml.pool.get-f64': `
gets a numeric value by key
idx: object node index
key: key name
v: ok=numeric value, nil=key does not exist or value is not a number
`
  'noml.pool.get-i64': `
gets an integer value by key (truncates decimals)
idx: object node index
key: key name
v: ok=truncated integer, nil=key missing or value not a number
`
  'noml.pool.get-str': `
Get a string value by key
idx: object node index
key: key name
v: ok=string content, nil=key missing or value is not a string
`
  'noml.pool.has': `
whether the object has the key
idx: object node index
key: key name
found: true=exists
`
  'noml.pool.has-byte': `
Scan raw bytes and determine whether a given value occurs (byte-by-byte comparison, not by code point)
s: text to scan
target: byte value to compare, range 0–255 (e.g. 10 = '\n', 96 = '`')
yes: true=occurs at least once, false=the byte is entirely absent (an empty string always yields false)
`
  'noml.pool.has-key': `
whether the object already has a member with the same name (duplicate keys are an error)
obj-idx: object node index
key: key name
found: true=a member with the same name already exists
`
  'noml.pool.hex4': `
read a 4-digit hexadecimal code point
s: source text
pos: position of the first digit
code: 0~0xFFFF; -1=invalid format
`
  'noml.pool.init': 'initializes the node pool (the heap vec must first be initialized to non-nil to push safely)'
  'noml.pool.is-arr': `
whether it is an array
idx: node index
yes: true=value is an array
`
  'noml.pool.is-bool': `
Whether it is a boolean
idx: node index
yes: true=the value is a boolean
`
  'noml.pool.is-nil': `
whether it is nil
idx: node index
yes: true=value is nil
`
  'noml.pool.is-num': `
Whether it is a number
idx: node index
yes: true=value is a number
`
  'noml.pool.is-obj': `
Whether it is an object
idx: node index
yes: true=value is an object
`
  'noml.pool.is-str': `
whether it is a string
idx: node index
yes: true=value is a string (single-quoted string or raw string)
`
  'noml.pool.join-ann': `
Join two annotation raw texts (multiple annotations are joined with ", ")
cur: accumulated raw text (may be empty)
add: newly added raw text
out: merged result
`
  'noml.pool.key': `
gets an object key name by position
idx: object node index
i: member index (0-based, in file order)
key: ok=key name, nil=not an object or index out of bounds
`
  'noml.pool.kind': `
Node kind
idx: node index
k: NOML-KIND-*; NOML-KIND-NIL when the index is invalid
`
  'noml.pool.len': `
number of child elements (object member count or array length, 0 otherwise)
idx: node index
n: number of child elements
`
  'noml.pool.line-of': `
Line number of a position (counting from 1)
s: source text
pos: byte offset to look up the line number (auto-clamped when out of range)
line: line number
`
  'noml.pool.match-kw': `
matches a bare keyword (must not be immediately followed by an identifier character)
s: source text
pos: byte offset at which to attempt the match
kw: keyword
next: position after the keyword; -1=no match
`
  'noml.pool.parse': `
Parses the entire document (including document-level annotations and trailing annotations on the top-level value)
s: source text
idx: root node index; -1=failure
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-array': `
Parse an array `[ elements, ... ]` (comma-separated, trailing comma allowed)
s: source text
pos: position of `[`
idx: array node index; -1=failure
next: position after `]`
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-bool': `
parse true/false
s: full source text
pos: start position of the literal (pointing at t or f)
val: parsed boolean value; fixed to false when ok=false
next: position after the literal; returns to the original pos when ok=false
ok: true=parsed successfully, false=not true/false here (fail was called to record the error)
`
  'noml.pool.parse-bool-node': `
parse a boolean value and materialize it into a node
s: source text
pos: start position of the literal
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-key': `
Parses a key (bare identifier or single-quoted string)
s: source text
pos: start position of the key
key: key name
next: position after the key
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-nil-node': `
Parses a nil value and materializes it as a node
s: source text
pos: start position of the literal
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-num': `
Parses a numeric literal (integer / decimal / scientific notation, allows a leading '-', does not accept '+')
Consumes only the bytes that form the number itself, stops at any other character, no separator required
s: full source text
pos: byte index where the number starts
val: the parsed numeric value (NOML has only one numeric type; integers are also stored as f64)
next: byte index after the number ends (on failure stops at the error position for .fail to mark)
ok: true=parse succeeded, false=syntax error (no integer digits, no fractional digits after '.', no digits in the exponent)
`
  'noml.pool.parse-num-node': `
Parse a numeric value and materialize it as a node
s: source text
pos: start position of the number
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-object': `
parse an object `{ members... }` (members separated by newlines, no commas)
s: source text
pos: position of `{`
idx: object node index; -1=failure
next: position after `}`
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-raw': `
Parses a backtick raw string (the opening ` must be immediately followed by a newline; the closing ` must be on its own line)
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
val: content as-is (no escape parsing)
next: position after the closing backtick
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-raw-node': `
Parses a raw string value and materializes it as a node
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-str': `
parse a single-quoted string (with `\\` escapes)
built by "segment concatenation" rather than a byte buffer: each escape point cuts a segment, and segments are concatenated directly with the decoded result,
so there is no need to pre-estimate capacity, and no lifetime issues with buffer views arise.
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
val: decoded string
next: position after the closing quote
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-str-node': `
parses a string value and materializes it into a node
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parse succeeded
`
  'noml.pool.parse-value': `
parses one value
s: source text
pos: start position of the value
idx: node index; -1=failure
next: position after the value
ok: true=parsed successfully
`
  'noml.pool.quote': `
produces a single-quoted string literal (with escaping)
escaping rules: prefix '\'' and '\\' with a backslash, write LF / CR / TAB as \n \r \t,
other control bytes < 0x20 are written as \u00XX; the output buffer reserves n*6+2 (worst case 6 characters per byte).
s: raw content (any bytes allowed, valid UTF-8 not required)
out: the literal with a single quote added at each end and the interior escaped
`
  'noml.pool.quote-value': `
produces a string literal in the simplest form chosen by content
defaults to .quote(); if it contains LF and passes .raw-safe(), uses a backtick raw string instead, preserving original newlines.
s: raw content
out: string literal writable directly into .noml (single-quote escaped form or '`' raw string)
`
  'noml.pool.raw-safe': `
determines whether content can be placed into a backtick raw string and retrieved unchanged
unusable if any of the following occurs: empty string, first or last byte is LF, contains CR,
any line, after stripping leading whitespace (space / TAB / CR), starts with '`' (would be mistaken for the closing marker).
s: content to serialize
yes: true=can be safely wrapped as '`\n' + s + '\n`', false=must use the single-quoted escaped form
`
  'noml.pool.require-end': `
Member-end validation: only inline whitespace, newline, a `;` comment, or container closing may follow
s: source text
pos: position after the value
key: member key name (only used in error messages)
next: position after the newline (or `}`)
ok: true=valid
`
  'noml.pool.scan-ann': `
scans over a group of `#{...}`, returns the position after `}` (validates structure only, does not parse entries)
s: source text
pos: position of `#`
next: position after `}`; -1=not a comment or unclosed
`
  'noml.pool.scan-quote': `
Scans from `'` to just after the next unescaped `'`
s: source text
pos: position of the opening quote
next: position after the closing quote; -1=unclosed
`
  'noml.pool.scan-raw': `
Scan from a backtick to just after the closing backtick that stands alone on a line
s: source text
pos: opening quote position
next: position after the closing backtick; -1=not closed
`
  'noml.pool.set-ann': `
Sets the raw `#{...}` annotation text of a node (empty string = remove)
idx: node index
text: raw annotation text (without `#{` and `}`)
ok: true=written
`
  'noml.pool.skip-ann-value': `
skips one annotation value (string/array/bare value), returning the position after it
body: annotation source text
pos: value start position
next: position after the value; -1=invalid format
`
  'noml.pool.skip-blanks': `
Skips only inline whitespace (spaces and Tab), not across newlines
s: source text
pos: byte offset to start scanning from
p: position of the first non-whitespace character
`
  'noml.pool.skip-trivia': `
skips whitespace (including newlines) and `;` line comments
s: source text
pos: byte offset at which to start scanning
p: position of the first significant character (end of string when all whitespace)
`
  'noml.pool.stringify': `
serializes a subtree
idx: subtree root index
indent: indentation level (two spaces per level)
out: NOML text (empty string if the index is invalid)
`
  'noml.pool.stringify-arr': `
serializes an array (one element per line, with a trailing comma)
idx: array node index
indent: indentation level
out: NOML text
`
  'noml.pool.stringify-obj': `
Serializes an object (one member per line, annotations output above the member)
idx: object node index
indent: indentation level
out: NOML text
`
  'noml.pool.val-bool': `
Gets a boolean value
idx: node index
v: ok=boolean value, nil=value is not boolean
`
  'noml.pool.val-i64': `
get the integer value (truncating decimals)
idx: node index
v: ok=truncated integer, nil=value is not a number
`
  'noml.pool.val-num': `
gets a numeric value
idx: node index
v: ok=numeric value, nil=value is not a number
`
  'noml.pool.val-str': `
gets the string value
idx: node index
v: ok=string content, nil=value is not a string
`
  'noml.value': `
value: node (pure scalar fields, safe to store in a heap vec)
kind/num-val/bool-val: scalar values
count: number of child elements of the array/object
first/last: head/tail edge index of the singly linked list of children in the edge table (-1=no children)
`
  'num': `
num — num union type and num methods

num = int | float (alias for the integer/float union type).
The member union types int / float are in std/int.no and std/float.no.
This module carries the methods of the num type: abs, clamp, sign.
The generic numeric functions max / min are in the math module;
Concrete type operations (range constants, type conversions, to-str, bitwise operations, etc.) are in the number module.
`
  'num.abs': `
absolute value — works for every num type (integers and floats)
call form: abs(x) (x is the receiver; a zero-argument num method currently supports only this form, because
x.abs() dot form is not supported — the nested num union does not monomorphise the receiver yet — same as num.sign);
negatives wrap under overflow=wrap (the absolute value of i64.MIN is out of i64 range)
r: non-negatives returned unchanged; negatives return the negated value (the most negative integer wraps back to itself, staying negative)
`
  'num.clamp': `
clamps into [lo, hi]
usage: x.clamp(lo, hi, r)
Ordered short-circuit judgment: lower bound first, then upper bound. So when lo > hi (reversed interval) the lower bound wins,
Values below lo are raised to lo; values above hi are pushed down to hi.
lo: the lower bound (inclusive); inputs smaller than lo yield lo
hi: the upper bound (inclusive); inputs greater than hi yield hi
r: the clamped value, inside [lo, hi] (when the interval is reversed, one of {lo, hi})
`
  'num.sign': `
the sign (-1, 0, 1) — works for every num type (integers and floats)
Accepts ±0.0 and the various integer types; NaN falls to 0 because both comparisons are false.
r: 1=greater than zero, -1=less than zero, 0=equal to zero (±0.0 included; NaN takes the else branch)
`
  'number': `
number — number operations (method-form API)

All "method-form" APIs of the std numeric family are collected in this module: concrete-type to-str
(i64/i32/i16/i8/u64/u32/u16/u8/byte/f64/f32), f64 configurable-precision to-str
(f64.to-str-fixed / f64.to-str-sci / f64.to-str-general; the former str.f64-to-str-*
functions were moved into this module and converted to method form), numeric width conversion methods
(f64.to-i64, i64.to-f64, f32.to-f64, etc. #{buildin}), f64/f32 math methods
(sqrt, cbrt, trigonometric/hyperbolic, ceil/floor/round/trunc, exp/log, degrees/radians),
integer sqrt / is-prime.
The "function-form" APIs (max, min, pow, hypot, atan2, fmod, clamp, even, odd, gcd,
lcm, div, mod, swap, arr-zero, char-to-str, bitwise operations)
are in the math module (std/math.no); the sole exception: integer-power pow (int generic) conflicts
by name with math's pow (f64), so it stays in this module.
note: the old function-form width conversions (math.i64-to-f64, etc.) and integer/float to-str
(math.i64/u64/f64-to-str) were removed — these are now carried directly by this module's methods above.
Union type aliases and member methods: num → std/num.no, int → std/int.no,
float → std/float.no.
Range constants (for range checks such as str.to-i8): the MIN/MAX and INF of i8/i16/i32/i64/u8/u16/u32/u64
remain in this module.
`
  'number.byte.to-i64': `
byte to i64 (unsigned zero-extension)
out: the equivalent i64
`
  'number.byte.to-str': `
converts a byte to a string (unsigned; widened to u64 and reused)
out: decimal representation (byte as unsigned 0..255, formatted after widening to u64)
`
  'number.byte.to-u64': `
converts byte to u64 (unsigned zero-extension)
out: equivalent u64
`
  'number.exp-f64': `
Exponentials and logarithms (f64/f32 methods)
free-function form (takes parameter x rather than a method receiver).
Serves as the single implementation source for f64.exp / cbrt / sinh / cosh / f32.exp.
Note: Nolang currently has an issue with "method calls on named region variables" (like t.exp())
codegen bug would wrongly use the outer receiver; so every indirect call of exp goes through this
a free function, avoiding that problem.
x: the exponent (f64)
res: e raised to the power x (x>709 returns +Inf, x<-745 returns 0.0; range halving then a z⁸ Taylor series)
`
  'number.f32.acos': `
Arccosine (f32 method, widened to f64 then reused)
res: acos(x) (result narrowed back to f32)
`
  'number.f32.asin': `
arcsine (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: asin(x) (result narrowed back to f32)
`
  'number.f32.atan': `
arctangent (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: atan(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.cbrt': `
Cube root (f32 method; widens to f64 and reuses f64.cbrt)
res: ³√x (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.ceil': `
round up (f32 method; widened to f64 and reused)
res: ceil(x) (result narrowed back to f32)
`
  'number.f32.cos': `
cosine
res: cos(x) (widened to f64 for computation, then narrowed)
`
  'number.f32.cosh': `
hyperbolic cosine (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: cosh(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.degrees': `
radians to degrees (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: degree value (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.exp': `
Exponential
res: e raised to the power x (widened to f64 for the computation then narrowed)
`
  'number.f32.floor': `
Round down (f32 method, widened to f64 then reused)
res: floor(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.log': `
logarithm
res: ln(x) (widened to f64 for computation, then narrowed)
`
  'number.f32.log10': `
Common logarithm (f32 method; widens to f64 and reuses)
res: log10(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.log2': `
base-2 logarithm (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: log2(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.radians': `
Degrees to radians (f32 method, widened to f64 then reused)
res: radian value (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.round': `
Rounds to nearest (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: round(x) (result narrowed back to f32)
`
  'number.f32.sin': `
sine
res: sin(x) (widened to f64 for the computation, then narrowed)
`
  'number.f32.sinh': `
hyperbolic sine (f32 method, widened to f64 and reused)
res: sinh(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.sqrt': `
Square root
res: √x (widened to f64 for the computation then narrowed; negative returns NaN)
`
  'number.f32.tan': `
tangent
res: tan(x) (widened to f64 for computation, then narrowed)
`
  'number.f32.tanh': `
Hyperbolic tangent (f32 method, widened to f64 then reused)
res: tanh(x) (result narrowed to f32)
`
  'number.f32.to-f64': `
widens an f32 to f64
out: the equivalent f64 (exact widening, no precision loss)
`
  'number.f32.to-str': `
converts an f32 to a string (method)
Widens through f32.to-f64 (a #{buildin}) and reuses the Nolang %g implementation of f64.to-str.
out: decimal string (widened to f64 then through the f64 %g shortest round-trip implementation)
`
  'number.f32.trunc': `
Truncates toward zero (f32 method, widened to f64 then reused)
res: trunc(x) (result narrowed back to f32)
`
  'number.f64.acos': `
Arccosine (acos = atan2(√(1-x²), x))
res: acos(x) (principal range [0, π]; returns NaN when |x|>1)
`
  'number.f64.asin': `
Arcsine (asin = atan2(x, √(1-x²)))
res: asin(x) (principal range [-π/2, π/2]; |x|>1 returns NaN)
`
  'number.f64.atan': `
Arctangent (atan = atan2(x, 1.0))
res: atan(x) (range (-π/2, π/2))
`
  'number.f64.cbrt': `
cube root (f64 method)
Implemented with exp(log(|x|) / 3); the method body does not call pow (integer power pow is in this module, f64 pow is in std/math.no)
res: cube root of x (implemented as exp(log(|x|)/3) carrying the sign; 0 returns 0.0)
`
  'number.f64.ceil': `
Rounding (f64/f32 methods; on integer types rounding is the identity, so no generic version is offered)
res: the smallest integer value not less than x (rounds up, result still f64)
`
  'number.f64.cos': `
cosine
res: cos(x) (x in radians)
`
  'number.f64.cosh': `
Hyperbolic cosine
res: cosh(x) = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2
`
  'number.f64.degrees': `
radians to degrees (f64/f32 method)
res: x converted from radians to degrees (× 180/π)
`
  'number.f64.exp': `
Exponential
res: e raised to the x power (delegates to the exp-f64 free function)
`
  'number.f64.floor': `
rounds down
res: largest integer value not greater than x (rounded down, still f64)
`
  'number.f64.log': `
natural logarithm
res: ln(x) natural logarithm (x≤0 returns NaN; first reduce the range to [1,√2) then approximate with a z³ series)
`
  'number.f64.log10': `
common logarithm (base 10)
res: log10(x) = ln(x)/ln(10)
`
  'number.f64.log2': `
Base-2 logarithm
res: log2(x) = ln(x)/ln(2)
`
  'number.f64.radians': `
degrees to radians (f64/f32 method)
res: x converted from degrees to radians (× π/180)
`
  'number.f64.round': `
rounds to nearest (rounds .5 up, symmetric for negatives)
res: integer value closest to x (rounds away from zero when |fraction|≥0.5)
`
  'number.f64.sin': `
Trigonometric functions (radians; f64/f32 methods)
res: sin(x) (x in radians, polynomial approximation)
`
  'number.f64.sinh': `
Hyperbolic functions (f64/f32 methods)
res: sinh(x) = (eˣ - e⁻ˣ)/2
`
  'number.f64.sqrt': `
Powers and roots (f64/f32 methods)
res: √x (native libm sqrt; negatives return NaN)
`
  'number.f64.tan': `
tangent (libm tan, equivalent to sin/cos)
res: tan(x) = sin(x)/cos(x) (diverges where cos(x)≈0)
`
  'number.f64.tanh': `
hyperbolic tangent
res: tanh(x) = (e²ˣ-1)/(e²ˣ+1) (range (-1, 1))
`
  'number.f64.to-bits': `
extracts the 64-bit IEEE-754 bit pattern of an f64 as a u64 (bitcast double->i64).
Registered as ForwardFunc "f64-to-bits" by src/builtin/bits.go, implemented as a native LLVM bitcast and independent of libm.
f64.to-str takes the bit pattern first, then splits sign/exp/frac, before expanding the shortest round-trip form.
out: the 64-bit pattern of the IEEE-754 double (bitcast, not a numeric conversion)
`
  'number.f64.to-f32': `
narrows an f64 to f32 (may lose precision)
out: the narrowed f32 (may lose precision or overflow to ±Inf)
`
  'number.f64.to-i32': `
float to 32-bit signed integer (saturating)
out: the i32 truncated toward zero (out-of-range saturates)
`
  'number.f64.to-i64': `
float to integer (truncating)
out: the integer truncated toward zero (fraction dropped, e.g. 3.9→3, -3.9→-3)
`
  'number.f64.to-str': `
f64 to string (direct Nolang implementation, **shortest round-trip** decimal, aligned with Go's %v)
Takes "the fewest significant digits that uniquely recover this double" (IEEE-754 bit decomposition + bigint exact arithmetic),
And follows %g's notation choice: E <= -5 or E >= 6 uses scientific notation, otherwise fixed-point.
Special values align with Go: NaN → "NaN", +Inf → "+Inf", -Inf → "-Inf", ±0 → "0".
The backend has no native f64 formatter (the sprintf CLibCall was removed), so the whole routine is inlined in the method body.
out: shortest round-trip decimal string (fewest significant digits that uniquely recover this double; scientific notation when E≤-5 or E≥6; NaN/±Inf aligned with Go)
`
  'number.f64.to-str-fixed': `
fixed-point conversion
precision: number of fractional digits (>= 0; keep it <= 17 to avoid i64 overflow)
returns out: the string form (e.g. "3.14", "-0.005", "100")
`
  'number.f64.to-str-general': `
general format (chooses fixed point or scientific notation automatically)
precision: maximum number of significant digits (>= 1)
returns out: the string form (trailing zeros stripped automatically)
`
  'number.f64.to-u32': `
float to 32-bit unsigned integer (saturating)
out: the u32 truncated toward zero (out-of-range saturates)
`
  'number.f64.to-u64': `
float to 64-bit unsigned integer (saturating)
out: the u64 truncated toward zero (negatives and out-of-range saturate)
`
  'number.f64.trunc': `
truncates to an integer (drops the fraction)
res: integer part of x (truncated toward zero, e.g. -3.9→-3.0)
`
  'number.i128.is-prime': `
determines whether it is prime
yes: whether it is prime (128-bit signed, n<2 always false)
`
  'number.i128.max': `
i128: -170141183460469231731687303715884105728 ~ 170141183460469231731687303715884105727
Type-level constant values come from the typeConstantText128 constant table in hir2mir (the i128 lane uses
full-precision decimal text); the following is defined for API/documentation use (consistent with other widths)
`
  'number.i128.sqrt': '── 128-bit integer method copies (i128 / u128) ──\n128-bit arithmetic itself works (operands go through the LLVM i128 path), but two points must be handled:\n① region variables need explicit types (`n i128 = .`, `lo i128 = 1` …). Without types,\nchecker infers intermediate values mixed with literals as i64, so the loop converges on a wrong value or even\nhangs (the binary search for i128.MAX behaves exactly so). Untyped u128 also errors on an i64→u128 assignment.\n② overflow-annotation layout: i128 (signed, division may overflow INT_MIN/-1) adds an\n`#{overflow=wrap}`; u128 (unsigned, ÷/% cannot overflow) only applies to + − and "division by a literal"\noperations get an annotation, while ÷/% between variables do not (otherwise overflow-ineffective warnings).\ncomments must be on their own line above the target statement (a guard chained with `;` on one line triggers\n128-bit codegen drops the whole main).\nAlso fixes codegen isUnsignedRaw omitting u128: comparisons/division of u128 previously went down the signed path by mistake\nsign predicate misorders the u128.MAX range (≥ 2^127); after the fix u128.sqrt(u128.MAX)=2^64-1 is exact.\nr: floor(√n) (128-bit; typed region variable + overflow-wrap annotation)'
  'number.i128.to-f64': `
128-bit signed integer to float
out: the equivalent f64 (precision lost when the 128-bit magnitude is large)
`
  'number.i16.is-prime': `
determines whether it is prime
yes: whether it is prime (always false for n<2)
`
  'number.i16.min': 'i16: -32768 ~ 32767'
  'number.i16.sqrt': `
i16
r: floor(√n) (signed, returns 0 when n<0)
`
  'number.i16.to-i64': `
i16 to i64
out: equivalent i64
`
  'number.i16.to-str': `
converts an i16 to a string (widened to i64 and reused)
out: decimal representation (formatted after widening to i64)
`
  'number.i32.is-prime': `
determine whether it is prime
yes: whether it is a prime number (always false for n<2)
`
  'number.i32.min': 'i32: -2147483648 ~ 2147483647'
  'number.i32.sqrt': `
i32
r: floor(√n) (signed, returns 0 when n<0)
`
  'number.i32.to-i64': `
i32 to i64
out: equivalent i64
`
  'number.i32.to-str': `
converts an i32 to a string (implicitly widened to i64, then reusing the native i64.to-str path)
out: decimal representation (implicitly widened to i64 then reusing the native path)
`
  'number.i64.is-prime': `
Whether it is prime — an i64 method
trial division O(√n): rule out evens first, then try odd divisors; termination uses d <= n/d
avoids d*d overflow; the divisor is always ≥ 1 so it never traps (likewise no int union receiver, see above)
yes: whether it is prime (always false for n<2; divisible only by 1 and itself)
`
  'number.i64.max': `
i64: -9223372036854775808 ~ 9223372036854775807
MIN cannot be written directly as a literal (the parser does not support it), express it with -MAX - 1
`
  'number.i64.sqrt': 'Integer square root floor(√n) — an i64 method\nn < 0 has no solution in the integer domain and returns 0; binary search O(log n), comparing with n/mid rather than\nmid*mid, no overflow (following the wrap-annotation convention of gcd/even in math.no).\nDoes not use an int union receiver (which would work across all widths): the int alias is defined in std/int.no,\ndeclaring an `int.X` method across modules gets double-prefixed by prefixMethodNames into\n`int.int.X`; the call site\'s monomorphization finds no match and reports unknown callee.\nr: floor(√n) (n<0 returns 0; n<2 returns n itself; binary search O(log n))'
  'number.i64.to-bytes-be': `
converts an int64 to 8 big-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-be()   // out = [0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x12,0x34]
out: an 8-byte array (high byte first, big endian)
`
  'number.i64.to-bytes-le': `
converts an int64 to 8 little-endian bytes
usage:
v = 0x1234
out = v.to-bytes-le()   // out = [0x34,0x12,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00,0x00]
out: an 8-byte array (low byte first, little endian)
`
  'number.i64.to-f64': `
integer to float
out: the equivalent f64 (integers with magnitude above 2^53 may lose precision)
`
  'number.i64.to-i32': `
narrows the integer width (truncating to the low 32 bits)
out: the low-32-bit truncated result (high bits discarded, no range check)
`
  'number.i64.to-str': 'i64 to string\n`#{buildin}: the underlying layer uses the native @str_from_i64 (builtin strconv.go registers the ForwardFunc "i64-to-str",\n(the compiler names the native inline path after it). i64 has a native formatter, so it is a direct builtin — no Nolang loop wrapper.\nout: decimal representation (inlined via native @str_from_i64, e.g. -42 → \'-42\')'
  'number.i8.is-prime': `
