  'os': `
os — operating system interface standard library

Provides OS-level functions: environment variables, directory operations, process management, system info, time, etc.
For file read/write functions, see the fs module.
`
  'os.arg': `
// LLVM: access argc from main
}
arg: gets the i-th command-line argument (nil when out of range)
idx: argument ordinal (0 is the program name)
val: ok=the idx-th argument, nil=out of range
`
  'os.args': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
command-line arguments
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
args: all command-line arguments ([]str, including the program name)
argv: the full command line (including the program name argv[0])
`
  'os.ch-dir': `
changes the working directory
d: the target directory path
ok: true=switch succeeded
`
  'os.ch-mod': `
changes file permissions
path: target file path
mode: new permissions (e.g. 0o644)
ok: true=change succeeded
`
  'os.chown': `
changes a file's owner and group (uid, gid)
path: target file path
uid: the new owner's user ID
gid: the new group ID
ok: true=change succeeded
`
  'os.chroot': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
root directory switch
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
chroot: changes the root directory (POSIX chroot(2))
path: the new root directory path
returns true on success, false on failure
Note: requires root privileges; after calling it, set the working directory with ch-dir('/')
ok: true=switch succeeded (requires root privileges)
`
  'os.flock': `
applies or releases an advisory lock on a file descriptor
fd: the destination file descriptor
op: the lock operation (1=LOCK_SH shared, 2=LOCK_EX exclusive, 4=LOCK_UN unlock, 8=LOCK_NB non-blocking)
returns true on success, false on failure
ok: true=lock/unlock succeeded
`
  'os.get-arch': `
gets the current CPU architecture (a compile-time constant, e.g. arm64, amd64)
arch: compile-time CPU architecture string (e.g. arm64, amd64)
`
  'os.get-domain-name': `
gets the NIS domain name
returns the domain string on success, an empty string on failure
name: NIS domain name (empty string on failure)
`
  'os.get-env': `
gets an environment variable
returns val: ok = the value, nil = unset
key: environment variable name
`
  'os.get-errno': `
gets the last errno value
errno: the errno left by the most recent system call
`
  'os.get-host-id': `
gets the host identifier (a 32-bit integer)
returns the host ID (a non-negative integer; platforms differ on error)
id: the host identifier (a 32-bit non-negative integer)
`
  'os.get-login': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
user and system information extras
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
get-login: gets the name of the currently logged-in user
returns the user name string on success, an empty string on failure
name: current logged-in user name (empty string on failure)
`
  'os.get-pid': `
gets the process ID
pid: PID of the current process
`
  'os.get-priority': `
gets the scheduling priority of a process
which: the kind of target (0=PRIO_PROCESS, 1=PRIO_PGRP, 2=PRIO_USER)
who: the target ID (0 means the current process/group/user)
returns prio: ok = the priority (-20 to 19), nil = it could not be read
Note: libc conventionally returns -1 and sets errno on error, but a priority can legitimately be -1, so
errno==0 is what distinguishes them. Here ?i64 states it explicitly (nil means error).
`
  'os.get-wd': `
gets the current working directory
dir: the absolute path of the current working directory
`
  'os.getegid': `
gets the current effective group ID
egid: the effective group ID
`
  'os.geteuid': `
gets the current effective user ID
euid: the effective user ID
`
  'os.getgid': `
gets the current group ID
gid: the real group ID
`
  'os.getgroups': `
gets the group IDs the current user belongs to
returns the group ID array and the count n on success, an empty array and n=0 on failure
gids: the array of group IDs the user belongs to (empty on failure)
n: number of groups (0 on failure)
`
  'os.getuid': `
gets the current user ID
uid: the actual user ID
`
  'os.host-name': `
gets the host name
name: host name (empty string when retrieval fails)
`
  'os.mkdir': `
creates a directory
p: the directory path to create
mode: directory permissions (e.g. 0o755)
ok: true=created successfully
`
  'os.now': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
time
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
now: gets the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
`
  'os.now-ms': `
gets the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
`
  'os.now-ns': `
gets the current timestamp (nanoseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (nanoseconds)
`
  'os.now-us': `
