`
  'os.num-cpu': `
gets the number of online CPU cores
Equivalent to sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN); the compiler handles the per-platform constants
n: number of online CPU cores
`
  'os.set-env': `
sets an environment variable
k: environment variable name
v: the value to set
ok: true=set successfully
`
  'os.set-priority': `
sets the scheduling priority of a process
which: the kind of target (0=PRIO_PROCESS, 1=PRIO_PGRP, 2=PRIO_USER)
who: the target ID (0 means the current process/group/user)
prio: the priority value (-20 highest to 19 lowest)
returns true on success, false on failure
ok: true=set successfully
`
  'os.set-sid': `
creates a new session and detaches from the controlling terminal
returns the new process group ID (>0) on success, -1 on failure
pid: new process group ID (-1 on failure)
`
  'os.signal': `
installs a signal handler (a simplified version that only supports SIG_IGN=1 / SIG_DFL=0)
sig: the signal number
handler: the handler constant (0=SIG_DFL default, 1=SIG_IGN ignore)
returns the previous handler value
prev: the handler value of that signal before the setting
`
  'os.sleep': `
sleeps for n seconds
s: seconds to sleep
ok: returns 0 when the full sleep elapsed; returns the remaining unslept seconds when interrupted by a signal
`
  'os.sleep-ns': `
sleeps for n nanoseconds
ns: sleep duration in nanoseconds
`
  'os.sleep-us': `
sleeps for n microseconds
us: microseconds to sleep
`
  'os.stat-gid': `
gets a file's group gid
returns gid: ok = the gid, nil = it could not be read
path: file path
`
  'os.stat-mode': `
gets the file mode (permission and type bits)
returns mode: ok = the mode value, nil = it could not be read
path: file path
`
  'os.stat-mtime': `
gets the file modification time (Unix seconds)
returns mtime: ok = the modification time, nil = it could not be read
path: file path
`
  'os.stat-uid': `
gets a file's owner uid
returns uid: ok = the uid, nil = it could not be read
path: file path
`
  'os.strerror': `
turns a POSIX errno into a human-readable error message
Implemented as a Nolang lookup table, replacing the libc @strerror CLibCall
Covers the standard POSIX errno values (1-40); an unknown value returns "Unknown error: N"
errnum: POSIX errno value (1~40)
msg: readable error message (unknown values return "Unknown error: N")
`
  'os.sysconf': `
queries a system configuration limit
name: the configuration item (a POSIX _SC_* constant; the numbers differ per platform)
_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN: macOS=58, Linux=84
_SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF:  macOS=57, Linux=83
returns the value on success, -1 on failure
val: the value of that _SC_ item (returns -1 on failure)
`
  'os.sysctl': `
queries a system control parameter (reading a string value)
name: the parameter name (e.g. 'kern.ostype', 'hw.model')
returns val: ok = the value string, nil = the query failed
Note: on Linux this maps to /proc/sys; macOS/BSD go through the sysctl(2) syscall
`
  'os.syslog': `
writes a system log entry
priority: the log priority (0=EMERG to 7=DEBUG, 6=INFO being the common one)
msg: the message body
`
  'os.ttyname': `
gets the terminal name
fd: the file descriptor pointing at the terminal
returns the terminal path string on success, an empty string on failure
name: terminal device path (empty string on failure)
`
  'os.uname': `
gets kernel and architecture information
returns a utsname struct with the five fields sysname/nodename/release/version/machine
u: the utsname struct (sysname/nodename/release/version/machine)
`
  'os.utsname': `
utsname: OS information struct (result of the uname system call)
Each field is a null-terminated string (up to 64 characters)
`
