  'path.dir': `
takes the directory part of the path
Modifies .p in place AND returns the modified .p (consistent with base()/ext()/join(); see the note on join)
out: the directory part after removing the file name (.p updated in place and returned)
`
  'path.dot': 'current directory (\'.\' = 46; ditto, double-quote char literal)'
  'path.exists': `
checks whether the path exists (a file or a directory both count)
yes: true=the path (file or directory) exists
`
  'path.ext': `
takes the file extension (including the dot)
returns the extension
out: extension including the leading dot (empty string when there is none)
`
  'path.is-abs': `
reports whether the path is absolute
returns true = yes, false = no
yes: true=.p is an absolute path, false=relative path
`
  'path.is-dir': `
checks whether the path is a directory
yes: true=.p points to a directory
`
  'path.is-file': `
checks whether the path is a file
yes: true=.p points to a regular file
`
  'path.join': `
⚠️ API consistency: join()/dir() originally only modified in place and returned nothing, while base()/ext()
returned a str, which made `x = p.join(...)` silently yield an empty string. All four methods now RETURN
a str (join/dir still update .p as well, so the existing in-place usage keeps working).
b: the path element appended to the end of .p
out: the complete path string joined after handling leading/trailing slashes (.p also updated in place)
`
  'path.make-dir': `
creates the directory
returns true = success, false = failure
ok: true=directory created successfully, false=failure
`
  'path.path': 'path: value object carrying a path in the string field p (all methods operate on .p)'
  'path.remove': `
deletes the file
returns true = success, false = failure
ok: true=file deleted successfully, false=failure
`
  'path.rename': `
renames this path to new-p
returns true = success, false = failure
new-p: target path string after renaming
ok: true=rename succeeded, false=failure
`
  'path.sep': `
Path separator ('/' = 47)
⚠️ must be a **char literal (double quotes)**, not a string literal `SEP = '/'`:
single quotes are str in Nolang; comparing a byte to a str is always false,
which makes `c == SEP` always false → dir()/base()/ext() never find the separator
(last-sep stays at -1, dir() always returns '.', base() returns the whole string).
SEP/DOT are only for **writing** (`.p[n] = SEP`); use SEP-BYTE / DOT-BYTE below when scanning.
`
  'path.sep-byte': `
Byte-version separator constant. `.p.byte(i)` reads a **numeric value** (not char), so comparisons in the scan loop
must use numeric constants. All "reads" go through .byte(i): the semantics are bytes, and share the same unit as the len-bytes(),
out-n byte cursors — mixing code-point indexes (.p[i]) with byte cursors will break non-ASCII
paths (see tests/std-byte-indexing.no).
`
  'path.size': `
gets the file size
sz: the byte size of the file .p points at
`
  'path.split': `
splits the path into a directory and a file name
Modifies .p in place to the directory part and returns the file name
e.g. path{ p: '/a/b/c.txt' }.split() -> .p='/a/b', f='c.txt'
(struct literals must span several lines; the above is shorthand)
f: the file name split off (.p is changed in place to its directory part)
`
  'pbkdf2': `
pbkdf2 — PBKDF2 key derivation function (RFC 2898 / PKCS#5 v2.0)

Uses HMAC-SHA256 as the pseudorandom function (PRF) to derive keys through iteration.

usage:
# std/crypto/pbkdf2
password []byte = 'password'
salt []byte = 'salt'
iterations = 10000
key-len = 32
derived = pbkdf2-hmac-sha256(password, salt, iterations, key-len)
`
  'pbkdf2.pbkdf2-hmac-sha1': `
PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA1
Legacy protocol compatibility only; new designs should use the SHA-256 / SHA-512 variants.
password: password (UTF-8 byte sequence)
salt: salt
iterations: iteration count; larger resists brute force, too small is insecure
key-len: desired key length in bytes, generated block by block of 20 bytes
returns out: derived key, exactly key-len bytes long
`
  'pbkdf2.pbkdf2-hmac-sha256': `
PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA256
password: password (UTF-8 byte sequence)
salt: salt, at least 16 bytes and unique per account recommended
iterations: iteration count; larger resists brute force (100000+ is common), too small is insecure
key-len: desired key length in bytes, generated block by block of 32 bytes
returns out: derived key, exactly key-len bytes long
`
  'pbkdf2.pbkdf2-hmac-sha512': `
PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA512
password: password (UTF-8 byte sequence)
salt: salt, at least 16 bytes and unique per account recommended
iterations: iteration count; larger resists brute force, too small is insecure
key-len: desired key length in bytes, generated block by block of 64 bytes
returns out: derived key, exactly key-len bytes long
`
  'pem': `
encoding/pem — PEM format encoding/decoding (RFC 7468)

PEM (Privacy-Enhanced Mail) format is widely used for X.509 certificates, RSA/ECDSA keys, etc.
format:
-----BEGIN LABEL-----
<Base64-encoded data, 64 characters per line>
-----END LABEL-----

usage:
; encode
pem-str = pem.encode('CERTIFICATE', raw-bytes)

; decode
result = pem.decode(pem-str)
result: {
ok -> {
label = result.label
data = result.data
}
}
`
  'pem.block': `
block: PEM decode result
label: the label between BEGIN/END (e.g. 'CERTIFICATE')
data: the Base64-decoded binary payload
`
  'pem.decode': `
Parses a PEM format string (takes only the first BEGIN…END block in the input)
pem-str: text containing at least one '-----BEGIN <label>-----' … '-----END <label>-----' section
result: ok=label and decoded payload, nil=missing marker, Base64 decode failure or content too short
`
  'pem.encode': `
Encode raw bytes as a PEM format string
label: PEM label (e.g. 'CERTIFICATE', 'RSA PRIVATE KEY'), must match BEGIN/END
data: raw binary data, encoded by base64.encode-std then wrapped at 64 characters
out: the complete PEM text, including BEGIN/END lines and a trailing newline on each line
`
  'pem.pem-line-len': 'Maximum Base64 characters per line'
  'pool': `
pool — TCP connection pool

Provides pooled management of TCP connections, avoiding frequent creation and closing of connections.
Supports connection reuse, a maximum connection limit, and idle-timeout reclamation.

usage:
p = pool{}
p.init('127.0.0.1', 8080, 10)    // init: target 127.0.0.1:8080, max 10 connections
c = p.get()                       // get a connection (returns ?net.conn)
c: {
nil -> print('no connection')
->
c.send('hello')
p.put(c)                   // return the connection (not close)
}
p.close-all()                     // close all connections
`
  'pool.create': `
Create and initialize a connection pool
host: target server hostname or IP
port: target server port
max-size: maximum number of connections the pool may hold
returns p: an initialized pool instance
`
  'pool.pool': `
pool: TCP connection pool
host: target host
port: target port
max-size: maximum number of connections
conns: connection array
in-use: whether a connection is in use
count: current number of connections
`
  'pool.pool-idle-timeout': 'Idle timeout (milliseconds)'
  'pool.pool-max-conns': 'Connection pool maximum capacity'
  'pool.pool.active-count': `
Get the number of active connections
returns n: active connection count
`
  'pool.pool.close-all': 'Close all connections'
  'pool.pool.close-conn': `
Close and remove a specific connection
idx: index in the pool of the connection to close and remove
`
  'pool.pool.get': `
Acquire a connection (reuse an idle one first, otherwise create a new one)
returns c: ok = connection, nil = failure
`
  'pool.pool.idle-count': `
Get the number of idle connections
returns n: idle connection count
`
  'pool.pool.init': `
Initialize the connection pool
host: target server hostname or IP
port: target server port
max-size: maximum number of connections the pool may hold simultaneously
`
  'pool.pool.put': `
Return a connection to the pool (without closing it)
c: connection to put back into the pool (kept open, not closed)
`
  'pool.pool.total-count': `
Get the total number of connections
returns n: total connection count
`
  'pool.with-size': `
Create a connection pool with the default size
host: target server hostname or IP
port: target server port
returns p: connection pool with the default cap of 8
`
  'process': `
process — process operations

Provides process creation, standard stream retrieval, process waiting, process information query, and more.
The underlying implementation uses POSIX fork/exec/pipe/waitpid.

usage:
p = process.new()
p.start('ls', '-la')
code = p.wait()
print('exit code: ' - code.to-str())

// low-level launch method (supports args, dir, input, merge-err)
p.start-with-opts('ls', ['-la'], '', '', false)
code = p.wait()

// convenience method: capture stdout output
content, code = process.new().output('ls', '-la')

// non-blocking spawn + wait
pid = process.spawn('echo hello')
code = process.wait(pid)

// convenience: run a shell command synchronously (spawn + wait combined)
code = process.shell('echo hello')

// full-featured subprocess runner (flat parameters: program, args, dir, stdin, env, timeout, merge-err)
out, stderr, code, err = process.cmd('ls', args, '', '', env, 5000, false)
print('out=' - out - ' code=' - code.to-str())

// struct result (returns exec-result, chainable access to stdout/stderr/code)
o = process.cmdopts{timeout: 5000}
res = process.exec('ls', ['-la'], o)
res.code == 0 -> print(res.stdout)
res.code != 0 -> print('error: ' - res.code.to-str())
`
  'process.close': 'closes every pipe and waits for the child process'
  'process.close-stderr': 'closes the child\'s stderr pipe'
  'process.close-stdin': 'closes the child\'s stdin pipe'
  'process.close-stdout': 'closes the child\'s stdout pipe'
  'process.cmd': `
cmd: full-featured subprocess executor (cross-platform production-grade implementation)
program   : program name to execute (PATH search)
args      : argument list passed to the program
dir       : working directory; empty string = inherit parent process working directory
input     : content written to the subprocess stdin; empty string = no stdin provided
(the parameter is named input rather than stdin because std/fs.no already predefines global stdin/file handles;
a same-named validated name would collide with that global's SSA name %stdin, causing callers to misuse the global.)
env       : environment variables (["K=V", ...]); empty array = inherit parent process environment
timeout   : timeout (milliseconds), <=0 = wait indefinitely
merge-err : true = merge stderr into the captured out
returns:
out: full standard output of the subprocess (when merge-err is true, the interleaved stdout+stderr result)
stderr: full standard error of the subprocess (when merge-err is true, an empty string)
code: exit code; >=0 normal exit, -1 indicates startup / exec failure, -2 indicates timeout (subprocess killed)
err: error description for startup failure or timeout; empty string on success
POSIX version: implemented in pure Nolang (process-fork/pipe/dup2/exec-shell/waitpid).
Does not depend on the process.c C runtime, symmetric with the Windows version.
`
  'process.cmd-2': `
a full-featured child-process runner (a production-grade cross-platform implementation)
Windows version: implemented in pure Nolang on the Win32 API (CreateProcessA, CreatePipe and friends).
It does not depend on the process.c runtime; process creation, pipe management and output capture all go through
ForwardFunc builtins such as win-create-process, win-create-pipe and win-read-pipe.
program   : program name to run (spliced into a command line launched via CreateProcessA)
args      : the argument list passed to the program (items containing spaces are automatically double-quoted)
dir       : working directory; empty string = inherit the parent's working directory
input     : content written to the child's stdin; empty string = provide no stdin
env       : environment variables (the Windows variant is not yet applied to child processes)
timeout   : timeout in milliseconds, <=0 = wait forever; a timeout forcibly terminates the process
merge-err : true = merge stderr into the captured out
out       : captured stdout (includes stderr when merge-err)
stderr    : captured stderr (empty when merge-err)
code      : exit code (-1=pipe/spawn/waitpid failed, -2=timed out)
err       : the error message (empty=no error)
`
  'process.cmdopts': `
cmdopts: execution options for process.start-with-opts (cross-module struct example).
Fields correspond to the flattened parameters of process.cmd:
dir       : working directory; empty string = inherit the parent process's working directory
stdin     : content written to the child process's stdin; empty string = no stdin provided
env       : environment variables (["K=V", ...]); empty array = inherit the parent process's environment
timeout   : timeout in milliseconds, <=0 = wait indefinitely
merge-err : true = merge stderr into the captured out
`
  'process.exec': `
usage:
o = process.cmdopts{timeout: 5000}
res = process.exec('ls', ['-la'], o)
res.code == 0 -> print(res.stdout)
res.code != 0 -> print('error code: ' + res.code.to-str())
program: program name to run
args: the argument list passed to the program
o: execution options (dir/stdin/env/timeout/merge-err)
res: execution result struct (stdout/stderr/code)
`
  'process.exec-result': `
exec-result: result struct returned by exec
stdout : captured stdout output (includes stderr when merge-err)
stderr : captured stderr output (empty when merge-err)
code   : exit code (>=0=normal, 127=command not found, -1=start failure, -2=timeout)
`
  'process.exit-code-of': `
Get the child process exit code (only valid after wait)
code: the recorded exit code (0 when not yet waited on)
`
  'process.find-by-name': `
finds process PIDs by name
name: the process name (partial match, case-insensitive)
returns the list of matching PIDs
pids: PIDs of processes whose name partially matches (case-insensitive)
`
  'process.find-match': `
partial, case-insensitive match on a process name
pname: process name
target: the search keyword (substring)
returns yes = true = matched
yes: whether pname lowercased contains target lowercased
`
  'process.force-kill': `
sends SIG-KILL
returns ok = true = success
ok: whether SIG-KILL was sent successfully
`
  'process.is-running': `
reports whether the child process is still running
yes: .running==1, i.e. started and not yet reaped by wait
`
  'process.kill': `
sends a signal to the child process
sig: the signal number (e.g. SIG-TERM=15, SIG-KILL=9)
returns ok = true = success
ok: whether the signal was sent successfully
`
  'process.list-all': `
lists every process on the system
Linux: walks /proc and reads /proc/<pid>/comm under each PID directory for the process name
macOS/Windows: returns an empty list for now (this needs libproc/sysctl or CreateToolhelp32Snapshot support)
returns the proc-info list
procs: (pid, name) list of all processes on the system
`
  'process.list-all-2': `
macOS variant: not implemented, returns an empty list (requires libproc proc_listallpids or sysctl)
procs: empty process list
`
  'process.list-all-3': `
Windows variant: not implemented, returns an empty list (requires CreateToolhelp32Snapshot)
procs: empty process list
`
  'process.new': `
creates and returns a process instance
usage: p = process.new()
p: the new process instance (not started yet, fields are zero values)
`
  'process.output': `
runs a command and captures its stdout (an instance method)
usage: content, code = process.new().output('ls', '-la')
