  'proxy': `
proxy — HTTP/HTTPS proxy support

Provides HTTP CONNECT proxy tunneling, allowing access to HTTP and HTTPS services through a proxy server.

usage:
// connect through an HTTP proxy
c = dial('proxy.example.com', 3128, 'target.example.com', 443)
c: {
nil -> print('proxy connection failed')
-> c.send('hello')
}

// establish a TLS connection through the proxy
tc = proxy-tls-dial('proxy.example.com', 3128, 'target.example.com', 443)
tc: {
nil -> print('proxy TLS connection failed')
-> w = tc.send('GET / HTTP/1.1\r\nHost: target.example.com\r\n\r\n')
}
`
  'proxy.config': `
config: proxy configuration
host: proxy server host
port: proxy server port number
username: authentication username (empty=no authentication)
password: authentication password
`
  'proxy.config.connect': `
Establish a proxied connection using config
Proxy address and credentials come from the receiver (.host / .port / .username / .password).
target-host: target hostname to tunnel to
target-port: target service port
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = failure
`
  'proxy.config.tls-connect': `
Establish a proxied TLS connection using config
First opens a CONNECT tunnel with the receiver's proxy settings, then completes the TLS handshake over the tunnel.
target-host: TLS server hostname (used for SNI / certificate verification)
target-port: TLS service port
returns tc: ok = TLS connection with handshake done, nil = failure
`
  'proxy.connect': `
Establish a tunnel through an HTTP CONNECT proxy
Connects to the proxy and sends CONNECT target-host:target-port; the handshake completes on a 200 response;
when username is non-empty a Basic authentication header is attached.
proxy-host: proxy server hostname or IP
proxy-port: proxy server port (HTTP proxies commonly 3128 / 8080)
target-host: target hostname to tunnel to
target-port: target service port (e.g. 443 for HTTPS)
username: Basic auth username; empty string means no auth header
password: Basic auth password (ignored when username is empty)
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = connect or handshake failure
`
  'proxy.dial': `
Establish a TCP connection through a proxy (convenience function, no auth)
Equivalent to connect(..., '', '').
proxy-host: proxy server hostname or IP
proxy-port: proxy server port
target-host: target hostname to tunnel to
target-port: target service port
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = failure
`
  'proxy.dial-auth': `
Establish a TCP connection through a proxy (with auth)
Same as connect(...) with credentials spelled out as parameters.
proxy-host: proxy server hostname or IP
proxy-port: proxy server port
target-host: target hostname to tunnel to
target-port: target service port
username: Basic auth username
password: Basic auth password
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = failure
`
  'proxy.proxy-buf-size': 'proxy buffer size'
  'proxy.proxy-tls-dial': `
Establish a TLS connection through a proxy
Performs an HTTP CONNECT (without auth) to get a tunnel, then starts the TLS handshake over it.
proxy-host: proxy server hostname or IP
proxy-port: proxy server port
target-host: TLS server hostname (also used for SNI / certificate verification in the handshake)
target-port: TLS service port (usually 443)
returns tc: ok = TLS connection handshaked over the tunnel, nil = tunnel or handshake failed at any stage
`
  'proxy.socks5-atyp-ipv4': 'SOCKS5 address type'
  'proxy.socks5-cmd-connect': 'SOCKS5 command'
  'proxy.socks5-connect': `
Establish a connection through a SOCKS5 proxy
First negotiates methods (0x02 username/password auth when username is non-empty, otherwise 0x00 no auth),
then hands the target address to the proxy with the CONNECT command.
proxy-host: SOCKS5 proxy hostname or IP
proxy-port: SOCKS5 proxy port (usually 1080)
target-host: target host to tunnel to; non-IP literals are passed to the proxy as a domain name string for it to resolve
target-port: target service port
username: username; empty string means no authentication
password: password (ignored when username is empty)
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = negotiation or connect failure
`
  'proxy.socks5-dial': `
Establish a connection through a SOCKS5 proxy (convenience function, no auth)
Equivalent to socks5-connect(..., '', ''), using 0x00 no-auth negotiation.
proxy-host: SOCKS5 proxy hostname or IP
proxy-port: SOCKS5 proxy port (usually 1080)
target-host: target host to tunnel to; non-IP literals are passed to the proxy as a domain name string for it to resolve
target-port: target service port
returns fd: established tunnel socket descriptor, -1 = failure
`
  'proxy.socks5-method-no-auth': 'SOCKS5 authentication methods'
  'proxy.socks5-version': 'SOCKS5 version'
  'queue': `
queue — generic queue (ring buffer based on a fixed-length array)

first-in-first-out (FIFO) data structure, using a [n]t fixed-length array as underlying storage.
implemented with a ring buffer; head points to the queue front, tail points to the next write position.
capacity is fixed at n-1 (one slot sacrificed to distinguish "full" from "empty").

generic design: value operations (push/pop/peek) are defined on [n]t,
the generic parameters n (array size) and t (element type) are inferred automatically by the compiler.
queue state (cap/head/tail) is stored separately in the queue struct.

differences from deque:
- queue supports only FIFO (in at the back, out at the front), with a simpler API
- queue uses generic [n]t, supporting any element type (i64/u8/byte, etc.)
- deque supports only []i64

usage:
buf [128]i64
q = buf.queue-init()
ok = buf.queue-push(q, 42)
ok = buf.queue-push(q, 7)
val = buf.queue-pop(q)      // val = ok
val = buf.queue-pop(q)      // val = ok
val = buf.queue-pop(q)      // val = nil (queue empty)
sz = q.size()
yes = q.empty()

// option unpacking
val = buf.queue-pop(q)
val: {
nil -> print('empty')
-> print(it)          // it = popped value
}
`
  'queue.queue': `
queue: queue state (without the buffer, which is provided by the [n]t receiver)
cap: ring buffer capacity (= array length n)
head: index of the queue front (logical index, mapped to a physical position after modulo)
tail: index of the queue rear (next write position)
`
  'queue.queue.clear': 'Clears the queue'
  'queue.queue.empty': `
Reports whether the queue is empty
Returns yes: true = empty, false = not empty
yes: true=tail <= head (no elements pending dequeue)
`
  'queue.queue.full': `
Reports whether the queue is full
The ring buffer always keeps one empty slot to distinguish "empty" from "full", so the element limit is cap-1.
yes: true=tail - head >= cap - 1 (full; another push would overwrite or fail), false=room remains
`
  'queue.queue.size': `
Returns the number of elements in the queue
Returns sz: the element count
sz: tail - head, i.e. the number of elements currently queued (empty slots excluded)
`
  'quic': `
quic — QUIC transport protocol (RFC 9000) core implementation

Implements the QUIC v1 packet format, variable-length integer encoding, frame parsing,
and Initial packet protection (key derivation + header protection + AEAD encryption/decryption).
Provides transport-layer support for HTTP/3 (RFC 9114).

The underlying layer uses UDP (net-udp-open/sendto/recvfrom)
Cryptographic primitives use AES-128-GCM, HKDF, SHA-256 from std/hash

usage:
# std/net/quic
# std/net/http3
resp = http3-get('https://example.com/path')
`
  'quic.apply-header-protection': `
Apply/remove header protection on a packet
packet: packet data to process (modified in place)
pn-offset: byte offset of the packet number within the packet
hp: 16-byte header protection key
is-long-header: whether this is a Long Header packet
`
  'quic.conn': 'conn: QUIC connection'
  'quic.conn.build-initial-packet': `
quic-build-initial-packet: build a QUIC Initial packet
crypto-data: CRYPTO frame payload to carry (TLS ClientHello)
returns out: on success the full encrypted Initial packet, nil on failure
`
  'quic.conn.close': 'Close the connection'
  'quic.conn.init': 'Initialize the connection'
  'quic.conn.recv-initial': `
Receive and decrypt the server Initial packet
returns frames: parsed frames
returns count: number of frames (-1 = error)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'quic.conn.recv-raw': `
conn.recv-packet: receive any QUIC packet
returns buf: raw packet bytes received
returns n: bytes received (-1 = error, 0 = timeout)
`
  'quic.decode-packet-number': `
Decode the packet number from a packet
packet: packet data with header protection removed
pn-offset: byte offset of the packet number within the packet
largest-pn: largest packet number successfully decoded so far (used to infer the high bits)
returns pn: full packet number
returns pn-len: number of bytes the packet number field occupies
`
  'quic.derive-initial-keys': `
Derive Initial keys from the Destination CID
dst-cid: peer (destination) Connection ID bytes
is-client: true derives client keys, false server-side keys
returns key: AEAD key
returns iv: AEAD IV
returns hp: header protection key
`
  'quic.dial': `
Establish a QUIC connection
host: server hostname or IP address
port: server UDP port
returns conn: ok = established connection, nil = failure
`
  'quic.frame': `
frame: QUIC frame
frame-type: frame type
offset: offset of CRYPTO/STREAM frames
length: frame data length
stream-id: stream ID of STREAM frames
data: frame data
`
  'quic.gen-cid': `
Generate a random Connection ID
len: number of CID bytes to generate (usually 8)
returns cid: randomly generated Connection ID bytes
`
  'quic.parse-frames': `
Parse all frames from buf starting at pos
buf: data buffer containing QUIC frames
pos: byte offset to start parsing
end: byte offset where parsing stops (exclusive)
returns frames: array of parsed frames
returns count: number of frames (-1 = error)
`
  'quic.quic-fixed-bit': `
Fixed Bit (RFC 9000 §17.2): long and short headers **share** the same bit 0x40, which must always be 1;
otherwise the peer must drop the packet.
`
  'quic.quic-frame-padding': 'Frame type'
  'quic.quic-initial-salt': 'QUIC Initial Salt (RFC 9001 §5.2)'
  'quic.quic-key-size': 'Key length'
  'quic.quic-long-header-fixed-bit': `
Long Header fixed bit (⚠️ the name is long-standing, but the value 0x80 is actually the RFC 9000 §17.2
**Header Form** bit, not the Fixed Bit; see QUIC-FIXED-BIT for the Fixed Bit)
`
  'quic.quic-max-cid-len': 'maximum CID length'
  'quic.quic-max-frames': 'Maximum number of frames'
  'quic.quic-max-packet-size': 'Maximum packet size'
  'quic.quic-packet-initial': 'packet type (high bits of the first byte of the Long Header)'
  'quic.quic-short-header-fixed-bit': 'Short Header fixed bit'
  'quic.quic-state-new': 'connection state'
  'quic.quic-version-1': 'QUIC version'
  'quic.remove-header-protection': `
Remove header protection (same as apply; XOR is symmetric)
packet: packet data to process (modified in place)
pn-offset: byte offset of the packet number within the packet
hp: 16-byte header protection key
is-long-header: whether this is a Long Header packet
`
  'quic.varint-decode': `
Decode a QUIC variable-length integer from buf at pos
buf: data buffer holding the varint
pos: byte offset to start decoding
returns val: the decoded integer value
returns next-pos: next position (-1 = error)
`
  'quic.varint-encode': `
Encode an i64 as a QUIC variable-length integer
val: non-negative i64 value to encode as a varint
returns buf: encoded result (1/2/4/8 bytes)
returns n: number of encoded bytes actually written into buf
`
  'rand': `
rand — simple random number generator (xorshift32)

Uses the xorshift32 algorithm, a simple and fast pseudo-random number generator.

usage:
seed = 123456789
r = rand(seed)    // r = random number 0..4294967295
`
  'rand.rand': `
Produce the next pseudorandom number (xorshift32)
state: input state (any nonzero seed; internally trimmed to 32 bits and remasked at every step)
new-state: updated state (32 bits), to seed the next call
r: value emitted this call (0..4294967295)
`
  'rand.rand-str': `
Produce a random alphanumeric string of the given length
state: input/output state variable
n: number of characters to produce
s: output string (length n)
chars: allowed character set (A-Z a-z 0-9)
`
  'rc4': `
rc4 — RC4 stream cipher

RC4 is a simple stream cipher using a 256-byte state array.
Encryption and decryption use the same operation (XOR).

note: RC4 is considered insecure and is provided only for legacy system compatibility.

usage:
# std/crypto/rc4
key []byte = 'secret key'
cipher = rc4(plain, key)   // encrypt
plain = rc4(cipher, key)   // decrypt (same operation)
`
  'rc4.rc4': `
RC4 encrypt/decrypt
The same function both encrypts and decrypts: out = in XOR keystream; running it on out again recovers in.
in: input data (plaintext or ciphertext, length unchanged)
key: key (1–256 bytes); when empty internal indexing goes out of bounds, so callers must guarantee non-empty
returns out: keystream-XOR result, same length as in
`
  'regexp': `
regexp — regular expression standard library (pure Nolang implementation)

The regular expression engine is implemented entirely in Nolang, with no dependency on the C standard regex.h.
It uses an instruction-based VM + backtracking matching architecture.

Supported syntax:
.          any character (except newline)
*          zero or more (greedy)
+          one or more (greedy)
?          zero or one (greedy)
[abc]      character class
[^abc]     negated character class
[a-z]      character range
^          start-of-line anchor
$          end-of-line anchor
\d \D      digit / non-digit
\w \W      word character / non-word character
\s \S      whitespace / non-whitespace
\n \t \r   newline / tab / carriage return
\. \* \+   escaped special characters

usage example:
re = regexp{
pattern: '^hello'
}
matched = re.matches('hello world')
// matched = true

re = regexp{
pattern: '\\d+'
}
match = re.find('price: 123')
// match = '123'

// convenience functions
matched = regexp-match('hello.*world', 'hello nolang world')
result = regexp-find('[0-9]+', 'abc123def456')
`
  'regexp._re-quant-none': `
internal quantifier codes (avoid using raw character values like 42/43/63 as comparison constants,
because LLVM has an optimization bug with == 42. Use safe values 1/2/3 instead.)
`
  'regexp.alloc-class': `
allocates a new character class and returns its index
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.clear-class-bit': `
clears a bit
cls-idx: the class index
c: the character value (0-255)
`
  'regexp.compile': 'compiles the pattern string into an instruction sequence'
  'regexp.emit': `
emits one instruction
op: the instruction type
arg1: argument 1
arg2: argument 2
`
  'regexp.emit-atom': `
emits an atom instruction
atom-type: the atom type
atom-char: the atom character value
atom-cls: the atom class index
`
  'regexp.emit-quantized': `
emits instructions according to the quantifier
Reads the quantifier's internal code from the .tmp-quant field (1=*, 2=+, 3=?)
Uses 1/2/3 as the comparison values, avoiding LLVM's == 42 optimisation bug
atom-type: the atom type
atom-char: the atom character value
atom-cls: the atom class index
atom-i: the position after the atom
`
  'regexp.find': `
finds the first substring of text matching .pattern
text: the text to match
returns result: the matched substring, or an empty string when absent
`
  'regexp.in-class': `
tests whether a character is in the class
cls-idx: the class index
c: the character value (0-255)
returns yes: true = in the class
`
  'regexp.init-class-all': `
initialises a class bitmap to all ones
cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.init-class-zero': `
initialises a class bitmap to all zeros
cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-digits': `
builds the digit class \d [0-9]
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-nondigits': `
builds the non-digit class \D
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-nonspace': `
builds the non-whitespace class \S
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-nonword': `
builds the non-word class \W
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-space': `
builds the whitespace class \s
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.make-class-word': `
builds the word class \w [a-zA-Z0-9_]
returns cls-idx: the class index
`
  'regexp.match-at': `
recursive backtracking match
pc: the instruction counter
sp: the text position
text: the text to match
returns ok: true = matched
side effect: on success it sets .match-end = sp
`
  'regexp.matches': `
tests whether text matches .pattern anywhere
text: the text to match
returns matched: true = matched
`
  'regexp.parse-atom': `
parses a single regex atom (without emitting instructions)
Stores the result in .tmp-atom-type, .tmp-atom-char and .tmp-atom-cls
pattern: the pattern string
i: the current position
returns new-i: the new position after parsing
`
  'regexp.parse-class': `
parses a [...] character class
pattern: the pattern string
i: the current position (pointing at '[')
returns new-i: the new position (past ']')
side effect: creates a new character class and stores its index in .tmp-atom-cls
`
  'regexp.parse-escape': `
parses a \x escape sequence
pattern: the pattern string
i: the current position (pointing at '\')
returns new-i: the new position after parsing
side effect: sets .tmp-atom-type, .tmp-atom-char and .tmp-atom-cls
`
  'regexp.re-cls-max': 'character class capacity (total cls bytes / RE-CLS-SIZE = number of classes that fit)'
  'regexp.re-cls-size': 'bitmap size of each character class (32 bytes = 256 bits)'
  'regexp.re-op-char': 'instruction type constants'
  'regexp.re-op-max': 'Instruction array capacity (op / arg1 / arg2, three arrays of equal length)'
  'regexp.regexp': `
regexp: regular expression
pattern: original pattern string
compiled: whether compiled
n: number of instructions after compilation
op: instruction type array
arg1: instruction argument 1 (char value / jump target / class index)
arg2: instruction argument 2 (second branch of SPLIT)
cls: character class bitmap (RE-CLS-SIZE bytes each)
ncls: number of character classes
match-end: match end position (used by find)
overflowed: whether RE-OP-MAX / RE-CLS-MAX limits were hit at compile time (true ⇒ instructions or classes were dropped, match results unreliable)
tmp-atom-type: parse temp — atom type
tmp-atom-char: parse temp — atom char value
tmp-atom-cls: parse temp — atom class index
`
  'regexp.regexp-compile': `
compiles a regular expression pattern and returns a compiled regexp instance.
Used by the regex literal /pattern/: the literal is desugared into a call to this function during codegen.
pattern: the regular expression
returns re: the compiled regular expression instance
`
  'regexp.regexp-find': `
finds the first substring of text matching pattern
pattern: the regular expression
text: the text to match
returns result: the matched substring, or an empty string when absent
`
  'regexp.regexp-match': `
tests whether text matches pattern anywhere
pattern: the regular expression
text: the text to match
returns matched: true = matched
`
  'regexp.set-class-bit': `
sets a bit
cls-idx: the class index
c: the character value (0-255)
`
  'regexp.set-class-escape': `
handles an escape sequence inside a character class
cls-idx: the class index
c: the escaped character (e.g. 'd', 'w', 's')
`
  'rsa': `
rsa — RSA encryption/decryption (multi-precision integer modular exponentiation)

Performs RSA modular exponentiation using multi-precision integers (base 2^32, little-endian):
result = base^exp mod modulus

No key generation; the caller must supply n, e, d.
Supports 1024~4096-bit keys (32~128 32-bit limbs).

usage:
// high-level interface (recommended): byte strings are interpreted as big-endian unsigned integers; output is the shortest big-endian encoding (0 is the single byte [0])
cipher = rsa-encrypt-bytes(msg, pub-exp, mod)
plain  = rsa-decrypt-bytes(cipher, priv-exp, mod)
// low-level numeric interface (manual limb count management; callers may pass a trimmed count to scan fewer bits):
// base, exp, mod are []i64 slices (base 2^32 little-endian)
result, result-n = rsa-modpow(base, base-n, exp, exp-n, mod, mod-n)
// other symbols starting with _ (limb wrapping/bignum utilities/byte conversion) are module-internal; cross-module use is forbidden (the private_std guard reports ERROR)

note:
- all internal functions are named _rsa-bn-* to avoid conflicts with the bn-* functions in ecdsa.no
(both belong to the crypto package; without a module prefix they resolve to the wrong functions)
- Nolang's && does not short-circuit, so the trim loop must not use && as a condition
Use a keep-trimming flag variable instead.
- ⚠️ historically `(keep-trimming) { ... }` was **dead code**: the parser's isCondLoopPrefix
(src/parser/stmt.go) misidentified "a single IDENT inside parens" as the parameter list of an anonymous function literal,
so `(flag) { ... }` was not a loop but a no-op statement — the loop never ran even once,
and the length was never trimmed. The workaround at the time was `(flag == true) { ... }`.
Fixed in the parser on 2026-09-24 (Nolang function-literal parameters always require a type; `(flag)`
is not a valid parameter list at all), so now **just write `(keep-trimming) { ... }` directly**,
all 4 places below have been reverted to this form. This bug once let an untrimmed rn cause rsa-modpow to read out of bounds,
and produced a SIGSEGV in tests/https-server.no.
`
  'rsa._bytes-to-limbs': `
bytes → limbs (module-private): converts a big-endian unsigned byte string to a base 2^32 little-endian limb array
v: big-endian unsigned integer (leading zero bytes are harmless)
out: limb array, length ceil(len/4); empty input returns [0]
`
  'rsa._limbs-to-bytes': `
limbs → bytes (private to the module): base 2^32 little-endian limb array to the shortest big-endian unsigned byte string
v: limb array (leading zero limbs allowed)
out: shortest big-endian encoding; value 0 returns a single byte [0]
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-add': `
c = a + b
a: addend (read-only, not modified)
an: number of significant limbs of a
b: addend (read-only, not modified)
bn: number of significant limbs of b
c: result of a+b (a final carry beyond the longer operand appends one limb)
cn: actual number of limbs of c (including carry, not trimmed)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-cmp': `
Compare two big integers a and b
returns cmp: 1 = a > b, 0 = a == b, -1 = a < b
a: first big integer (array of 32-bit limbs, least significant first)
an: number of significant limbs of a participating in the comparison
b: second big integer
bn: number of significant limbs of b participating in the comparison
cmp: 1=a>b, 0=a==b, -1=a<b (limb counts compared first, then limbs from most significant down)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-div-mod': `
q = a / b, r = a % b
a: dividend (limb array with the low limb first)
an: number of significant limbs in the dividend
b: divisor (same limb layout)
bn: number of significant limbs in the divisor (bn=1 and b[0]=0 is treated as division by zero and outputs all zeros)
q: quotient (output), qn: quotient length
r: remainder (output), rn: remainder length
qn: limbs of the quotient after trimming (when b=0 both q and r are all zero and qn=rn=0)
rn: limbs of the remainder after trimming (always smaller than b)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-mul': `
c = a × b
a: multiplier
an: number of significant limbs of a
b: multiplicand
bn: number of significant limbs of b
c: result of a×b (at most an+bn limbs, leading zeros trimmed)
cn: limbs of c after trimming (at least 1)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-shl1': `
a = a << 1 (left shift 1 bit in place)
a: big integer to shift (modified in place; when the top bit overflows it is written to a[an], so the caller must reserve space)
an: number of significant limbs before the shift
new-an: limbs after the shift (an+1 when the original top limb ≥ 2^31)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-shr1': `
a = a >> 1 (right shift 1 bit in place)
a: big integer to shift (modified in place)
an: number of significant limbs before the shift
new-an: limbs after shifting and trimming leading zeros (at least 1)
`
  'rsa._rsa-bn-sub': `
c = a - b (requires a ≥ b)
a: minuend (must be ≥ b, otherwise the result wraps around borrowing modulo 2^32)
an: number of significant limbs of a (determines the subtraction loop length and output allocation)
b: subtrahend
bn: number of significant limbs of b (terms with i ≥ bn count as 0)
c: result of a-b (leading zeros trimmed)
cn: limbs of c after trimming (at least 1)
`
  'rsa._rsa-decrypt': `
RSA decrypt (m = c^d mod n)
ct: ciphertext big integer c (must be less than mod)
ctn: limbs of ct
priv-exp: private exponent d
privn: limbs of priv-exp
mod: modulus n
modn: limbs of mod
out: plaintext m = c^d mod n (no PKCS#1 unpadding)
outn: limbs of out
`
  'rsa._rsa-encrypt': `
RSA encrypt (c = m^e mod n)
msg: plaintext big integer m (must be less than mod; no PKCS#1 padding applied)
msgn: limbs of msg
pub-exp: public exponent e
pubn: limbs of pub-exp
mod: modulus n
modn: limbs of mod
out: ciphertext c = m^e mod n
outn: limbs of out
`
  'rsa._rsa-modpow-step': `
Single square-and-multiply step (a separate function so each step frees scratch buffers)
result = result^2 mod modulus; when exp-bit == 1, additionally result = result * base mod modulus
result: current accumulator (the base before squaring)
rn: limbs of result
base: power base, multiplied in only when exp-bit=1
bn: limbs of base
exp-bit: current exponent bit (1 = multiply base this step, 0 = square only)
modulus: divisor for reduction
mn: limbs of modulus
new-result: (result² × base^exp-bit) mod modulus
new-rn: limbs of new-result
`
  'rsa._rsa-modpow-trimmed': `
The public interface no longer passes a limb count manually: input is measured by the vec's own .len() (leading zero limbs only cost extra scanning and do not affect correctness);
output is a precisely trimmed vec whose out.len() is the valid limb count (the low-level rsa-modpow returns a buffer length including extra zero limbs).
Trim the wide buffer output of rsa-modpow into a vec of exact length
base: base of the power
bn: limb count of base
exp: exponent
en: limb count of exp
modulus: modulus
mn: limb count of modulus
out: base^exp mod modulus (length exactly the valid limb count; returns a single zero limb when rn==0)
`
  'rsa.rsa-decrypt-bytes': `
RSA decryption (byte interface): m = c^d mod n
ct: ciphertext big integer c, big-endian unsigned byte string (must be less than mod)
priv-exp: private exponent d, big-endian unsigned byte string
mod: modulus n, big-endian unsigned byte string
out: plaintext m = c^d mod n, shortest big-endian unsigned encoding (no PKCS#1 unpadding)
`
  'rsa.rsa-encrypt-bytes': `
RSA encryption (byte interface): c = m^e mod n
msg: plaintext big integer m, big-endian unsigned byte string (no PKCS#1 padding, must be less than mod)
pub-exp: public exponent e, big-endian unsigned byte string
mod: modulus n, big-endian unsigned byte string
out: ciphertext c = m^e mod n, minimal big-endian unsigned encoding (0 is the single byte [0])
`
  'rsa.rsa-modpow': `
result = base^exp mod modulus
Uses left-to-right square-and-multiply
Note: the loop body is extracted into rsa-modpow-step so each step frees its scratch buffers
(Nolang heap variables are freed only at function return; allocating inside the loop body would accumulate until OOM)
base: power base (must be less than modulus)
bn: limbs of base
exp: exponent (array of limbs, scanned from most significant bit down)
en: limbs of exp (used to compute the actual bit count)
modulus: modulus
mn: limbs of modulus (result buffers are allocated to this length)
result: base^exp mod modulus (square-and-multiply per bit starting from 1)
rn: limbs of result
`
  'scrypt': `
scrypt — Scrypt memory-hard key derivation function (RFC 7914)

Scrypt is designed as a memory-hard KDF, resisting ASIC/GPU cracking.
uses PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 + Salsa20/8 core function.

usage:
# std/crypto/scrypt
password []byte = 'password'
salt []byte = 'salt'
N = 16384    // CPU/Memory parameter (must be a power of 2)
r = 8        // block size parameter
p = 1        // parallelism parameter
key-len = 32
derived = scrypt(password, salt, N, r, p, key-len)
`
  'scrypt.rotl32': `
═══════════════════════════════════════════════
32-bit rotate left (the rotation used by the Salsa20 quarter-round)
x: 32-bit value to rotate
n: number of bits to rotate left (0..31; 32 overflows the shift amount)
r: rotation result, already & 0xFFFFFFFF
`
  'scrypt.salsa-quarter-round': `
Salsa20/8 core: 8 rounds of mixing over a 64-byte block
First reads 16 little-endian 32-bit words, runs 8 rounds of add-rotate-xor, then adds back to the original and writes out.
block: 64-byte input/output; modified in place, missing part treated as 0 when fewer than 64 bytes
[salsa20-8-core](#scrypt.salsa20-8-core)
Salsa20 quarter round (updates the 16-word working vector in place)
Per RFC 7914 §3.1 QR(a,b,c,d): b/c/d/a mixed by left rotation of 7/9/13/18 bits respectively
x: 16-word working vector (updated in place)
a, b, c, d: indices of this round's four words in the vector
mask: 32-bit mask
Each step reads the word just written by the previous step, so each is read out by name, with no shared temporary.
`
  'scrypt.salsa20-8-core': `
Salsa20/8 core: 8-round mixing of a 64-byte block
Reads 16 little-endian 32-bit words, runs 8 rounds of add-rotate-xor, then adds the original values back and writes out.
block: 64-byte input/output; modified in place, missing bytes below 64 are treated as 0
`
  'scrypt.scrypt': `
Scrypt key derivation (RFC 7914 §7)
First expands the password to p*128*r bytes with PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (1 iteration),
runs ROMix on each section, then does a final PBKDF2 round to emit key-len bytes.
password: password (UTF-8 byte sequence)
salt: salt, 16 bytes or more and unique per account recommended
n: CPU/memory cost, must be a power of 2 greater than 1; larger is both safer and slower
r: block size (e.g. 8); together with n determines memory usage of 128*n*r bytes
p: parallelism (e.g. 1), affects CPU time
key-len: desired key length in bytes
returns out: derived key, exactly key-len bytes long
`
  'scrypt.scrypt-blockmix': `
Block mixing function (RFC 7914 §4)
Uses the trailing 64 bytes as initial X, iterates Salsa20/8 over the 2r 64-byte sub-blocks,
even blocks go to the first half and odd blocks to the second, yielding a new 128*r-byte output.
b: input block (128*r bytes)
r: block size parameter (2r sub-blocks of 64 bytes each)
returns out: mixed block (length 128*r)
`
  'scrypt.scrypt-romix': `
Memory-hard mixing function (RFC 7914 §5, also known as ROMix)
Fills the V table with n blocks, then performs n random-index reads plus BlockMix, producing a new 128*r-byte block.
b: input block (128*r bytes)
n: CPU/memory cost parameter, must be a power of 2 greater than 1 (e.g. 16384)
r: block size parameter, determines the number of 64-byte chunks per sub-block
returns out: mixed block (length 128*r)
`
  'server': `
server — HTTP/1.1 server

Provides a simple HTTP/1.1 server with routing dispatch, request parsing and response building.
Supports GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH methods and Keep-Alive connection reuse.
Uses the listener/conn structs from net.no underneath.

usage:
srv = server{}
ok = srv.listen('0.0.0.0', 8080)
ok -> {
! {
req = srv.accept()           // returns ?server-conn
req: {
nil -> break
->
req.parse()
req.method == 'GET' -> {
req.write-text(200, 'Hello, World!')
`
  'server.http-srv-buf-size': 'Server receive buffer size'
  'server.http-srv-keepalive-timeout': 'Keep-Alive timeout (milliseconds)'
  'server.http-srv-max-body': 'maximum request body size'
  'server.http-srv-max-headers': 'maximum number of request headers'
  'server.http-srv-max-keepalive': 'Maximum Keep-Alive requests'
  'server.http-srv-recv-size': 'Single receive size'
  'server.https-listen-on': `
Create an HTTPS server and start listening
host: IP address to bind
port: port to listen on
returns s: ok = server, nil = failure
`
  'server.https-server': `
https-server: HTTPS server
fd: listening socket fd (-1=not opened)
host: bound IP address
port: listening port
`
