`
  'set': `
set — generic set data structure

The set struct carries its own element buffer (`data []t`) and state (cap/size); the element type `t` is
inferred from the concrete `[N]E` / `[]E` buffer passed to the factory call `set.init(data)`; the compiler
monomorphizes via monomorphizeSliceStructs (src/build/slice_structs.go) into the set-E instantiated type
and all its methods. Thus set supports any element type with `==` semantics (i64/u8/byte/str, etc.).

usage:
use std/set
buf [128]i64 = [0,0,0 /* ... 128 elements */]
s = set.init(buf)                // s : set-i64, capacity = buffer length
n = s.add(1)                     // add 1, returns the new size
s.add(2)
yes = s.contains(1)              // yes = true
n = s.remove(1)                  // remove 1, returns the new size
sz = s.size()

// generic string set: just provide another [n]str buffer
sbuf [16]str = ['', '', /* ... */]
ss = set.init(sbuf)
ss.add('apple')

note: the capacity limit is the buffer array length; instances are created by set.init(buffer).
`
  'set.add': `
adds an element to the set (if absent)
val: the element to add
returns new-size: the new size
`
  'set.contains': `
reports whether an element is in the set
val: the element to look for
returns yes: true = present, false = absent
`
  'set.difference': `
computes the difference (in this set but not in other)
the result is written into the caller-supplied out buffer and the number of elements is returned
other: the other set's element buffer
osize: the other set's element count
out: the buffer for the difference
returns cnt: the number of elements in the difference
`
  'set.init': `
factory — builds an empty set on a caller-supplied element buffer
data: the element buffer (its length is the capacity ceiling)
returns s: the initialised set instance
`
  'set.intersection': `
computes the intersection (elements in both this set and other)
the result is written into the caller-supplied out buffer and the number of elements is returned
other: the other set's element buffer
osize: the other set's element count
out: the buffer for the intersection
returns cnt: the number of elements in the intersection
`
  'set.is-empty': `
reports whether the set is empty
yes: true when the collection holds no elements
`
  'set.remove': `
removes an element from the set
fills the hole with the last element (so order is not preserved)
val: the element to remove
returns new-size: the new size
`
  'set.set': `
set: set state (carries its own element buffer)
data: element buffer slice (capacity = its length)
cap:  capacity (= buffer array length)
size: current element count
`
  'set.size': `
returns the set size
sz: number of elements currently held by the set
`
  'set.to-vec': `
converts the set to a slice (unordered)
returns out: the slice of elements (length = size)
`
  'set.union': `
computes the union (adds the elements of other's buffer that this set lacks)
other: the other set's element buffer
osize: the other set's element count
returns new-size: the size after the union
`
  'sha1': `
sha1 — SHA-1 secure hash algorithm (160-bit)

Provides two public API layers and one low-level API:
sha1(data []byte) (hash [20]byte) — full hash, with padding and multi-block handling
sha1-hex(data []byte) (hex str) — full hash, returns a 40-character hex string
sha1-block(s []u32, h0 u32, h1 u32, h2 u32, h3 u32, h4 u32) — processes a single 512-bit block (low-level)

usage:
// public API: compute SHA-1 over a byte array
hash = sha1(data)       // hash = 20 bytes [20]byte
hex = sha1-hex(data)    // hex = 40-character hex string

// low-level: manage blocks manually
h0 = 1732584193
h1 = 4023233417
h2 = 2562383102
h3 = 271733878
h4 = 3285377520
sha1-block(block, h0, h1, h2, h3, h4)
`
  'sha1.sha1': `
Compute the SHA-1 hash (padding and multi-block handling included)
data: input byte array (any length)
hash: 160-bit digest stored big-endian as defined by FIPS 180-4
Note: SHA-1 no longer offers collision resistance; use it only for legacy protocol compatibility / checksums
`
  'sha1.sha1-block': `
Process one 512-bit block (80-round compression, FIPS 180-4)
s: 16 32-bit big-endian message words (already unpacked from bytes by the caller)
h0 u32, h1, h2, h3, h4: the 160-bit hash state entering this block
r0 u32, r1, r2, r3, r4: the new state after compression (working variables added back to the input state modulo 2^32)
`
  'sha1.sha1-hex': `
Compute the SHA-1 hash and return a hex string
data: input byte array (any length)
hex: 40-character lowercase hexadecimal digest
`
  'sha224': `
sha224 — SHA-224 secure hash algorithm (224-bit)

SHA-224 uses the same compression function as SHA-256, but with different initial values and truncates the output to 28 bytes.
Under the hood it calls sha256-block to process 512-bit blocks.

usage:
# std/crypto/sha224
hash = sha224(data)       // hash = 28 bytes [28]byte
hex = sha224-hex(data)    // hex = 56-character hex string
`
  'sha224.sha224': `
Compute the SHA-224 hash (padding and multi-block handling included; same compression function as SHA-256, different initial values)
data: input byte array (any length)
hash: 224-bit digest (the first 7 SHA-256 state words), stored big-endian
`
  'sha224.sha224-hex': `
Compute the SHA-224 hash and return a hex string
data: input byte array (any length)
hex: 56-character lowercase hexadecimal digest
`
  'sha256': `
sha256 — SHA-256 secure hash algorithm (256-bit)

Provides two public API layers and one low-level API:
sha256(data []byte) (hash [32]byte) — full hash, with padding and multi-block handling
sha256-hex(data []byte) (hex str) — full hash, returns a 64-character hex string
sha256-block(s []u32, h0 u32, h1 u32, h2 u32, h3 u32, h4 u32, h5 u32, h6 u32, h7 u32) — processes a single 512-bit block (low-level)

usage:
// public API: compute SHA-256 over a byte array
hash = sha256(data)       // hash = 32 bytes [32]byte
hex = sha256-hex(data)    // hex = 64-character hex string

// low-level: manage blocks manually
h0 = 1779033703
h1 = 3144134277
h2 = 1013904242
h3 = 2773480762
h4 = 1359893119
h5 = 2600822924
h6 = 528734635
h7 = 1541459225
sha256-block(block, h0, h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, h7)
`
  'sha256.sha256': `
Compute the SHA-256 hash (padding and multi-block handling included)
data: input byte array (any length)
hash: 256-bit digest stored big-endian as defined by FIPS 180-4
`
  'sha256.sha256-block': `
Process one 512-bit block (64-round compression, FIPS 180-4)
s: 16 32-bit big-endian message words (already unpacked from bytes by the caller)
h0 u32..h7 u32: the 256-bit hash state entering this block (initial values of a..h)
r0 u32..r7 u32: the new state after compression (working variables added back to the input state modulo 2^32)
`
  'sha256.sha256-hex': `
Compute the SHA-256 hash and return a hex string
data: input byte array (any length)
hex: 64-character lowercase hexadecimal digest
`
  'sha3': `
sha3 — SHA-3 (Keccak) secure hash algorithm

Implements the SHA-3 family defined by NIST FIPS 202, based on the Keccak sponge construction.
Provides four output lengths: SHA3-224, SHA3-256, SHA3-384, SHA3-512.

The internal state is 1600-bit (25 × 64-bit lanes); Keccak-f[1600] has 24 rounds.

usage:
# std/crypto/sha3
hash28 = sha3.sha3-224(data)     // [28]byte
hash32 = sha3.sha3-256(data)     // [32]byte
hash48 = sha3.sha3-384(data)     // [48]byte
hash64 = sha3.sha3-512(data)     // [64]byte
hex = sha3.sha3-256-hex(data)    // 64-character hex
`
  'sha3.keccak-f1600': `
Run the Keccak-f[1600] permutation (24 rounds)
state: state array of 25 64-bit lanes (in-place input/output)
`
  'sha3.keccak-rc': `
round constants (iota), 24 rounds
rc[t] is generated by an 8-bit LFSR (x^8 + x^6 + x^5 + x^4 + 1),
bits are distributed at 2^j - 1 (j = 0..6, i.e. bit 0/1/3/7/15/31/63).
`
  'sha3.keccak-rho': `
Rotation offset table (rho), rotation amounts for 25 lanes
arrangement: lane[x][y] -> index = x + 5*y (x=0..4, y=0..4)
`
  'sha3.keccak-sponge': `
Keccak sponge function
data: raw bytes to absorb into the sponge state, may be empty
rate: absorption rate in bytes (must be a multiple of 8)
capacity: capacity in bytes (rate + capacity = 200)
out-len: desired output length in bytes
suffix: padding suffix (SHA-3 = 0x06, SHAKE = 0x1F)
returns out: the squeezed hash/output bytes
`
  'sha3.rotl64': `
64-bit rotate left
x: 64-bit value to rotate left
n: rotation amount (0..63, taken mod 64 first to avoid the undefined behavior of shifting by 64)
r: result after rotating left
`
  'sha3.sha3-224': `
Compute the SHA3-224 hash
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hash: 28-byte hash value
`
  'sha3.sha3-224-hex': `
Compute SHA3-224 and return a hex string
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hex: 56-character hex string
`
  'sha3.sha3-256': `
Compute the SHA3-256 hash
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hash: 32-byte hash value
`
  'sha3.sha3-256-hex': `
Compute SHA3-256 and return a hex string
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hex: 64-character hex string
`
  'sha3.sha3-384': `
Compute the SHA3-384 hash
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hash: 48-byte hash value
`
  'sha3.sha3-384-hex': `
Compute SHA3-384 and return a hex string
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hex: 96-character hex string
`
  'sha3.sha3-512': `
Compute the SHA3-512 hash
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hash: 64-byte hash value
`
  'sha3.sha3-512-hex': `
Compute SHA3-512 and return a hex string
data: raw bytes to hash, may be empty
returns hex: 128-character hex string
`
  'sha3.sha3-mask64': '64-bit all-ones mask'
  'sha3.sha3-pad-high': 'pads the highest bit (used when 0x80 lands in the lane\'s highest byte, to avoid i64 shift overflow)'
  'sha384': `
sha384 — SHA-384 secure hash algorithm (384-bit)

SHA-384 uses the same compression function as SHA-512, but with different initial values and truncates output to 48 bytes.
Underneath it calls sha512-block to process 1024-bit blocks.

usage:
# std/crypto/sha384
hash = sha384(data)       // hash = 48-byte [48]byte
hex = sha384-hex(data)    // hex = 96-character hex string
`
  'sha384.sha384': `
Compute the SHA-384 hash (padding and multi-block handling included; same compression function as SHA-512, different initial values)
data: input byte array (any length)
hash: 384-bit digest (the first 6 SHA-512 state words), stored big-endian
`
  'sha384.sha384-hex': `
Compute the SHA-384 hash and return a hex string
data: input byte array (any length)
hex: 96-character lowercase hexadecimal digest
`
  'sha512': `
sha512 — SHA-512 secure hash algorithm (512-bit)

Provides two public API layers and one low-level API:
sha512(data []byte) (hash [64]byte) — full hash, with padding and multi-block handling
sha512-hex(data []byte) (hex str) — full hash, returns a 128-character hex string
sha512-block(s []u64, h0 u64, h1 u64, h2 u64, h3 u64, h4 u64, h5 u64, h6 u64, h7 u64) — processes a single 1024-bit block (low-level)

usage:
// public API: compute SHA-512 over a byte array
hash = sha512(data)       // hash = 64 bytes [64]byte
hex = sha512-hex(data)    // hex = 128-character hex string

// low-level: manage blocks manually
h0 = 7640891576956012808
h1 = 13503953896175478587
h2 = 4354685564936845355
h3 = 11912009170470909681
h4 = 5840696475078001361
h5 = 11170449401992604703
h6 = 2270897969802886507
h7 = 6620516959819538809
sha512-block(block, h0, h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, h7)
`
  'sha512.sha512': `
Compute the SHA-512 hash (padding and multi-block handling included)
data: input byte array (any length; message length limit 2^128-1 bits)
hash: 512-bit digest stored big-endian as defined by FIPS 180-4
`
  'sha512.sha512-block': `
Process one 1024-bit block (80-round compression, FIPS 180-4)
s: 16 64-bit big-endian message words (already unpacked from bytes by the caller)
h0 u64..h7 u64: the 512-bit hash state entering this block (initial values of a..h)
r0 u64..r7 u64: the new state after compression (working variables added back to the input state modulo 2^64)
`
  'sha512.sha512-hex': `
Compute the SHA-512 hash and return a hex string
data: input byte array (any length)
hex: 128-character lowercase hexadecimal digest
`
  'sort': `
sort — ord comparison interface and ascending/descending in-place sort

ord requires only one method: `.gt(b) bool` (true when the receiver is greater than b). This file also provides
gt implementations for built-in types (i8…u64, f32/f64, byte, char, str), and in-place insertion sort
on array/slice views whose elements are ord: `[n]ord.asc` / `.desc` and `[]ord.asc` / `.desc`.
`
  'sort.byte.gt': `
byte.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: the byte value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.char.gt': `
char.gt: ord interface implementation - determines whether the receiver is greater than b by code point
b: character to compare
res: true when the receiver's code point is greater than b
`
  'sort.f32.gt': `
f32.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: the f32 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.f64.gt': `
f64.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: f64 value to compare against
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.i16.gt': `
i16.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: the i16 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.i32.gt': `
i32.gt: ord interface implementation - determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: i32 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.i64.gt': `
i64.gt: ord interface implementation — determine whether the receiver is greater than b
b: i64 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.i8.gt': `
i8.gt: ord interface implementation—determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: i8 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.str.gt': `
str.gt: ord interface implementation — determine whether the receiver sorts after b in lexicographic order
b: string to compare
res: true when the receiver is lexicographically greater than b (compare returns a positive value)
`
  'sort.u16.gt': `
u16.gt: ord interface implementation —— determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: u16 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.u32.gt': `
u32.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: the u32 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.u64.gt': `
u64.gt: ord interface implementation—determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: u64 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sort.u8.gt': `
u8.gt: ord interface implementation — determines whether the receiver is greater than b
b: u8 value to compare
res: true when the receiver is greater than b
`
  'sql': `
sql — database access interface definitions

This file defines the standard interface for database access.
Each driver (such as SQLite, MySQL, PostgreSQL, etc.) implements this interface,
providing concrete struct types and methods.

interface:
db    — database connection (merges enter/leave, auto-closes at scope exit)
rows  — query result set (merges enter/leave, auto-closes at scope exit)
stmt  — prepared statement (merges enter/leave, auto-closes at scope exit)

Inheritance (interface merging) is written in the body, one parent interface name per line (no commas):
db {
enter
leave
exec(sql str) (r result)
}

Shared struct:
result — execution result (shared by all drivers)

Functions a driver should provide:
open = (dsn str) (d db) — provided by the driver
`
  'sql.db': `
interface: db — database connection
Merges the enter/leave interfaces; leave() is called automatically when the variable goes out of scope
`
  'sql.result': `
Historical note: this file once declared `#{buildin} db-*` (15) and `ffi-cstr-*` (3) builtin stubs.
Their lowering originally lived in the legacy backend (`callDatabase` in `src/build/llvm/call_stdlib.go`,
cb24869); that directory was removed wholesale in c7febdb and was never ported to MIR.
The result was a half-life trap of "`no vet` 0 error, `no run` reports unsupported builtin", removed on 2026-09-21
together with the Go-side registrations (`src/builtin/database.go`, `src/builtin/ffi.go`).
Database access always goes through drivers (see `example/sqlite-driver`, `example/mysql-driver`);
when a C string array is needed, call it directly with `#{c}` FFI inside the driver; do not make it a builtin again.
result: execution result (shared by all drivers)
`
  'sql.rows': `
interface: rows — query result set
Merges the enter/leave interfaces; leave() is called automatically when the variable leaves scope
`
  'sql.stmt': `
interface: stmt — prepared statement
Merges the enter/leave interfaces; leave() is called automatically when the variable leaves scope
`
  'sse': `
sse — Server-Sent Events client and server

supports receiving and sending SSE streams per the W3C EventSource specification.
uses HTTP/1.1 long connections underneath; supports plaintext HTTP and HTTPS (TLS, client).

usage (client):
client = sse-connect('http://localhost:3000/events')   // returns ?client
client: {
nil -> print('SSE connection failed')
->
! {
ev = client.next-event()       // returns ?event
ev: {
nil -> break
err -> print('error: ' - it)
break
-> print(ev.event - ': ' - ev.data)
}
}
client.close()
}

usage (server):
srv = listen-on('0.0.0.0', 3000)   // returns ?server
srv: {
nil -> return
->
! {
c = srv.accept()             // returns ?server-conn
c: {
nil -> break
->
c.send-event('message', 'hello', '', -1)
c.send-comment('heartbeat')
c.close()
}
}
}
`
  'sse.client': `
client: SSE client connection
fd: TCP socket file descriptor (-1=not connected)
tls-c: TLS connection (used when use-tls=true)
use-tls: whether to use TLS
connected: connection state
host: server hostname
port: port number
path: request path
last-event-id: last received event ID (for reconnection)
recv-buf: receive buffer
recv-buf-len: actual data length in the buffer
`
  'sse.client.close': 'Close the SSE connection'
  'sse.client.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
returns yes: true = connected
`
  'sse.client.next-event': `
Read the next SSE event
Blocks until a complete event arrives or the connection closes.
returns ev:
ok = event
nil   = connection closed (EOF)
err = error
`
  'sse.client.reconnect': `
Reconnect (resuming reception with last-event-id)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'sse.event': `
event: SSE event
event: event type (default 'message')
data: event data (multiple data lines joined with \n)
id: event ID
retry: reconnect wait in milliseconds (-1=unset)
`
  'sse.listen-on': `
Create an SSE server and start listening
host: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0')
port: port to listen on
returns s: ok = server, nil = failure
`
  'sse.server': `
server: SSE server listener
fd: listening socket file descriptor (-1=not opened)
host: bound IP address
port: listening port
`
  'sse.server-conn': `
server-conn: SSE server-side client connection
fd: client socket file descriptor (-1=not connected)
connected: connection state
`
  'sse.server-conn.close': 'Close the client connection'
  'sse.server-conn.handshake': `
Complete the SSE HTTP handshake
Reads the client HTTP request, verifies the method is GET, and sends the SSE response headers.
returns ok: true = success
`
  'sse.server-conn.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
returns yes: true = connected
`
  'sse.server-conn.send-comment': `
Send an SSE comment (used for heartbeat keep-alive)
comment: comment content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'sse.server-conn.send-event': `
Send an SSE event
event: event type (empty string = omit the event field, use the default 'message')
data: event data (may be multiline; each line sent with a data: prefix)
id: event id (empty string = omit the id field)
retry: reconnect wait in milliseconds (-1 = omit the retry field)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'sse.server.accept': `
Accept a new SSE client connection and complete the HTTP handshake
returns c: ok = handshaked client, nil = failure
`
  'sse.server.close': 'Close the SSE server'
  'sse.server.listen': `
Start listening
host: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0' or '127.0.0.1')
port: port to listen on
returns ok: true = success
`
  'sse.sse-buf-size': 'receive buffer size'
  'sse.sse-connect': `
Connect to an SSE endpoint
url: SSE endpoint URL (http://host:port/path or https://host:port/path)
returns client:
ok = connected client
nil   = connection failed
err = error message
`
  'sse.sse-default-port': 'default HTTP port'
  'sse.sse-default-tls-port': 'default HTTPS port'
  'sse.sse-recv-size': 'single receive size'
  'stack': `
stack — stack

last-in-first-out (LIFO) data structure, implemented with []i64.

usage:
use std/stack
buf [128]i64
s = stack { data: buf, n: 0 }
s.push(42)
s.push(7)
val = s.pop()    // val=ok (last in, first out)
val: {
nil -> print('empty')
-> print(it)
}
`
  'stack.stack': `
stack: stack state
data: underlying storage slice, capacity limit = len(data)
n: current element count; valid elements are in data[0..n)
`
  'stack.stack.clear': `
Clear the stack (set n to 0; data is not freed and old contents are not erased)
No parameters and no return value; size() is 0 after clearing
`
  'stack.stack.empty': `
Reports whether the stack is empty
yes: true means n == 0
`
  'stack.stack.peek': `
Peeks at the top element without removing it
val: ok=data[n-1], nil=the stack is empty
`
  'stack.stack.pop': `
Pops an element from the top of the stack
val: ok=the original top element (removed), nil=the stack is empty
`
  'stack.stack.push': `
Pushes an element onto the top of the stack
Note: this method does not check capacity; the caller must guarantee n < len(data).
val: the value to push; written to data[n] then n increments by 1
`
  'stack.stack.size': `
returns the current element count of the stack
sz: equals n (0 means empty, at most len(data))
`
  'static-hashmap': `
static-hashmap — fixed-capacity generic hash map

This file provides three generic hashmap templates, specialized by the compiler for any k/v combination:
- static-hashmap-str-tmpl: str keys (#{generic=[v]})
- static-hashmap-int-tmpl: int keys (#{generic=[k,v]})
- static-hashmap-bool-tmpl: bool keys (#{generic=[v]})

All implementations are fixed-capacity with linear probing.
str/int templates have 256 slots (recommended ≤ 192 entries, load factor ≤ 0.75).
bool template has 2 slots (true/false indexed directly, no probing needed).

usage (after specialization, e.g. v=i64):
m = static-hashmap-str-tmpl{}
m.init()
m.put('name', 42)
val = m.get('name')   // val = ok(42) or nil
`
  'static-hashmap.fnv-offset': 'FNV-1a constant'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.clear': 'Clears the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.contains': `
Reports whether a key exists
key: the key to check
found: whether the table contains that key
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.for-each': `
Iterates over every (key, value) pair
k: the key of this iteration
v: the value of this iteration
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.get': `
Looks up a key and returns ?v (nil = not found)
key: the key to look up
result: ok=the stored value, nil=key absent
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.hash': `
Direct indexing (true → 1, false → 0)
key: boolean key
idx: the fixed slot index (true→1, false→0)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.init': 'Initialises the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.is-empty': `
Whether the map is empty
empty: whether both slots are empty
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n: number of elements stored so far (0~2)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.put': `
Inserts or updates key→value; returns whether it was inserted
key: the boolean key to insert
val: the value to store
is-new: true=a new key was inserted, false=an existing value was overwritten
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-bool-tmpl.remove': `
Removes a key (marks a tombstone)
key: the key to delete
removed: whether something was actually deleted
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.clear': 'Clears the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.contains': `
Reports whether a key exists
key: the key to check
found: whether the table contains that key
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.for-each': `
Iterates over every (key, value) pair
k: the key of this iteration
v: the value of this iteration
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.get': `
Looks up a key and returns ?v (nil = not found)
key: the key to look up
result: ok=the stored value, nil=key absent
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.hash': `
Computes the slot index (bit mask)
key: integer key
idx: the key's index masked into 256 slots
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.init': 'Initialises the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.is-empty': `
Whether the map is empty
empty: whether it contains no elements at all
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n: current number of live key/value pairs (tombstones excluded)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.put': `
Inserts or updates key→value; returns whether it was inserted
key: the integer key to insert
val: the value to store
is-new: true=a new key was inserted, false=an existing value was overwritten
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-int-tmpl.remove': `
Removes a key (marks a tombstone)
key: the key to delete
removed: whether something was actually deleted (only marks a tombstone)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.clear': 'Clears the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.contains': `
Reports whether a key exists
key: the key to check
found: whether the table contains that key
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.for-each': `
Iterates over every (key, value) pair
k: the key of this iteration
v: the value of this iteration
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.get': `
Looks up a key and returns ?v (nil = not found)
key: the key to look up
result: ok=the stored value, nil=key absent
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.hash': `
FNV-1a string hash
key: the string key to hash
idx: the FNV-1a hash masked into 256 slots
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.init': 'Initialises the map'
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.is-empty': `
Whether the map is empty
empty: whether it contains no elements at all
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n: current number of live key/value pairs (tombstones excluded)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.put': `
Inserts or updates key→value; returns whether it was inserted
key: the string key to insert
val: the value to store
is-new: true=a new key was inserted, false=an existing value was overwritten
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.remove': `
Removes a key (marks a tombstone)
key: the key to delete
removed: whether something was actually deleted (only marks a tombstone; unchanged when the key is absent)
`
  'static-hashmap.static-hashmap-str-tmpl.str-eq': `
Compares two strs for equality
a: the first string
b: the second string
eq: whether both sides are byte-for-byte equal
`
  'str': `
str — string operations standard library
str is the unified string type exposed to the outside
internal implementation: str-long (heap allocation)

a slice is a view, not a separate type: slicing a str (e.g. s[1..3]) still produces a str view,
sharing the underlying memory of str without copying data.
therefore all str methods (to-upper, to-lower, index, contains, slice, copy, fill, etc.)
apply to str slice views. Modifying elements through a slice affects the original str.
`
  'str-map': `
str-map — str→str hash map

Fixed capacity (256 slots), linear probing, hashes strings with FNV-1a.
Suited to small key-value scenarios (recommended ≤ 192 entries).

usage:
m = str-map{}
m.init()
m.put('name', 'Alice')
val = m.get('name')   // val = ok('Alice') or nil
m.for-each(k, v)
`
  'str-map.fnv-offset': 'FNV-1a constant'
  'str-map.str-map.clear': 'Clears the map'
  'str-map.str-map.contains': `
Reports whether a key exists
key: the key whose presence in the table is checked
found: true when the table already has that key
`
  'str-map.str-map.for-each': `
Iterates over every (key, value) pair
k: the key of the occupied entry scanned (a slot whose occ marker is 1)
v: the value corresponding to that occupied entry
`
  'str-map.str-map.get': `
Looks up a key and returns ?str (nil = not found)
key: the key to query
result: ok(the corresponding value) on a hit, nil when not found
`
  'str-map.str-map.hash': `
FNV-1a string hash
key: the key string to hash
idx: the FNV-1a hash modulo the capacity, i.e. bucket index (h & (cap - 1), cap must be a power of two)
`
  'str-map.str-map.init': 'Initialises the map'
  'str-map.str-map.is-empty': `
Whether the map is empty
empty: true when the table holds no elements
`
  'str-map.str-map.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n: current number of live key/value pairs in the table
`
  'str-map.str-map.put': `
Inserts or updates key→value; returns whether it was inserted
key: the key of the key/value pair to insert or update
val: the string value written (replaces the old value on overwrite)
is-new: true on the first insertion of a key, false when overwriting an existing one
`
  'str-map.str-map.remove': `
Removes a key
key: the key to delete
removed: true when an entry was actually removed, false when the key is absent
`
  'str-map.str-map.str-eq': `
Compares two strs for equality
a: the left string to compare
b: the right string to compare
eq: true when the two strings are byte-for-byte identical
`
  'str-map.str-map.to-str': `
A string representation
Formatted like `{name: Alice, age: 18}`
out: a readable string rendered as {key: value, ...}
`
  'str-set': `
str-set — str hash set

Fixed capacity (256 slots), linear probing, hashes strings with FNV-1a.
Suited to string deduplication and membership checks (recommended ≤ 192 entries).

usage:
s = str-set{}
s.init()
s.add('hello')
yes = s.contains('hello')   // yes = true
s.for-each(val)
`
  'str-set.ss-fnv-offset': 'FNV-1a constant'
  'str-set.str-set': `
str-set: str set instance hashed with FNV-1a
cap: total number of slots (fixed 256, must be a power of 2)
keys: strings stored in each slot (256 slots total); validity is determined by occ
occ: occupancy flag, 1=slot occupied, 0=empty slot
size: current number of elements
`
  'str-set.str-set.add': `
Adds an element; returns whether it was inserted
linear probing finds a slot; when the same string is hit, no duplicate insert happens.
with the table full and the string absent, probing loops forever; the caller must keep the element count under control.
key: the string to add (copied into the slot)
is-new: true=it was absent and got inserted, false=already existed, unchanged
`
  'str-set.str-set.clear': 'Clears the set'
  'str-set.str-set.contains': `
Reports whether an element exists
key: the string to query
found: true=the string exists in the set, false=absent (returns immediately when the set is empty)
`
  'str-set.str-set.for-each': `
Iterates over every element
Walks [0, cap) in slot-index order (not insertion order), assigning each occupied slot's string to val in turn.
This language has no callbacks; when the function returns, val stops at the element of the last non-empty slot.
val: the string of the current slot (the last assignment)
`
  'str-set.str-set.hash': `
FNV-1a string hash
key: the string to hash (walked byte by byte)
idx: slot index within [0, cap) (hash & (cap-1), case-insensitive)
`
  'str-set.str-set.init': 'Initialises the set'
  'str-set.str-set.is-empty': `
Whether the set is empty
empty: true=size == 0, false=elements remain
`
  'str-set.str-set.len': `
Returns the number of elements
n: current number of strings present in the set
`
  'str-set.str-set.remove': `
Removes an element
Clears the slot's occ and keys[i].len without rearranging the probe chain; adding the same key again reuses the slot.
key: the string to delete
removed: true=found and deleted, false=absent
`
  'str-set.str-set.str-eq': `
Compares two strs for equality
Compares length first for fast rejection, then matches byte by byte (no case-insensitive handling).
a: left operand
b: right operand
eq: true=same length and every byte identical, false=unequal
`
  'str._from-bytes': `
str._from-bytes: private constructor — wraps []byte into str with "take over the buffer" semantics (zero-copy)
🔒 private (`_` prefix): only src/std/str.no itself may call it; user code calls are rejected by the checker
(raqvyltn). For the public path use from-chars() — it has the compiler allocate a new buffer,
no ownership transfer, no double free.
builds str directly from the underlying data pointer of bs, without copying; n is the string length, clamped into
[0, bs.len()] (negative or overlong values never go out of bounds), cap takes the clamped value too.
the resulting str shares the buffer with bs (codegen already vec_retain), each released once; the original bs
must not be used as an independent buffer again.
handled by the compiler builtin (#{buildin}): after codegen intercepts, from the %vec data pointer + n
build %str-long; the signature is also registered in src/builtin/str.go (ForwardFunc "str-from-bytes").
bs: byte slice (shares the buffer with the result)
n: string length (byte count, clamped to [0, bs.len()])
s: str sharing the bs buffer (zero-copy)
`
  'str._set-byte': `
returns ?bool (fallible): on out-of-bounds (i < 0 or i >= physical capacity cap) returns nil and **skips the write**,
returns some(true) on success. Bounds use the cap (physical capacity) of %str-long rather than len, so code that "fills the buffer
then sets len" still works. Callers may discard the ?bool as a bare statement (optunuse only checks bound names).
i: byte index
b: byte value to write
ok: ok=true=write succeeded, nil=i out of bounds (write skipped)
`
  'str._str-dig-val': `
converts a single digit character (0-15) to its value; an invalid character returns -1
c: the code point of a digit character
d: 0~15; invalid characters return -1
`
  'str.at': `
[deprecated] str.at: character (code point) index read — use s[idx] instead (native indexing, returns ?char)
str.at: safe indexed access (by character index; for a byte offset use s.byte(i))
i: the character (code point) index
returns val: the code point value of that character (truncated when it exceeds the byte range); nil when out of range
`
  'str.bigint-bitlen': `
returns the bit length of a positive bigint (floor(log2)+1)
b: a positive bigint value
out: bit length (floor(log2)+1)
`
  'str.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at byte index i (raw byte read)
unlike s[i]: s[i] indexes by code point, this method reads by byte offset into the underlying UTF-8 buffer
Returns ?byte: out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()) returns nil, consistent with arr[i]'s
Safe-indexing semantics are consistent; callers must handle the option (match / ?=)
Handled by the compiler builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts and expands it into "bounds check + GEP+load",
There is no std function body; the signature is also registered in BuiltinMethodList in src/builtin/str.go
(MethodName "byte", Intercepted, the bare-name fallback covers both str and txt)
i: byte index (not a code point)
c: ok=the raw byte at that position, nil=i out of range
`
  'str.byte-index': `
converts a code point (character) index to a byte offset
Returns the byte position where the cp-pos-th code point starts; when cp-pos is past the end, returns .len
cp-pos: code point (character) index
byte: the byte
`
  'str.bytes': `
str.bytes(): returns a "zero-copy view" of the underlying UTF-8 bytes (actually just .data)
Unlike to-bytes(): no new buffer is allocated, no byte-by-byte copy. The returned []byte shares the str's
data buffer; writing elements is reflected in the original string; the view's cap is 0 (borrow marker), and at scope exit
vec_free skips freeing, ownership remains with the original str. The caller must ensure str outlives the view.
Handled as a compiler builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts it and builds %vec{len, cap=0, data} directly from the
len/data of %str-long; there is no std function body; the signature is also registered in
BuiltinMethodList of src/builtin/str.go (ForwardFunc "str-bytes").
note: writing `val []byte = s.data` directly is currently broken (codegen loads the data pointer as the whole
aggregate), so this method must be used.
val: borrowed view of the underlying UTF-8 bytes (length equals len-bytes())
`
  'str.compare': `
Compare two strings lexicographically
returns -1 (a<b), 0 (equal), 1 (a>b)
b: comparison target string
r: lexicographic -1 (receiver<b) / 0 (equal) / 1 (>)
`
  'str.contains': `
Determine whether s contains the substring target
returns true = contains, false = does not
target: the substring to find
yes: whether s contains target
`
  'str.copy': `
Copy src into dst (the whole string)
⚠️ DEPRECATED: assignment `dst = src` already has copy semantics — ownership analysis will, at src
If it is still read later it is automatically rewritten into a deep copy (OpClone); no manual copy needed. Kept only for compatibility.
dst: the copy (deprecated: plain assignment already has deep-copy semantics)
`
  'str.count': `
counts the chars (code points) in the string
.len already gives the byte count, so count provides the code point count
cnt: number of code points (chars)
`
  'str.cp-index': `
converts a byte offset to a code point (character) index
Returns the number of code points within s[0..byte-pos)
byte-pos: byte offset
cp: the number of code points in s[0..byte-pos)
`
  'str.decode-cp': `
decodes one UTF-8 code point starting at position i
returns cp = the code point value, clen = the sequence length in bytes (1-4)
i: the byte index to start decoding at
cp: the decoded code point value
clen: byte length of this code point's UTF-8 sequence (1~4)
`
  'str.empty': `
Determine whether the string is empty
returns true = empty, false = not empty
yes: whether the string length is 0
`
  'str.ends-with': `
Determine whether s ends with target
returns true = yes, false = no
target: the suffix substring
yes: whether s ends with target
`
  'str.eq-full': `
compares two strings for exact equality
b: the other string
returns yes: true = equal, false = not equal
`
  'str.fields': `
splits the string on one or more consecutive whitespace characters
Whitespace: space (32), tab (9), newline (10), carriage return (13)
returns out: the non-empty substrings
Consecutive whitespace counts as a single separator, and leading or trailing whitespace produces no empty element
`
  'str.fill': `
fills the whole string with a byte value
val: the byte value used to fill the entire string
`
  'str.find-byte-from': `
str.index: searches s for the substring target
Uses the Sunday algorithm, O(n/m) on average and O(n*m) in the worst case
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
str.find-byte-from: the internal byte-level search (Sunday algorithm)
Returns the BYTE offset of the first occurrence of target, or -1 when absent
from-byte is the starting byte position; used internally when slicing by bytes, as split does
target: the substring to find
from-byte: the start byte offset
pos: byte offset of target's first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'str.from-chars': `
str.from-chars: safely constructs a str from []char (public constructor, each char written as one raw byte)
Handled by a compiler builtin (#{buildin}, ForwardFunc "chars-new", same implementation as new()):
allocates a new buffer once, writes byte by byte and sets the length (len = cap = cs.len()), without borrowing
caller memory, so it is completely safe for the user (no double free / out of bounds).
note: each char keeps only its low 8 bits written as 1 byte (trunc i32→i8), with no UTF-8 re-encoding;
for UTF-8 bytes use char.to-bytes() and concatenate, or call to-str() then to-bytes().
cs: code point slice ([]char)
s: str built from cs byte by byte (new buffer, owns the data)
`
  'str.from-hex': `
decodes a hexadecimal string into []byte
returns Option:
ok(bytes) - success
nil        - empty string
err('msg') - contains an invalid hexadecimal character
out: ok=the decoded bytes, nil=empty string, err=illegal hex characters or odd length
`
  'str.index': `
searches s for the substring target
Uses the Sunday algorithm, O(n/m) on average and O(n*m) in the worst case
Returns the CODE POINT (character) position of the first occurrence, matching s[i]'s code point indexing; -1 when absent
target: the substring to find
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'str.index-from': `
searches s for the substring target starting at from (a code point position)
Returns the CODE POINT (character) position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
target: the substring to find
from: the start code point position (inclusive)
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'str.last-index': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring (last-index is an alias of rindex)
target: the substring to look for
returns pos: the code point (character) position found, or -1
`
  'str.len': `
returns the string's character (code point) count
Matches s[i]'s code point indexing; for a byte count use s.len-bytes()
n: number of code points (characters), matching the s[i] indexing semantics
`
  'str.len-bytes': `
str.len-bytes(): returns the string's "byte count" (length of the underlying UTF-8 buffer)
handled by the compiler as a builtin (build-in, equivalent to the old .len struct field); use this method for byte-level loops/slices in the standard library
`
  'str.lines': `
splits on newlines
Supports \n and \r\n
returns out: the list of lines (a trailing newline produces no empty line)
`
  'str.new': `
str.new: build a str from a char slice (module-level constructor)
compile-time builtin (ForwardFunc: chars-new): []char is converted byte by byte into bytes with the length set in one shot,
No byte-by-byte .no loop. str is a builtin type whose bytes/length cannot be manipulated in user code, so the whole conversion is emitted by the compiler as IR.
cs: the code point slice ([]char)
