`
  'str.push': `
appends one code point (grows in place, UTF-8 encoded)
After the global ban on `s[i] = v` this is the only variable-length write entry point:
index writes were dangerous because they changed the length implicitly; growth must be
one explicit, auditable call.
c: the code point to append
`
  'str.push-str': `
appends a string (grows in place, byte copy)
Handled by the compiler as a builtin (#{buildin}, ForwardFunc "str-push-bytes", no std body):
it reallocates %str-long's buffer in place (doubling + memmove) instead of building a new
string and concatenating, which would cost O(N) per call. Self-append `s.push-str(s)` is
safe (retain first, then copy).
s: the string to append
`
  'str.repeat': `
Repeat a string times times
returns the new string
times: repetition count (<= 0 yields an empty string)
val: a copy after concatenating times copies
`
  'str.replace-char': `
Replace every old-ch in s with new-ch (UTF-8 codepoints)
returns the result string
old-ch: the code point that was replaced
new-ch: the code point after replacement
val: a copy after per-codepoint replacement
