`
  'str.string': `
string = str | txt — union type alias for the string family (mirroring num = int | float in number.no)
usage: a function parameter/return value declared as string takes a str or txt argument at the call site,
monomorphized at compile time by the concrete argument type (ValidateUnionTypes + FlattenUnion + clone),
one concrete function generated per member (e.g. f__str / f__txt).
capability boundary (the same existing limitations as num):
- calling member methods (s.len()/s.to-upper()) on a union parameter inside the function body is not yet supported
(monomorphization name-qualification defect), use a global builtin instead (len(s) is byte count) or split into
two concrete functions, str/txt;
- union variable declarations (s string = 'x') are not yet supported;
- do not define alias methods like string.len that "share a name with a member": they take over s.len()
calls, and the method body recursively dispatches back to itself (segfault). For member methods use the original str.*/txt.* versions.
`
  'str.to-bigint': `
parses a string into a bigint, detecting the base automatically
Prefixes 0b/0B -> binary, 0o/0O -> octal, 0x/0X -> hexadecimal; no prefix -> decimal
Accepts optional leading whitespace and a sign (+ / -)
returns ?bigint: ok(bigint) on success / nil for an empty string / err('msg') when there are no digits
out: ok=a bigint parsed with the prefix radix (0b/0o/0x or decimal), nil=empty string, err=no valid digits
`
  'str.to-bool': `
parses the string "true" / "false" into a bool
Case-sensitive; an empty string -> nil, anything else -> err
Uses byte-by-byte comparison to avoid strcmp's memory problems across function calls
val: ok=true/false (only '"true"'/'"false"' recognized, case sensitive), nil=empty string, err=anything else
`
  'str.to-byte': `
parses the string into a byte (equivalent to u8)
returns Option:
ok(n) - success (0 .. 255)
nil    - empty string
err('msg') - contains a non-digit character, a minus sign, or is out of range
val: ok=a byte in range (0~255), nil=empty string, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'str.to-bytes': `
