`
  'tar': `
tar — TAR archive read/write

Reads/writes TAR archives in POSIX ustar format.
No external libraries; operates only on []byte.

usage (read):
archive = tar{
data: raw-bytes
}
n = archive.count()
i <- [0..n): {
e = archive.entry(i)
name = e.name()
sz = e.size()
typ = e.type()
content = e.read()
}

usage (read .tar.gz):
archive = open-gz(gz-data)
n = archive.count()
...

usage (write):
builder = builder{}
builder.add-file('hello.txt', 'hello world')
builder.add-dir('subdir/')
archive = builder.finish()
`
  'tar.builder': 'builder: TAR archive builder'
  'tar.builder.add-bytes': `
Add a raw byte entry
name: entry name
content: entry content bytes
typ: entry type code (TAR-TYPE-FILE / TAR-TYPE-DIR)
`
  'tar.builder.add-dir': `
Add a directory entry
name: directory name (conventionally ending with /, no content area)
`
  'tar.builder.add-file': `
Add a file entry
name: file name (written to the header name field)
content: file content (written, then zero-padded to the 512-byte boundary)
`
  'tar.builder.finish': `
Finish building, write the end marker and return the tar archive
archive: read-only tar view containing the two all-zero end blocks
`
  'tar.builder.pre-alloc': `
Pre-allocate the buffer (push via a local variable to work around the struct-field push limitation)
size: number of bytes to pre-allocate in the buffer (zero-filled to this length)
`
  'tar.builder.write-header': `
Write the 512-byte header (internal method)
name: file name (written at header offset 0)
typ: typeflag character code (48=file, 53=directory)
sz: content byte count (written to the size field in octal)
`
  'tar.builder.write-octal': `
Write an octal number at the given offset (internal method)
offset: field offset within the current header block
width: field width (octal digits right-aligned, left padded with spaces)
val: integer value to encode (a NUL is appended at the block end)
`
  'tar.entry': 'entry: TAR entry (points to a header in archive.data)'
  'tar.entry.is-dir': `
Whether the entry is a directory
yes: whether this entry is a directory
`
  'tar.entry.is-file': `
Whether the entry is a regular file
yes: whether this entry is a regular file
`
  'tar.entry.mode': `
Read the entry permissions
mode: permission bits (octal decoded)
`
  'tar.entry.mtime': `
Read the entry modification time
ts: modification time (Unix seconds, octal decoded)
`
  'tar.entry.name': `
Read the entry file name
name: this entry's file name
`
  'tar.entry.read': `
Extract the entry content
out: entry content bytes (empty for directories)
`
  'tar.entry.size': `
Read the entry file size
sz: entry's raw byte count
`
  'tar.entry.type': `
Read the entry type
typ: type string ("file", "dir" or "unknown")
`
  'tar.open-gz': `
Create a tar view from gzip-compressed data (.tar.gz)
gz-data: gzip-compressed tar data ([]byte)
archive: the decompressed tar archive
`
  'tar.tar': 'tar: TAR archive read-only view'
  'tar.tar-block': 'TAR block size'
  'tar.tar-type-file': 'Entry type'
  'tar.tar.count': `
Count the valid entries in the TAR
count: number of entries before the end marker (all-zero block)
`
  'tar.tar.entry': `
Get the idx-th entry
idx: entry ordinal
e: tar-entry pointing at its 512-byte header (fields read via its methods)
`
  'tar.tar.is-dir': `
Whether the idx-th entry is a directory
idx: entry ordinal
yes: whether it is a directory entry (typeflag 5)
`
  'tar.tar.is-file': `
Whether the idx-th entry is a regular file
idx: entry ordinal
yes: whether it is a regular file entry (typeflag 0)
`
  'tar.tar.mode': `
Read the idx-th entry's permissions (octal)
idx: entry ordinal
mode: file permission bits (mode field octal decoded, e.g. 0o644)
`
  'tar.tar.mtime': `
Read the idx-th entry's modification time (octal Unix timestamp)
idx: entry ordinal
ts: modification time (Unix seconds, mtime field octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar.name': `
Read the idx-th entry's file name
idx: entry ordinal
name: file name (header name field, truncated at NUL/space)
`
  'tar.tar.offset': `
Compute the idx-th entry's actual byte offset (traversing all preceding entries)
idx: entry ordinal (0 ~ count-1)
off: starting byte offset of the entry's header within the archive data
`
  'tar.tar.read': `
Extract the idx-th entry's content
idx: entry ordinal
out: entry content bytes (read for the length given by the size field, empty for directories)
`
  'tar.tar.size': `
Read the idx-th entry's file size (octal)
idx: entry ordinal
sz: file's raw byte count (size field octal decoded)
`
  'tar.tar.type': `
Read the idx-th entry's type string
returns "file", "dir" or "unknown"
idx: entry ordinal
typ: entry type string (mapped from typeflag to "file", "dir" or "unknown")
`
  'tdes': `
tdes — Triple DES (3DES) encryption/decryption (ECB mode)

Triple DES uses three 8-byte keys (K1, K2, K3), applying DES three times.
encrypt: C = E_K3(D_K2(E_K1(P)))
decrypt: P = D_K1(E_K2(D_K3(C)))

supports 2-key mode (K1=K3) and 3-key mode.

usage:
# std/crypto/tdes
key [24]byte  // K1=key[0:8], K2=key[8:16], K3=key[16:24]
out = tdes-enc(plain, plain.len(), key)   // encrypt
out = tdes-dec(cipher, cipher.len(), key) // decrypt
`
  'tdes.tdes-cbc-dec': `
Triple DES-CBC decrypt
Each block is 3DES-ECB decrypted first, then XORed with the previous ciphertext segment (the iv for the first block) to recover plaintext.
in: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 8)
key: key (K1 || K2 || K3), must match the encryption key
iv: initial vector, must be the same as during encryption
returns out: decrypted data, same length as in
`
  'tdes.tdes-cbc-enc': `
Triple DES-CBC encrypt
Each block is XORed with the previous ciphertext block (the iv for the first) before 3DES-ECB,
so the same plaintext yields different ciphertext under different ivs.
in: input data (length must be a multiple of 8)
key: key (K1 || K2 || K3)
iv: initial vector, should be unique per message
returns out: encrypted data, same length as in
`
  'tdes.tdes-dec': `
Triple DES decrypt (ECB mode)
The inverse of encryption: D_K3 → E_K2 → D_K1; each 8-byte block is processed independently.
in: input ciphertext (length must be a multiple of 8)
n: number of bytes actually processed (usually in.len())
key: key (K1 || K2 || K3), must match the encryption key
returns out: decrypted data, same length as n
`
  'tdes.tdes-enc': `
Triple DES encrypt (ECB mode)
Runs E_K1 → D_K2 → E_K3 on each 8-byte block; blocks are independent (same plaintext gives same ciphertext).
in: input data (length must be a multiple of 8; a trailing partial block is not processed)
n: number of bytes actually processed (usually in.len())
key: key (K1 || K2 || K3, 8 bytes each)
returns out: encrypted data, same length as n
`
  'time': `
time — time operations

provides Unix timestamps, formatting, sleep, duration calculation, timers.
depends on os module syscalls underneath (now, now-ms, now-us, now-ns, sleep, sleep-us, sleep-ns).

also provides a time struct (an absolute point in time represented by sec + nsec): functions such as now-time, unix-to-time,
duration-time, since-time, parse-time, plus methods like .unix/.date/.format,
letting callers work with time rather than raw i64 timestamps.

note: now-ms, now-us, now-ns, sleep-us, sleep-ns are already provided by builtins,
and are not redefined here (a user function would shadow the same-named builtin and cause recursion);
they are called directly as builtins.
`
  'time._days-in-month': 'days-in-month table (non-leap year)'
  'time.add-seconds': `
adds seconds and returns a new time
n: seconds to add (may be negative)
t: the new time (sec+n, nsec unchanged)
`
  'time.date': `
converts to a date struct (UTC, nsec ignored)
d: the UTC date struct corresponding to .sec
`
  'time.date-2': 'date: date-time structure (UTC)'
  'time.date-to-unix': `
converts a date struct (UTC) into a Unix timestamp (seconds)
Uses the days-from-civil algorithm (Howard Hinnant)
d: the UTC date struct
ts: the Unix timestamp (seconds) of the corresponding moment
`
  'time.days-in-month-fn': `
_days-in-month: gets the number of days in the given year and month
y: the year (used for the leap-year test)
m: the month (1..12)
d: days in that month (February returns 29/28 by leap year; returns 31 when m is out of range)
`
  'time.duration-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (seconds)
start: starting timestamp (seconds)
end: the end timestamp (seconds)
diff: end - start (seconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.duration-ms-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (milliseconds)
start: starting timestamp (milliseconds)
end: the end timestamp (milliseconds)
diff: end - start (milliseconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.duration-time': `
computes the difference between two time values (seconds)
start: starting time point
end: the end point in time
diff: the difference of both sec fields (seconds, nsec ignored)
`
  'time.duration-us-between': `
computes the difference between two timestamps (microseconds)
start: starting timestamp (microseconds)
end: the end timestamp (microseconds)
diff: end - start (microseconds, wrapping on overflow)
`
  'time.format': `
formats as 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' (UTC, nsec ignored)
out: a 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' string
`
  'time.format-date': `
formats a date struct into a string
Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (a negative year gets a '-' prefix)
d: the date struct to format
out: a 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS' string (each field zero-padded)
`
  'time.format-duration': `
formats a number of seconds into a human-readable string
e.g. 3661 -> '1h1m1s'
seconds: duration (seconds)
out: an 'NhHmMs' style string (all three segments always shown, e.g. 60 → '0h1m0s'; negatives not handled)
`
  'time.format-time-str': `
formats a Unix timestamp into a readable string
Format: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS (UTC)
ts: Unix timestamp (seconds)
out: formatted UTC time string
`
  'time.is-leap': `
tests whether a year is a leap year
y: the year
leap: whether it is a leap year (divisible by 4 and not by 100, or divisible by 400)
`
  'time.now-date': `
gets the current date-time struct (UTC)
d: the current UTC date and time
`
  'time.now-ms': `
the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (milliseconds)
`
  'time.now-ns': `
the current timestamp (nanoseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (nanoseconds)
`
  'time.now-s': `
the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
Wraps the builtin now()
ts: the current Unix timestamp (seconds)
`
  'time.now-time': `
the current instant (UTC)
Splits sec and nsec out of the builtin now-ns() (CLOCK_REALTIME nanoseconds)
t: the current time point (both sec and nsec from CLOCK_REALTIME)
`
  'time.now-us': `
the current Unix timestamp (microseconds)
ts: the current Unix timestamp (microseconds)
`
  'time.parse-date': `
parses a date string into a Unix timestamp
Accepted formats: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS or YYYY-MM-DD
returns ?i64: ok = the timestamp, nil = parsing failed
s: date string (YYYY-MM-DD, or the full form with HH:MM:SS)
ts: ok=the corresponding Unix timestamp (seconds), nil=insufficient length, illegal separator/digits, or field out of range
`
  'time.parse-time': `
parses a date string into a time
Accepts the same formats as parse-date: YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS or YYYY-MM-DD
returns ?time: ok = the time, nil = parsing failed
s: date string (same format as parse-date)
t: ok=the parsed time (nsec=0), nil=parse failed
`
  'time.since-ms': `
milliseconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting timestamp (milliseconds)
diff: milliseconds elapsed so far
`
  'time.since-s': `
seconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting timestamp (seconds, typically taken from now-s/os.now)
diff: seconds elapsed so far
`
  'time.since-time': `
seconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting time point
diff: seconds between now and start (nsec ignored)
`
  'time.since-us': `
microseconds elapsed from start until now
start: starting timestamp (microseconds)
diff: microseconds elapsed so far
`
  'time.sleep-ms': `
sleeps for the given number of milliseconds
There is no matching builtin, so it is implemented as sleep-us(ms * 1000)
ms: sleep duration (milliseconds; converted internally to microseconds for sleep-us)
`
  'time.sleep-ns': `
sleeps for the given number of nanoseconds
ns: sleep duration (nanoseconds)
`
  'time.sleep-s': `
sleeps for the given number of seconds
Wraps the builtin sleep()
s: sleep duration (seconds)
`
  'time.sleep-us': `
sleeps for the given number of microseconds
us: sleep duration (microseconds)
`
  'time.sub': `
the difference from another time (seconds, . - other)
other: the time point being subtracted
diff: .sec - other.sec (seconds, nsec ignored)
`
  'time.time': `
time: time-point struct (UTC)
represents an absolute point in time with Unix seconds (sec) and nanoseconds (nsec, 0~999,999,999).
combined with constructors like now-time, unix-to-time and methods like .unix, .date, .format,
lets callers work with time rather than a bare i64 timestamp.
`
  'time.timer': `
timer: timer struct
start-ts: start timestamp (milliseconds)
end-ts: end timestamp (milliseconds)
`
  'time.timer.elapsed-ms': `
elapsed milliseconds
ms: difference of end-ts minus start-ts (milliseconds; end-ts is a stale value or 0 when stop was never called)
`
  'time.timer.elapsed-s': `
elapsed seconds
s: the seconds obtained by dividing the millisecond difference by 1000
`
  'time.timer.elapsed-us': `
elapsed microseconds
us: approximate microseconds from the millisecond difference × 1000 (precision remains milliseconds)
`
  'time.timer.start': 'starts the timer'
  'time.timer.stop': 'stops the timer'
  'time.unix': `
gets the Unix timestamp in seconds
ts: the .sec field (Unix seconds)
`
  'time.unix-ms': `
gets the Unix timestamp in milliseconds
ms: .sec × 1000 + .nsec/10⁶ (milliseconds; wraps on overflow)
`
  'time.unix-to-date': `
converts a Unix timestamp (seconds) into a date struct (UTC)
Uses the classic civil-from-days algorithm (Howard Hinnant)
ts: Unix timestamp (seconds)
d: the corresponding UTC date and time (year/month/day/hour/min/sec)
`
  'time.unix-to-time': `
builds a time from a Unix timestamp in seconds (nsec=0)
ts: Unix timestamp (seconds)
t: the corresponding time point (nsec=0)
`
  'tls': `
tls — TLS 1.2 / 1.3 client (pure Nolang implementation, no OpenSSL dependency)

TLS 1.3: supports cipher suite TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 (0x1301)
uses AES-128-GCM (AEAD) + X25519 ECDHE key exchange + HKDF key schedule

TLS 1.2: supports cipher suites
ECDHE (X25519 key exchange):
TLS_ECDHE_RSA/AES128-GCM-SHA256 (0xC02F), TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_AES128-GCM-SHA256 (0xC02B)
TLS_ECDHE_RSA_AES128_CBC-SHA256 (0xC027), TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_AES128-CBC-SHA256 (0xC023)
static-RSA (compatibility fallback): TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA256 (0x003C)
uses AES-128-GCM (AEAD, RFC 5288) or AES-128-CBC + HMAC-SHA256

uses TCP builtins (net-dial, net-send, net-recv, close) underneath
crypto primitives come from AES-128, SHA-256, RSA, HMAC-SHA256, X.509 under std/hash,
AES-GCM, HKDF, X25519

usage:
conn = dial('example.com', 443)         // returns ?conn
conn: {
nil -> return
-> w = conn.send(data)          // returns ?i64
w: { nil -> return }
r = conn.recv(buf, 4096)       // returns i64 (-1=error, 0=closed, >0=byte count)
r < 0 -> return
n = r
conn.close()
}
`
  'tls-server-keys': `
tls-server-keys — pre-generated 2048-bit RSA key pair (embedded)

Source: openssl genrsa 2048
Purpose: self-signed certificates for the HTTPS server and TLS handshake signatures.
Since RSA key generation in pure Nolang takes 20+ minutes, a pre-generated key is embedded here.
The certificate itself is produced by Nolang code with DER encoding + RSA signing, verifying Nolang's PEM/DER capability.
`
  'tls-server-keys.tls-server-rsa-d': 'RSA private exponent d (256 bytes, big-endian)'
  'tls-server-keys.tls-server-rsa-e': 'RSA public exponent e = 65537 (0x10001)'
  'tls-server-keys.tls-server-rsa-n': 'RSA modulus n (256 bytes, big-endian, without the DER leading 0x00)'
  'tls.bn-to-bytes': `
[]i64 (little-endian base 2^32) → big-endian byte array
num: big integer
returns out: big-endian bytes
`
  'tls.build-nonce': `
Build the AEAD nonce
iv: AEAD IV derived from the write key
seq: record sequence number (right-aligned to 8 bytes then XORed into the tail of the IV)
nonce: the AEAD nonce for this record
`
  'tls.bytes-cat': `
Concatenate two byte arrays
a: front array
b: back array (appended after a)
out: the new concatenated array (length = a.len() + b.len())
`
  'tls.bytes-to-bn': `
big-endian byte array → []i64 (little-endian base 2^32)
data: big-endian bytes
returns out: big integer (each element a 32-bit limb)
`
  'tls.cipher-is-ecdhe': `
Whether a TLS 1.2 cipher suite uses ECDHE key exchange
Currently covers the four combinations of ECDHE-ECDSA/RSA with AES128-CBC-SHA256 and AES128-GCM-SHA256.
c: cipher suite code (TLS-CIPHER-* constants)
r: true = one of those four (ECDHE key exchange possible), false = other suites
`
  'tls.cipher-is-gcm': `
Whether a TLS 1.2 cipher suite uses the AEAD (AES-GCM) record format
Only ECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256 and ECDHE-RSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256.
c: cipher suite code (TLS-CIPHER-* constants)
r: true = AES-GCM records (explicit nonce required), false = CBC + HMAC format
`
  'tls.conn': `
conn: TLS connection (supports 1.2 and 1.3)
fd: underlying TCP socket fd
host: server hostname (for SNI)
connected: connection state
encrypted: whether encryption is enabled
version: negotiated TLS version (0x0303=1.2, 0x0304=1.3)
write-seq / read-seq: record layer sequence numbers (used by TLS 1.2)
rand-state: PRNG state
hs-buf: handshake message buffer (for Finished verification)
`
  'tls.conn.append-hs': `
Append a handshake message to the buffer
data: message bytes to append to the end of the handshake buffer
`
  'tls.conn.append-hs-from': `
Append n bytes from buf at offset to the handshake buffer
buf: source buffer (including the record header)
offset: starting offset (usually 5, skipping the record header)
n: number of bytes to append
`
  'tls.conn.close': 'Close the TLS connection'
  'tls.conn.compute-shared-secret-13': 'Compute the ECDHE shared secret'
  'tls.conn.connect': `
Establish a TLS connection (negotiates 1.3/1.2 automatically)
host: server hostname
port: port
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.connect-12': `
Full TLS 1.2 handshake (pure TLS 1.2, no supported_versions extension sent)
returns ok: true = success
host: server hostname or IP
port: TCP port
ok: true = TLS 1.2 handshake completed
`
  'tls.conn.connect-13': `
Full TLS 1.3 handshake
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.decrypt-record': `
Decrypt a record
buf: received record data (starting from the header)
returns ct: original content type (-1 = error)
returns out: decrypted plaintext
expected-ct: expected content type (compared after decryption; a mismatch counts as an error)
`
  'tls.conn.decrypt-record-13': `
TLS 1.3 AEAD record decryption
rec: received record (with header)
key: AEAD key
iv: AEAD IV
seq: expected sequence number of this record (participates in nonce derivation; a wrong order fails AEAD verification)
returns ct: original content type (-1 = error)
returns out: decrypted plaintext (excluding the content type byte)
`
  'tls.conn.decrypt-record-gcm': `
TLS 1.2 AES-128-GCM AEAD record decryption (RFC 5288)
buf: received record (starting from the header)
expected-ct: expected content type (used as AAD)
key: server_write_key
salt: server_write_IV (implicit salt)
seq: record sequence number
returns ct: success = expected-ct, -1 = error
returns out: decrypted plaintext
`
  'tls.conn.derive-application-keys-13': 'Derive the application keys'
  'tls.conn.derive-handshake-keys-13': `
derive-traffic-keys-13: derive handshake keys and application keys
Using the HKDF key schedule (RFC 8446 §7.1)
`
  'tls.conn.derive-keys': `
Derive all keys from the premaster secret
premaster: premaster secret (static-RSA 48 bytes / ECDHE 32 bytes)
`
  'tls.conn.encrypt-record': `
Encrypt a record and send it
ct: original content type
data: plaintext data
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.encrypt-record-13': `
TLS 1.3 AEAD record encryption and send
ct: original content type
data: plaintext data
key: AEAD key
iv: AEAD IV
seq: sequence number of this record (incremented after each send, participates in nonce derivation)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.encrypt-record-gcm': `
TLS 1.2 AES-128-GCM AEAD record encryption (RFC 5288)
ct: content type
data: plaintext
key: client/server_write_key
salt: client/server_write_IV (implicit salt)
seq: record sequence number
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.gen-p256-keypair': `
Generate an ephemeral P-256 private key (big-endian) and uncompressed public key
Stored in .client-priv/.pub-p256 (for secp256r1 ECDHE)
`
  'tls.conn.gen-x25519-keypair': `
send-client-hello: send ClientHello
returns ok: true = success
Generate an ephemeral X25519 key pair (stored in .client-priv/.client-pub)
Implemented inline to work around a compiler bug with multi-return [32]byte
`
  'tls.conn.get-fd': `
Diagnostics — expose the underlying socket fd
fd: file descriptor of the underlying socket (diagnostics only)
`
  'tls.conn.handshake-12-body': `
Full TLS 1.2 handshake after ServerHello
(Certificate → [ECDHE: ServerKeyExchange] → ServerHelloDone → key exchange → CCS/Finished)
Depends on .cipher-id (recorded by read-server-hello / read-server-hello-13) and, for ECDHE, .client-priv/.client-pub
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.init': 'Initialize the connection (not yet connected)'
  'tls.conn.read-certificate': `
Read Certificate
cert-out: output certificate DER data
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.read-change-cipher-spec': `
Read ChangeCipherSpec
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.read-encrypted-handshake-13': `
Read an encrypted handshake message
Supports multiple handshake messages inside a single record (leftover data buffered in hs-plain-buf)
returns msg-type: message type (-1 = error)
returns msg-data: message data (without header)
`
  'tls.conn.read-encrypted-record-13': `
Read and decrypt one TLS 1.3 record
key: AEAD key
iv: AEAD IV
seq: sequence number (automatically incremented after reading)
returns ct: original content type (-1 = error)
returns out: decrypted plaintext
`
  'tls.conn.read-finished': `
Read the encrypted Finished message
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.read-server-hello': `
Read ServerHello
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.read-server-hello-13': `
Read ServerHello and detect the TLS version
returns ok: true = TLS 1.3, false = TLS 1.2 or failure
`
  'tls.conn.read-server-hello-done': `
Read ServerHelloDone
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.read-server-key-exchange': `
Read the ECDHE ServerKeyExchange (ECDHE suites only)
Parses the server's ephemeral X25519 public key into .server-pub (the certificate and signature are not verified, consistent with this implementation's trust model)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.recv': `
Receives and decrypts one data record, writing the plaintext into buf
buf: receive buffer (string, capacity must be large enough for n bytes)
n: maximum number of bytes to receive
read-n: number of bytes actually received (-1=error)
`
  'tls.conn.send': `
Send data over TLS
data: plaintext to send
written: bytes written (nil = encrypt or send failed)
`
  'tls.conn.send-change-cipher-spec': 'Send ChangeCipherSpec'
  'tls.conn.send-client-hello': `
Send the TLS 1.2 ClientHello (with ECDHE cipher suites and extensions)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.send-client-hello-13': `
Send the TLS 1.3 ClientHello (with extensions)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.send-client-key-exchange': `
Send ClientKeyExchange
premaster: premaster secret (for static-RSA suites, 48 bytes; ignored for ECDHE suites)
cert: server certificate DER (for static-RSA suites)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.send-finished': `
Send the encrypted Finished message
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.conn.send-finished-13': `
Send the TLS 1.3 Finished message
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.dial': `
Convenience function to establish a TLS connection
host: server hostname
port: port
returns conn: connection (nil = connect failed)
`
  'tls.dial-12': `
TLS 1.2 connection (pure TLS 1.2, no 1.3 negotiation)
host: hostname or IP
port: port
returns conn: ok = connection, nil = failure
`
  'tls.get-u16': `
Read a 16-bit big-endian value from a buffer
buf: source buffer (reads buf[pos..pos+1])
pos: start position (most significant byte first)
val: decoded 16-bit big-endian value (out-of-range bytes count as zero)
`
  'tls.get-u24': `
Read a 24-bit big-endian value from a buffer
buf: source buffer (reads buf[pos..pos+2])
pos: start position (most significant byte first)
val: decoded 24-bit big-endian value (out-of-range bytes count as zero)
`
  'tls.https-serve-once': `
Complete HTTPS request-response cycle (single function)
Does accept + handshake + recv HTTP + send HTTP + close in one function.
All TLS state stays in the local variable c, avoiding a global-variable bug and a struct-copy bug.
listen-fd: listening socket fd
status: HTTP response status code
body: HTTP response body
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.net-recv-wait': `
Blocking receive (implemented with non-blocking recv plus short sleeps)
Works around the native blocking recv hanging on macOS when data is ready but never returned.
returns r: >0 = byte count, 0 = peer closed (FIN), -1 = error
fd: socket fd (received non-blockingly while polling)
buf: string buffer to store received data
n: maximum bytes per single receive
r: >0 = bytes received, 0 = peer closed normally (FIN), -1 = error
`
  'tls.prf': `
TLS 1.2 PRF (P_SHA256)
secret: key material
label: label string
seed: seed
out-len: required output length
returns out: PRF output
`
  'tls.put-u16': `
Write a 16-bit big-endian value into a buffer
buf: target buffer, writes buf[pos..pos+1]
pos: start position (most significant byte first)
val: 16-bit integer value to write
`
  'tls.put-u24': `
Write a 24-bit big-endian value into a buffer
buf: target buffer, writes buf[pos..pos+2]
pos: start position (most significant byte first)
val: 24-bit integer value to write
`
  'tls.rand-bytes': `
Generate random bytes
n: number of bytes required
returns out: random byte array
state: random seed state (advanced byte by byte inside rand.rand)
`
  'tls.read-record': `
Read one TLS record
fd: socket fd
buf: record buffer (header + data, buf[0..4] = header, buf[5..] = body)
returns ct: content type (-1 = error)
returns rlen: data length (excluding header)
`
  'tls.recv-n': `
Read exactly n bytes from the socket
fd: socket fd
buf: receive buffer
n: number of bytes to read
returns ok: true = success
`
  'tls.rsa-pkcs1-decrypt': `
RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 decrypt
ciphertext: RSA-encrypted data
n: modulus (big-endian)
d: private exponent (big-endian)
returns out: decrypted plaintext (nil = failure)
`
  'tls.rsa-pkcs1-encrypt': `
RSA PKCS#1 v1.5 encrypt
msg: message (premaster secret, 48 bytes)
mod-bytes: RSA modulus (big-endian)
exp: RSA public exponent
returns out: encrypted data (length = mod-bytes.len)
`
  'tls.send-raw': `
Send a raw TLS record (unencrypted)
fd: socket fd
ct: content type (written as the first byte of the record header)
data: payload bytes for this record (sent directly without encryption)
`
  'tls.server-accept': `
Accept a TCP connection and run the TLS handshake
Uses local variables for the handshake (global variables hit a compiler bug: inconsistent addresses across functions)
listen-fd: listening socket fd
returns conn: ok = connection (with handshake keys), nil = failure
`
  'tls.server-conn': `
server-conn: TLS server connection
fd: client socket fd
rand-state: PRNG state
client-random / server-random: handshake random numbers
client-mac-key / server-mac-key: MAC keys (32 bytes each)
client-enc-key / server-enc-key: encryption keys (16 bytes each)
write-seq / read-seq: record layer sequence numbers
hs-buf / hs-buf-len: handshake buffer (for Finished verification)
encrypted: whether encryption is enabled
master-secret: master secret (48 bytes)
cert: pre-generated certificate DER
n-bytes / d-bytes: RSA key material
e-val: RSA public exponent
version: negotiated TLS version
is-tls13: whether TLS 1.3 is used
client-priv / server-priv / server-pub / client-pub: X25519 key pairs
shared-secret: ECDHE shared key (temporarily stores handshake secret for deriving application keys)
hs-c-secret / hs-s-secret: handshake traffic keys
hs-{client,server}-{key,iv}: handshake-layer AEAD keys/IVs
ap-{client,server}-{key,iv}: application-layer AEAD keys/IVs
hs-{write,read}-seq / ap-{write,read}-seq: TLS 1.3 sequence numbers
hs-plain-{buf,pos,len}: handshake plaintext buffer
`
  'tls.server-conn.append-hs': `
Append handshake data to the buffer
data: data to append to the handshake buffer
`
  'tls.server-conn.append-hs-from': `
Append n bytes from buf at offset
buf: source buffer
offset: starting read offset
n: number of bytes to append
`
  'tls.server-conn.close': 'Close the connection'
  'tls.server-conn.compute-shared-secret-13': 'Compute the ECDHE shared secret'
  'tls.server-conn.decrypt-record': `
Decrypt a record (using CLIENT keys)
buf: received record data (starting from the header)
expected-ct: original content type (TLS-CT-HANDSHAKE or TLS-CT-APP-DATA)
returns ct: original content type (-1 = error)
returns out: decrypted plaintext
`
