`
  'toml.value-complete': `
determines whether a value is closed; used for multiline arrays, inline table, multiline string.
raw: raw value text
complete: whether quotes/brackets are closed (decides whether a multiline value read can end)
`
  'tree-map': `
tree-map — ordered map (based on AVL self-balancing binary search tree)

Ordered i64→i64 map, implemented with an AVL tree.
All operations are O(log n); keys are traversed in ascending order.

Differences from linked-hash-map:
- tree-map sorts by key (ascending), linked-hash-map by insertion order
- tree-map supports lower-bound/upper-bound ordered queries
- tree-map uses an AVL tree, linked-hash-map uses a hash table + linked list
- tree-map's get returns a ?i64 option, linked-hash-map uses (found, result) parameters

usage:
m = tree-map{}
m.init()
ok = m.put(3, 30)       // ok=true (new)
ok = m.put(1, 10)       // ok=true
ok = m.put(2, 20)       // ok=true
ok = m.put(1, 99)       // ok=false (updating existing key)
val = m.get(2)           // val = ok
key = m.first()          // key = ok (smallest key)
key = m.last()           // key = ok (largest key)
key = m.lower-bound(2)   // key = ok (first key ≥ 2)
key = m.upper-bound(2)   // key = ok (first key > 2)
ok = m.remove(2)         // ok=true
yes = m.contains(3)      // yes=true
m.for-each(k, v)         // traverse all key-value pairs in ascending order

// option unwrapping
val = m.get(42)
val: {
nil -> print('not found')
-> print(it)
}
`
  'tree-map.tree-map': `
tree-map: AVL ordered map
root: root node index, -1=empty tree
free-head: head index of the free node pool, -1=pool full
size: current element count
keys/vals: key/value storage
left/right/parent: tree structure indices, -1=NIL
height: node height (leaf=1, NIL=0)
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.alloc-node': `
Allocates a node from the free pool
Returns idx: the node index, or -1 when the pool is full
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.bf': `
Returns the balance factor (left height minus right height)
idx: the node index
Returns b: the balance factor
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.clear': 'Removes every element and rebuilds the free pool'
  'tree-map.tree-map.contains': `
Reports whether a key exists
key: the key
Returns yes: true = present, false = absent
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.empty': `
Reports whether the tree is empty
Returns yes: true = empty, false = not empty
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.first': `
Returns the smallest key
Returns key: ok = the smallest key, nil = the tree is empty
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.for-each': `
Iterates over every key/value pair in ascending key order
k: the output key (updated per element)
v: the output value (updated per element)
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.free-node': `
Returns a node to the free pool
idx: the index of the node to release
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.full': `
Reports whether the node pool is full
Returns yes: true = full, false = not full
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.get': `
Looks up the value for a key
key: the key
Returns val: ok = found, nil = not found
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.ht': `
Returns the node height (NIL = 0)
idx: the node index
Returns h: the height
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.init': 'Initialises an empty tree and its free node pool'
  'tree-map.tree-map.last': `
Returns the largest key
Returns key: ok = the largest key, nil = the tree is empty
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.lower-bound': `
Finds the first key >= target
target: the target key
Returns key: ok = found, nil = no such key
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.put': `
Inserts or updates key→value
key: sort key
val: integer value to write (overwrites the old value when the key already exists)
Returns ok: true = inserted, false = an existing key was updated or the pool is full
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.rebalance': `
Checks and repairs the balance at node idx
idx: the node index
Returns nr: the subtree root index after rebalancing
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.rebalance-up': `
Rebalances level by level upwards from idx
idx: the starting node index
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.remove': `
Removes a key
key: the key
Returns ok: true = removed, false = not found
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.rot-left': `
Left rotation
x                y
/ \              / \
a   y     =>     x   c
/ \          / \
b   c        a   b
x: the pivot node
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.rot-right': `
Right rotation
x              y
/ \            / \
y   c   =>     a   x
/ \                / \
a   b              b   c
x: the pivot node
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.size': `
Returns the number of elements
Returns sz: the element count
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.successor': `
Finds the in-order successor of a node
idx: the node index
Returns succ: the successor index, or -1 when there is none
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.update-ht': `
Recomputes the node height
idx: the node index
`
  'tree-map.tree-map.upper-bound': `
Finds the first key > target
target: the target key
Returns key: ok = found, nil = no such key
`
  'tree-set': `
tree-set — ordered set (based on an AVL self-balancing binary search tree)

Ordered set of i64, implemented with an AVL tree.
All operations are O(log n); elements are traversed in ascending order.

Differences from hash-set:
- tree-set sorts by value (ascending), hash-set is unordered
- tree-set supports lower-bound/upper-bound ordered queries
- tree-set uses an AVL tree, hash-set uses a hash table
- tree-set's contains/add/remove return bool option

usage:
s = tree-set{}
s.init()
ok = s.add(3)             // ok=true (new)
ok = s.add(1)             // ok=true
ok = s.add(2)             // ok=true
ok = s.add(1)             // ok=false (already exists)
yes = s.contains(2)       // yes=true
val = s.first()           // val = ok (minimum)
val = s.last()            // val = ok (maximum)
val = s.lower-bound(2)    // val = ok (first ≥ 2)
val = s.upper-bound(2)    // val = ok (first > 2)
ok = s.remove(2)          // ok=true
s.for-each(v)             // iterate all elements in ascending order

// option unpacking
val = s.lower-bound(42)
val: {
nil -> print('not found')
-> print(it)
}
`
  'tree-set.tree-set': `
tree-set: AVL ordered set
root: root node index, -1=empty tree
free-head: free node pool head index, -1=pool full
size: current number of elements
keys: key storage
left/right/parent: tree structure indices, -1=NIL
height: node height (leaf=1, NIL=0)
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.add': `
Adds an element
key: the value to add
Returns ok: true = inserted, false = already present or the pool is full
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.alloc-node': `
Allocates a node from the free pool
Returns idx: the node index, or -1 when the pool is full
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.bf': `
Returns the balance factor (left height minus right height)
idx: the node index
Returns b: the balance factor
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.clear': 'Removes every element and rebuilds the free pool'
  'tree-set.tree-set.contains': `
Reports whether an element exists
key: the value to check
Returns yes: true = present, false = absent
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.empty': `
Reports whether the set is empty
Returns yes: true = empty, false = not empty
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.first': `
Returns the smallest value
Returns val: ok = the smallest value, nil = the set is empty
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.for-each': `
Iterates over every element in ascending order
val: the output value (updated per element)
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.free-node': `
Returns a node to the free pool
idx: the index of the node to release
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.full': `
Reports whether the node pool is full
Returns yes: true = full, false = not full
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.ht': `
Returns the node height (NIL = 0)
idx: the node index
Returns h: the height
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.init': 'Initialises an empty tree and its free node pool'
  'tree-set.tree-set.last': `
Returns the largest value
Returns val: ok = the largest value, nil = the set is empty
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.lower-bound': `
Finds the first element >= target
target: the target value
Returns val: ok = found, nil = no such element
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.rebalance': `
Checks and repairs the balance at node idx
idx: the node index
Returns nr: the subtree root index after rebalancing
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.rebalance-up': `
Rebalances level by level upwards from idx
idx: the starting node index
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.remove': `
Removes an element
key: the value to remove
Returns ok: true = removed, false = not found
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.rot-left': `
Left rotation
x                y
/ \              / \
a   y     =>     x   c
/ \          / \
b   c        a   b
x: the pivot node
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.rot-right': `
Right rotation
x              y
/ \            / \
y   c   =>     a   x
/ \                / \
a   b              b   c
x: the pivot node
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.size': `
Returns the number of elements
Returns sz: the element count
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.successor': `
Finds the in-order successor of a node
idx: the node index
Returns succ: the successor index, or -1 when there is none
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.update-ht': `
Recomputes the node height
idx: the node index
`
  'tree-set.tree-set.upper-bound': `
Finds the first element > target
target: the target value
Returns val: ok = found, nil = no such element
`
  'txt': `
txt — fixed-length text type

txt is a fixed 256-byte text type:
- first 255 bytes store data (data [255]byte)
- last 1 byte stores the length (len byte, range 0-255, unit is "byte")
- the whole struct is fixed at 256 bytes, no heap allocation needed

length semantics (aligned with str):
- t.len()        returns "character (code point) count", equivalent to t.count()
- t.len-bytes()  returns "byte count" (actual length of the underlying UTF-8 buffer)
- internal len field is still byte length; byte operations such as indexing/slicing/writing use len-bytes()
- ASCII content makes all three equal; with multibyte UTF-8, len() < len-bytes()

index semantics (by code point, aligned with str read behavior):
- txt[i] read: returns the char at code point index i (UTF-8 decode); out-of-range is a **checked** read, returning
err('index out of range') (after idxhndld consolidation there is no more -1 sentinel; str's s[i] also returns err)
- txt[i] = v write: **globally disabled** (checker idxwrite, 2026-11-12). Old writes would
re-UTF-8-encode v and shift trailing bytes, implicitly appending when i == code point count, silently discarding the part beyond 255
— the length being silently changed by a write is exactly the reason for this ban. To change content use place-match
(`t[i]: { ok -> it = "x" }`, gate returning nil skips automatically), to change length use .push(c) /
.push-str(s) / .push-txt(t) (str-side s[i] = v is also disabled, semantics consistent on both sides)
- byte-level raw read/write uses the paired methods .byte(i) / ._set-byte(i, b) (for buffer-building code only)

usage (type annotation required):
t txt = 'hello'
n = t.len()          ; character count
b = t.len-bytes()    ; byte count
c = t[0]             ; first code point (char)
b = t.byte(0)        ; first byte (byte)
difference from str:
- str is a heap-allocated variable-length string
- txt is a stack-allocated fixed-size string (256 bytes)
- txt needs no heap allocation, suitable for short text scenarios
- txt requires a type annotation (t txt = 'abc')
`
  'txt._from-bytes': `
clamping of n: negative -> 0; above b.len() -> b.len(); then truncated to txt's hard limit of 255 bytes.
no UTF-8 validation (pure byte copying); may cut through the middle of a multibyte character — caller's responsibility.
b: source bytes
n: number of bytes requested to copy (clamped to [0, min(b.len(), 255)])
out: copy of the first n bytes (len = the clamped n; txt has no cap concept)
`
  'txt._set-byte': `
Returns ?bool (fallible): on out-of-bounds (i < 0 or i >= 255 physical capacity) returns nil and **skips the write**,
returns some(true) on success. The bound is based on "physical capacity" (txt is a fixed 255-byte buffer), not
len, so builders that "fill the buffer then set len" (fill / from-str / to-hex / join) keep working.
Discarding the ?bool with a bare statement at the call site is legal (optunuse only targets bound names).
i: byte index
b: byte value to write
ok: ok=true=write succeeded, nil=i out of bounds (write skipped)
`
  'txt._txt-dig-val': `
converts a single digit character (0-15) to its value; an invalid character returns -1
c: the code point of a digit character
d: 0~15; invalid characters return -1
`
  'txt.at': `
[deprecated] txt.at: character (code point) index read — use t[idx] instead (native indexing, returns ?char)
txt.at: gets the code point value at a character position (for a byte offset use t.byte(i))
returns b: nil when out of range; a code point beyond the byte range is truncated
idx: code point (character) index
b: ok=the code point value (truncated when it exceeds the byte range), nil=out of range
`
  'txt.byte': `
returns the raw byte value at BYTE offset i (a raw byte read)
Different from txt[i]'s code point indexing; for code that builds the buffer byte by byte
Returns ?byte: nil when out of range (i < 0 or i >= len-bytes()), and the caller must handle the option
Handled by the compiler as a builtin (#{buildin}): codegen intercepts it and expands a bounds check plus GEP+load, with
no std function body; the signature is registered in src/builtin/str.go's BuiltinMethodList
(Intercepted, sharing one entry with str.byte through the bare-name fallback)
i: byte index (not a code point)
c: ok=the raw byte at that position, nil=i out of range
`
  'txt.clear': 'empties the text (len=0)'
  'txt.compare': `
Lexicographic comparison
returns -1 (a<b), 0 (equal), 1 (a>b)
b: comparison target txt
r: lexicographic -1 (receiver<b) / 0 (equal) / 1 (>)
`
  'txt.contains': `
whether it contains the substring
sub: the sub-txt to look for
yes: whether it contains sub
`
  'txt.copy': `
copies a txt
⚠️ DEPRECATED: assignment `a = b` already has copy semantics — txt is a value type (256
byte inline buffer, data[255] + len); assignment is a byte-by-byte copy. Switch to direct assignment; kept only for compatibility.
dst: the copy (deprecated: txt is a value type, assignment already copies byte by byte)
`
  'txt.count': `
counts the code points
cnt: number of code points (chars)
`
  'txt.decode-cp': `
decodes one UTF-8 code point starting at position i
returns cp = the code point value, clen = the sequence length in bytes (1-4)
i: the byte index to start decoding at
cp: the decoded code point value
clen: byte length of this code point's UTF-8 sequence (1~4)
`
  'txt.empty': `
Determine whether it is empty.
yes: whether the actual length len is 0
`
  'txt.ends-with': `
Whether it ends with the given suffix
sub: the suffix sub-txt
yes: whether it ends with sub
`
  'txt.eq': `
Compare whether two txt are equal
⚠️ Rewritten 2026-09-21. The old implementation used a single `->` pipeline (`.byte(k) - b.byte(k); c != 0 -> ok = 0;
k == an - 1 -> ok == 0 -> eq = false; k = k + 1`) only backtracks on the **last byte**,
so `t('hello').eq('world')` — "same length, different content" — always returned true
—— the `neq:` line in `tests/txt.no` kept printing 1, and it was frozen into the golden baseline.
txt.compare already compares byte by byte and branches correctly when lengths differ, so reuse it directly.
b: the target txt to compare against
eq: true=both sides byte-identical
`
  'txt.eq-full': `
exact equality of two txt values (length plus byte-by-byte)
b: comparison target txt
yes: whether the length and every byte are all equal
`
  'txt.fields': `
splits on whitespace into []txt (consecutive whitespace counts as a single separator)
out: slice of non-empty child txts split on runs of whitespace
`
  'txt.fill': `
Fill the entire data area with a byte value
val: the byte value used to fill the entire data area
`
  'txt.from-bytes': `
Create a txt from []byte (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncating if the []byte exceeds 255 bytes
b: the source byte
out: content copied into the txt (parts beyond 255 bytes are truncated)
`
  'txt.from-chars': `
builds a new txt from a char slice (written code point by code point, UTF-8 encoded; anything past 255 bytes is dropped)
⚠️ you cannot write code points one at a time with `val[i]: { ok -> it = cs[i] }`: D9's place-match gates on a
READ, and reading `val[0]` on a fresh txt with len=0 goes out of range and returns nil, so the ok arm never runs and
the whole function returns an empty string (txt.from-chars silently lost data because of this).
txt.new (ForwardFunc: chars-new) exists precisely for this: it writes all code points at once and sets the byte length.
cs: the code point slice ([]char)
t: ok=a new txt encoded per code point in UTF-8, nil=the encoded result exceeds 255 bytes (the excess discarded)
`
  'txt.from-hex': `
decodes hexadecimal text into a txt
returns Option: ok(txt) / nil (empty) / err('msg')
out: ok=the decoded txt, nil=empty, err=illegal hex characters or odd length
`
  'txt.from-str': `
Create a txt from str (truncated to 255 bytes)
returns a txt, truncating if the str exceeds 255 bytes
s: the source str
out: content copied into the txt (parts beyond 255 bytes are truncated)
`
  'txt.index': `
finds the position of a substring (starting from 0)
sub: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.index-from': `
searches for a substring starting at from
Returns the position of the first occurrence, or -1 when absent
target: the sub-txt to look for
from: the start code point position (inclusive)
pos: code point position of the first occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.join': `
[]txt.join: joins a txt slice with a separator (truncating past 255 bytes)
sep: the separator
returns out: the joined result
`
  'txt.last-index': `
an alias of rindex
target: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the last occurrence, -1 when not found (alias of rindex)
`
  'txt.len': `
returns the character (code point) count (aligned with str.len; for byte length use txt.len-bytes())
n: number of code points (characters), aligned with str.len (use len-bytes() for byte length)
`
  'txt.lines': `
splits on newlines into []txt (supports \n and \r\n)
out: slice of lines split on newlines (handles \n and \r\n; a trailing newline yields no empty line)
`
  'txt.new': `
txt.new: build a txt from a char slice (module-level constructor, returns a concrete txt)
compile-time builtin (ForwardFunc: chars-new): []char is converted byte by byte into bytes with the length written once,
No byte-by-byte .no loop. txt is a builtin type whose bytes/length cannot be manipulated in user code, so the whole conversion is emitted by the compiler as IR.
anything beyond 255 bytes is dropped automatically (the length byte is clamped to 255).
cs: the code point slice ([]char)
t: the txt after byte-by-byte re-encoding (parts beyond 255 bytes dropped automatically)
`
  'txt.push': `
appends one code point (UTF-8 encoded; dropped entirely when the whole character does not fit under the 255 limit)
all-or-nothing: a multi-byte code point is either written whole or not at all — this
matches what the old `t[i] = v` did at i == code point count, and avoids cutting a UTF-8
sequence in half (that is .push-str's semantics; the two differ on the boundary case).
c: the code point to append
`
  'txt.push-str': `
appends a str (byte copy, does not exceed the 255 limit; truncates BY BYTES when it would)
⚠️ truncation can cut a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence in half; use .push(c char) when you want
"drop the whole character if it does not fit".
s: the str to append (truncated when it exceeds the 255 limit)
`
  'txt.push-txt': `
appends a txt (byte copy, does not exceed the 255 limit; truncates by bytes when it would)
t: the txt to append (truncated when it exceeds the 255 limit)
`
  'txt.repeat': `
repeats the content times times (capped at 255 bytes)
returns a new txt
times: repetition count
val: a new txt after concatenating times copies (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-char': `
replaces every old-ch with new-ch (UTF-8 code points)
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
old-ch: the code point that was replaced
new-ch: the code point after replacement
val: a new txt after per-codepoint replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-n': `
replacement with a bounded count
old txt, new, n: the maximum number of replacements (n<=0 replaces nothing)
old: the child txt that was replaced
new: the child txt after replacement
n: maximum replacements (n <= 0 means no replacement)
val: a new txt after limited-count replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.replace-str': `
replaces every occurrence of the substring old with new
returns a new txt (truncated past 255 bytes)
old: the child txt that was replaced
new: the child txt after replacement
val: a new txt after replacement (truncated beyond 255 bytes)
`
  'txt.reverse': `
Reverse the text content
out: a new txt with the bytes reversed
`
  'txt.rindex': `
reverse search for the last occurrence of a substring
returns the position, or -1 when absent
target: the sub-txt to look for
pos: code point position of the last occurrence, -1 when not found
`
  'txt.rindex-from': `
reverse search for a substring starting at from (returns the last occurrence at position >= from)
target: the sub-txt to look for
from: the reverse-search lower bound (inclusive, code points)
pos: code point position of the last occurrence (>= from), -1 when not found
`
  'txt.slice': `
takes the substring [start, end) and returns a new txt
start: starting code point index (inclusive)
end: the end code point index (exclusive)
out: a new txt cut by the code point range [start,end)
`
  'txt.split': `
splits on a separator into []txt
sep: the separator txt
out: slice of the split child txts
`
  'txt.split-n': `
splitting with a bounded count
sep txt, maxsplit: the maximum number of splits (<=0 means unlimited)
sep: the separator txt
maxsplit: maximum number of splits (<= 0 means unlimited)
out: slice of split results (at most maxsplit+1 parts)
`
  'txt.starts-with': `
Whether it starts with the given prefix
sub: the prefix sub-txt
yes: whether it starts with sub
`
  'txt.to-bigint': `
parses the text into a bigint, detecting the base automatically
Prefixes 0b/0B -> binary, 0o/0O -> octal, 0x/0X -> hexadecimal; no prefix -> decimal
Accepts optional leading whitespace and a sign (+ / -)
returns ?bigint: ok(bigint) on success / nil for an empty string / err('msg') when there are no digits
out: ok=a bigint parsed with the prefix radix (0b/0o/0x or decimal), nil=empty, err=no valid digits
`
  'txt.to-bool': `
parses "true"/"false" into a bool
val: ok=true/false (only "true"/"false" recognized), nil=empty, err=anything else
`
  'txt.to-byte': `
parses into a byte (equivalent to u8)
val: ok=a byte in range (0~255), nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-bytes': `
Convert a txt to []byte
out: copy of the first len bytes ([]byte)
`
  'txt.to-f32': `
parses the text into an f32
val: ok=a single-precision f32, nil=empty, err=contains non-digit characters
`
  'txt.to-f64': `
parses the text into an f64 (returns ?f64; empty string -> nil, non-numeric -> err)
Reuses str's parsing (convert to str first, then call str.to-f64)
val: ok=the parsed f64, nil=empty, err=contains non-digit characters
`
  'txt.to-hex': `
Convert a txt to a hex string
out: str with each byte turned into two lower-case hex characters
`
  'txt.to-i16': `
parses into an i16 (range -32768 to 32767)
val: ok=an i16 in range (-32768~32767), nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-i32': `
parses into an i32 (range -2147483648 to 2147483647)
val: ok=an i32 within range, nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-i64': `
parses the text into a signed 64-bit integer
returns Option: ok(n) / nil (empty) / err('msg')
val: ok=the parsed i64, nil=empty, err=non-digit characters / lone +/- / out of range
`
  'txt.to-i8': `
parses into an i8 (range -128 to 127)
val: ok=an i8 within range (-128~127), nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-lower': `
converts the text to lower case (ASCII)
returns a new txt
val: a new txt with ASCII upper converted to lower (other bytes kept as-is)
`
  'txt.to-str': `
Convert a txt to str
out: a str with the same content (a fresh heap buffer sized to the actual byte length)
`
  'txt.to-u16': `
parses into a u16 (range 0 to 65535)
val: ok=a u16 in range (0~65535), nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-u32': `
parses into a u32 (range 0 to 4294967295)
val: ok=a u32 within range, nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-u64': `
parses into a u64 (rejects a minus sign)
val: ok=a non-negative integer (rejects a sign), nil=empty, err=contains non-digit characters
`
  'txt.to-u8': `
parses into a u8 (range 0 to 255)
val: ok=a u8 in range (0~255), nil=empty, err=illegal or out of range
`
  'txt.to-upper': `
converts the text to upper case (ASCII)
returns a new txt
val: a new txt with ASCII lower converted to upper (other bytes kept as-is)
`
  'txt.trim': `
strips leading and trailing whitespace
returns a new txt
val: a new txt with leading and trailing whitespace removed
`
  'txt.trim-char': `
strips the given character (UTF-8 code point) from both ends
returns a new txt
ch: the code point to strip from both ends
val: a new txt with leading and trailing ch removed
`
  'txt.truncate': `
Truncate to the given length
n: byte length to keep after truncation (takes effect when n is smaller than the current length)
`
  'txt.txt-hex-val': `
converts a single hexadecimal character to its value (an invalid character returns -1)
c: the code point of a hex character
v: 0~15; invalid characters return -1
`
  'unix': `
unix — Unix Domain Socket support

Provides server-side listening and client-side connection for Unix Domain Socket (AF_UNIX).
The underlying layer uses the POSIX socket API (socket/unlink/bind/listen/accept/connect).
Data send/receive reuses built-in functions from net.no (net-accept, net-send, net-recv, close).

usage:
// server
l = unix-listen-on('/tmp/myapp.sock')       // returns ?unix-listener
l: {
nil -> print('listen failed')
-> c = l.accept()                        // returns ?unix-conn
}
n = c.recv(buf, 1024)
n = c.send('hello')
c.close()
l.close()

// client
c = unix-dial-to('/tmp/myapp.sock')          // returns ?unix-conn
c: {
nil -> print('connect failed')
-> n = c.send('hello')
}
c.close()
`
  'unix.unix-backlog': 'listen backlog length'
  'unix.unix-buf-size': 'default buffer size'
  'unix.unix-conn': `
unix-conn: Unix Domain Socket connection
fd: file descriptor of the connection socket (-1=not connected)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.close': 'Close the connection'
  'unix.unix-conn.dial': `
Connect to a Unix domain socket server
path: socket file path (e.g. '/tmp/myapp.sock')
ok: true=connected, false=syscall failed (fd stays -1)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.fd-of': `
Get the connection socket's file descriptor
fd: file descriptor of the connected socket (-1 when not connected)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.recv': `
Receive data into a buffer
buf: receive buffer
n: maximum bytes to receive
returns read-n: bytes actually received (-1 = error, 0 = connection closed)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.recv-all': `
Receive all data until the connection closes
content: all data accumulated so far (concatenated chunk by chunk until the peer closes or an error occurs)
total: total number of bytes received (0 when not connected)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.recv-with-timeout': `
Receive with a timeout
buf: receive buffer
n: maximum bytes to receive
timeout-ms: timeout in milliseconds
returns read-n: bytes actually received (-1 = error, 0 = timeout or connection closed)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.send': `
Send string data
data: string to send
returns written: bytes actually sent (-1 = error)
`
  'unix.unix-conn.set-recv-timeout': `
Set the receive timeout
timeout-ms: timeout in milliseconds (0 = block, >0 = timeout)
returns ok: true = success
`
  'unix.unix-conn.set-timeout': `
Set the receive timeout (alias, same semantics as set-recv-timeout)
timeout-ms: timeout in milliseconds
returns ok: true = success
`
  'unix.unix-dial': `
Connect to a Unix domain socket
Performs socket(AF_UNIX) + connect
path: target socket file path
fd: connected descriptor; -1 on failure
`
  'unix.unix-dial-timeout': `
Dial a Unix domain socket with a timeout
path: socket file path
timeout-ms: receive timeout after connecting
returns c: ok = connection, nil = failure
`
  'unix.unix-dial-to': `
Create a Unix domain socket connection and dial
path: socket file path
returns c: ok = connection, nil = failure
`
  'unix.unix-listen': `
Start listening on a Unix domain socket
Performs unlink + socket(AF_UNIX) + bind + listen (unlink first so an old path can be reused)
path: socket file path; length limited by UNIX-MAX-PATH
fd: descriptor in listening state; -1 on failure
`
  'unix.unix-listen-on': `
Create a Unix domain socket listener and start listening
path: socket file path
returns l: ok = listener, nil = failure
`
  'unix.unix-listener': `
unix-listener: Unix Domain Socket listener
fd: file descriptor of the listening socket (-1=not opened)
path: socket file path
`
  'unix.unix-listener.accept': `
Accept a new Unix domain socket connection
returns c: ok = connection struct, nil = failure
`
  'unix.unix-listener.accept-with-timeout': `
Accept with a timeout
timeout-ms: timeout in milliseconds
returns c: ok = connection, nil = timeout or failure
`
  'unix.unix-listener.close': 'Close the listening socket (does not delete the socket file automatically)'
  'unix.unix-listener.fd-of': `
Get the listening socket's file descriptor
fd: file descriptor of the listening socket (-1 when not open)
`
  'unix.unix-listener.listen': `
Start listening on a Unix domain socket
path: socket file path (e.g. '/tmp/myapp.sock')
ok: true=socket created and bound for listening, false=syscall failed (fd stays -1)
`
  'unix.unix-listener.path-of': `
Get the socket file path
path: socket file path passed in when the listener was created
`
  'unix.unix-listener.set-timeout': `
Set the accept timeout
timeout-ms: timeout in milliseconds
returns ok: true = success
`
  'unix.unix-max-path': 'maximum Unix Domain Socket path length'
  'url': `
url — URL parsing and construction

Provides URL parsing, construction, encoding, and decoding.
Supports the scheme://host:port/path?query#fragment format.

usage:
u = url-parse('https://example.com:8080/api/v1?key=value#section')
u.ok -> {
print(u.scheme)    // 'https'
print(u.host)      // 'example.com'
print(u.port)      // 8080
print(u.path)      // '/api/v1'
print(u.query)     // 'key=value'
print(u.fragment)  // 'section'
`
  'url.url-basic-auth': `
Produce an HTTP Basic authorization header value
user: username
password: password
returns out: header value (e.g. 'Basic dXNlcjpwYXNz')
`
  'url.url-build-query': `
Build a query string from key/value pairs
keys: key array (first count entries valid)
values: value array (one-to-one with keys)
count: number of valid key/value pairs
returns out: query string (e.g. key1=val1&key2=val2)
`
  'url.url-decode': `
URL decode (percent decoding)
s: percent-encoded string to decode
returns out: the decoded raw string
`
  'url.url-encode': `
URL encode (percent encoding)
s: raw string to encode
returns out: percent-encoded string
`
  'url.url-encode-component': `
URL encode (component mode, also encoding / and ?)
s: raw string to encode
returns out: percent-encoded string
`
  'url.url-info': `
url-info: URL parse result
ok: whether parsing succeeded
scheme: protocol (e.g. http, https)
host: hostname
port: port number (-1=unspecified)
path: path (e.g. /api/v1)
query: query string (excluding ?)
fragment: fragment (excluding #)
user: username (e.g. user in user:pass@host)
password: password
`
  'url.url-info.get-query': `
Get a query parameter value
key: query parameter name to read
returns val: ok = parameter value, nil = not found
`
  'url.url-info.set-query': `
Set a query parameter value
key: query parameter name to set
value: new value for the parameter
`
  'url.url-info.to-str': `
Reassemble the URL into a string
returns out: the assembled full URL
`
  'url.url-join': `
Join a base URL and a path
base: base URL
path-str: path to append at the end
returns out: the joined full URL
`
  'url.url-parse': `
Parse a URL string
url-str: the full URL string to parse
returns u: parse result (u.ok = true on success)
`
  'url.url-parse-basic-auth': `
Parse an HTTP Basic authorization header
header: Authorization header value (e.g. 'Basic dXNlcjpwYXNz')
returns user: username (empty = parse failed)
returns password: password
`
  'uuid': `
uuid — universally unique identifier (RFC 4122)

Provides UUID v4 (random) generation, parsing, formatting, and validation.
A UUID is a 128-bit (16-byte) identifier.
`
  'uuid.cmp': `
Compares two UUIDs lexicographically
b: the other UUID to compare
r: lexicographic comparison result (-1=self<b, 0=equal, 1=self>b; byte by byte to the first difference)
`
  'uuid.eq': `
Reports whether two UUIDs are equal
b: the other UUID to compare
yes: true=the two UUIDs' 16 bytes are equal one by one
`
  'uuid.from-str': `
Parses a UUID from a string
Accepts both the hyphenated (36-character) and the plain (32-character) form
s: the UUID string
out: the resulting UUID
Returns ok: true = parsed successfully
s: UUID string (36 characters with hyphens or 32 without)
out: output parameter, writes 16 bytes when parsing succeeds (original content overwritten)
ok: true=format valid and written, false=illegal length/characters
`
  'uuid.hex': 'lowercase hex character table'
  'uuid.hex-byte': `
Writes one byte as two hex characters at position pos of out
b: byte to encode (high nibble written first, low nibble second)
pos: offset in out where the first hex character is written (the second character is written at pos+1)
out: target string, two cells written in place (an out-of-range cell write is skipped by safe indexing)
`
  'uuid.hex-byte-upper': `
Writes a byte as uppercase hex
b: the byte to convert into two upper-case hex characters
pos: starting byte position written into out
out: output parameter, writes two hex characters in place (out-of-range writes ignored by safe indexing)
table: upper-case hex digit table (e.g. '0123...ABCDEF')
`
  'uuid.hex-val': `
Converts a single hex character to its value
c: the code point of a single hex character
v: the corresponding 0~15 value (non-hex characters give 0)
`
  'uuid.is-nil': `
Reports whether this is the nil UUID (all zeros)
yes: whether it is the nil UUID (all 16 bytes zero)
`
  'uuid.new-v4': `
Generates a random UUID v4
state: the xorshift32 state (input/output; seed it with a non-zero value)
out: generated v4 random UUID (state advances with xorshift32)
`
  'uuid.nil-uuid': `
Builds the nil UUID (all zeros)
out: output parameter, filled as an empty UUID of 16 all-zero bytes
`
  'uuid.parse-hex-byte': `
Parses two hex characters from a string into one byte
s: the source string containing hex characters
pos: index of the first hex character to read (takes the pair s[pos], s[pos+1])
v: the synthesized byte value 0~255 (illegal characters counted as 0)
`
  'uuid.parse-no-dashes': `
Parses a UUID without hyphens
s: the complete UUID string to parse
pos: start index of this 32-character segment within s
out: output parameter, writes the parsed 16 bytes on success
ok: true=this section parsed successfully (all hex characters)
`
  'uuid.parse-with-dashes': `
Parses a hyphenated UUID
s: the complete UUID string to parse
pos: start index of this 36-character segment within s
out: output parameter, writes the parsed 16 bytes on success
ok: true=this section parsed successfully (hyphen positions and hex characters all valid)
`
  'uuid.to-str': `
Formats a UUID as the standard string
Format: xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx (lowercase)
Returns out: the UUID string
`
  'uuid.to-str-upper': `
Formats a UUID as an uppercase string
Returns out: the uppercase UUID string
`
  'uuid.uuid': 'uuid: 128-bit universally unique identifier'
  'uuid.validate': `
Validates the UUID format
ok: true=variant bits valid (top 2 bits of byte8 are 10b, the RFC 4122 variant); the version number is read informationally only and not enforced to 4
`
  'uuid.variant': `
Returns the UUID variant
v: the variant identifier (the high 2 bits of byte8, 2 per RFC 4122)
`
  'uuid.variant-rfc4122': 'RFC 4122 variant'
  'uuid.version': `
Returns the UUID version
v: the UUID version number (the high 4 bits of byte6, 4 for v4)
`
  'uuid.version-4': 'UUID version 4 (random)'
  'vec': `
vec — slice operations

creation, cloning, and comparison of dynamic slices.

a slice is a view, not a distinct type: vec's methods apply to the following slice views:
- arr slice (a[1..3] -> []t view, sharing arr's underlying memory)
- vec slice (v[1..3] -> []t view, sharing vec's underlying memory)
a slice does not copy data; it only records pointer + length + capacity, pointing at the original buffer.
modifying elements through a slice affects the original data, and vice versa.

usage:
use std/vec.vec-create
data [16]i64
data = vec-create(10, 42)

vec-slice — overview of slice view syntax
the following forms all produce []t views (sharing the underlying buffer, no copy):
full range, start only, end only, or both ends, each with four open/closed combinations;
endpoints may be omitted as unbounded (..). The view's own len() is the range length.
[]t[..]
[]t[..)
[]t(..]
[]t(..)

[]t[n0..]
[]t[n0..)
[]t(n0..]
[]t(n0..)

[]t[..n1]
[]t[..n1)
[]t(..n1]
[]t(..n1)

[]t[n0..n1]
[]t[n0..n1)
[]t(n0..n1]
[]t(n0..n1)
`
  'vec.at': `
[deprecated] []t.at: safe indexed access; nil when out of range — use v[i] instead (native indexing, returns ?t; out of range yields err)
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.clone': '[]t.clone: copies the slice'
  'vec.contains': `
[]t.contains: reports whether the value val is present
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz, or the compiler emits a wrong loop condition i < 0 and the loop never runs
`
  'vec.eq': `
[]t.eq: compares two slices for equality (element-wise; capacity and origin are not compared)
b: the other slice; returns false immediately when the lengths differ
eq: true=length and all corresponding elements are equal (elements compared with !=)
`
  'vec.extend': `
[]t.extend: appends every element of another slice
Note: the push logic is inlined to work around a pre-existing LLVM bug in calling .push() from inside a method.
The caller must ensure there is enough capacity.
`
  'vec.fill': '[]t.fill: fills with val'
  'vec.first': `
[]t.first: the first element; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.index-of': `
[]t.index-of: returns the index of a value, or -1
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.insert': `
[]t.insert: inserts val at position i
i >= .len() behaves like push
Note: the push logic is inlined (.len = cur + 1; .[cur] = val) to work around a pre-existing LLVM bug
(_LB__RB_t.push undefined) triggered by calling .push() inside a method. The caller must ensure there is enough capacity.
`
  'vec.last': `
[]t.last: the last element; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.len': `
[]t.len: returns the number of slice elements (regardless of element type, unrelated to byte count)
out: current length, in the range 0..cap
`
  'vec.max': `
[]t.max: the maximum value; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.min': `
[]t.min: the minimum value; an empty slice returns nil
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.pop': `
[]t.pop: pops the element at the end
An empty slice returns nil
`
  'vec.remove': `
[]t.remove: removes the element at position i and returns it
Out of range returns nil
`
  'vec.reverse': '[]t.reverse: reverses in place'
  'vec.sum': `
[]t.sum: the sum
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
Note: the range-for bound must use len (the .len field), not the local sz
`
  'vec.swap': `
[]t.swap: swaps the elements at two positions
Note: sz = .len() forces the vec self type, so it is not specialised into an arr method by mistake
`
  'vec.to-arr': '[]t.to-arr: a slice method that converts to an [n]t array'
  'vec.to-str': `
[]t.to-str: the string form
Formatted like '[1, 2, 3]'
Elements are stringified by calling each one's own .to-str() (see number.no for the concrete types)
`
  'vec.truncate': '[]t.truncate: truncates to cnt elements; cnt < 0 is treated as 0, and cnt >= .len does nothing'
  'vec.vec': `
Memory layout is determined by the LLVM backend's `%vec` type, 16 bytes:
offset 0  u32 len
offset 4  u32 cap
offset 8  i64 data   - element pointer encoded via ptrtoint
The public API (.len()) always returns i64; u32 exists only inside the descriptor; cap exposes no getter.
cap == 0 is the "borrowed view" marker: it does not own the buffer, so @vec_free / @vec_retain are skipped;
cap > 0 means it owns the heap block pointed to by data.
`
  'vec.vec-clear': `
[]t.clear: clears the slice (in-place length set to 0) — builtin, body expanded by codegen
Does not free the heap and does not shrink the capacity, so the data pointer is unchanged
Only a write with new content of non-zero length goes through the growth logic
No parameters and no return value: clearing is an in-place modification of the receiver itself
`
  'vec.vec-push': `
[]t.push: appends an element at the end (grows automatically) — builtin, body expanded by codegen
Growth policy:
cap == 0 -> new-cap = 4
cap < 1024 -> new-cap = cap * 2
cap >= 1024 -> new-cap = cap * 5 / 4
val: the element to append; the type is determined by the receiver slice's element type
after appending, .len grows by 1; on growth existing elements move to the new buffer and the old one is freed
`
  'ws': `
ws — WebSocket client and server

Supports the RFC 6455 WebSocket protocol, providing server listening and client connections.
Under the hood uses the HTTP/1.1 upgrade handshake, supporting plaintext WS and encrypted WSS (TLS 1.2).

usage (server):
srv = ws.listen-on('0.0.0.0', 8080)   // returns ?server
srv: {
nil -> return
->
! {
c = srv.accept()             // returns ?server-conn
c: {
nil -> break
->
msg = c.recv()        // returns ?message
msg: {
nil -> break
err -> break
-> c.send-text('echo: ' - msg.data)
c.close()
}
}
}
}

usage (client WS):
c = ws.connect('ws://127.0.0.1:8080/path')   // returns ?client
c: {
nil -> print('connect failed')
->
c.send-text('hello')
msg = c.recv()
msg: {
nil -> print('closed')
err -> print('error')
-> print(msg.data)
}
c.close()
}

usage (client WSS):
c = ws.connect('wss://echo.websocket.org/path')   // returns ?client
c: {
nil -> print('connect failed')
->
c.send-text('hello over TLS')
msg = c.recv()
msg: {
nil -> print('closed')
err -> print('error')
-> print(msg.data)
}
c.close()
}
`
  'ws.build-frame': `
Assemble a WebSocket frame
opcode: message type
data: message content
mask: whether to mask (clients must be true, servers must be false)
mask-key: 4-byte mask key (used when mask=true)
returns frame: the complete frame string
`
  'ws.client': `
client: WebSocket client connection
fd: TCP socket file descriptor (-1=not connected)
use-tls: whether to use TLS (WSS)
tls-c: TLS connection (used when use-tls=true)
connected: connection state
host: server hostname
port: port number
path: request path
recv-buf: receive buffer
recv-buf-len: actual data length in the buffer
send-counter: mask key pseudo-random counter
`
  'ws.client.close': 'Close the connection'
  'ws.client.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
returns yes: true = connected
`
  'ws.client.recv': `
Blocking read of one complete WebSocket message
The server does not mask, so no demask is needed (the mask bit is still checked).
returns msg:
ok = message
nil = connection closed
err = error
`
  'ws.client.send': `
Send a WebSocket frame (clients must mask)
opcode: message type
data: message content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.client.send-binary': `
Send a binary message
data: binary content (byte sequence)
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'ws.client.send-close': `
Send a close frame
close-code: close status code
reason: close reason
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.client.send-ping': `
Send a ping
data: ping payload (the peer echoes it back as pong)
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'ws.client.send-pong': `
Send a pong
data: pong payload (usually the received ping payload echoed back verbatim)
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'ws.client.send-text': `
Send a text message
data: text content
ok: true = sent successfully
`
  'ws.compute-accept': `
Compute the Sec-WebSocket-Accept value
key: the Sec-WebSocket-Key sent by the client
returns accept: base64(sha1(key - WS-GUID))
`
  'ws.connect': `
Connect to a WebSocket endpoint
url: WS/WSS endpoint URL (ws://host:port/path or wss://host:port/path)
returns c:
ok = connected client
nil = connection failed
err = error message
`
  'ws.gen-client-key': `
Generate a 16-byte Sec-WebSocket-Key and base64 encode it
seed: seed value
returns key: base64 string (24 characters including padding)
`
  'ws.gen-mask-key': `
Generate a 4-byte mask key string
seed: seed value
returns key: 4-character string
`
  'ws.listen-on': `
Create a WebSocket server and start listening
host: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0')
port: port to listen on
returns s: ok = server, nil = failure
`
  'ws.message': `
message: WebSocket message
opcode: 1=text, 2=binary, 8=close, 9=ping, 10=pong
data: message content
fin: whether this is the final fragment
`
  'ws.next-rand': `
Produce the next pseudorandom i64
Combines a global counter with a simple LCG
r: the new pseudorandom number (global counter + LCG output, not cryptographically secure)
`
  'ws.rand-counter': 'Global pseudo-random counter (used to generate the mask key and Sec-WebSocket-Key)'
  'ws.server': `
server: WebSocket server listener
fd: listening socket file descriptor (-1=not open)
host: bound IP address
port: listening port
`
  'ws.server-conn': `
server-conn: WebSocket server-side client connection
fd: client socket file descriptor (-1=not connected)
connected: connection state
recv-buf: receive buffer
recv-buf-len: actual data length in the buffer
`
  'ws.server-conn.close': 'Close the client connection'
  'ws.server-conn.handshake': `
Complete the WebSocket HTTP handshake
Reads the client HTTP upgrade request, validates the required headers, and sends the 101 response.
Sets .connected = true on success.
`
  'ws.server-conn.is-connected': `
Check the connection state
returns yes: true = connected
`
  'ws.server-conn.recv': `
Blocking read of one complete WebSocket message
Supports collecting fragmented messages (continuation opcode).
Automatically answers ping with pong; on close sets connected=false and returns nil.
returns msg:
ok = message
nil = connection closed
err = error
`
  'ws.server-conn.recv-nb': `
Note: to suit non-blocking polling, this method does not accumulate fragmentation across calls;
a single-frame text/binary message (the nowork frontend's usage) becomes one message directly.
Use the blocking recv when full fragment reassembly is required.
msg: ok=complete message assembled by this poll; nil=no data yet or the frame has not fully arrived; err=connection closed or error
`
  'ws.server-conn.send': `
Send a WebSocket frame (server does not mask)
opcode: message type (WS-OP-TEXT / WS-OP-BINARY / ...)
data: message content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-binary': `
Send a binary message
data: message content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-close': `
Send a close frame
close-code: close status code (e.g. 1000)
reason: close reason
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-ping': `
Send a ping
data: ping content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-pong': `
Send a pong
data: pong content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server-conn.send-text': `
Send a text message
data: message content
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.server.accept': `
Accept a new WebSocket client connection and complete the handshake
returns c: ok = handshaked client, nil = failure
`
  'ws.server.close': 'Close the WebSocket server'
  'ws.server.listen': `
Start listening
host: IP address to bind (e.g. '0.0.0.0')
port: port to listen on
returns ok: true = success
`
  'ws.ws-buf-size': 'Receive buffer size'
  'ws.ws-default-port': 'Default WS port'
  'ws.ws-default-tls-port': 'Default WSS port'
  'ws.ws-guid': 'RFC 6455 fixed GUID (used for Sec-WebSocket-Accept computation)'
  'ws.ws-op-cont': 'Opcode constants'
  'ws.ws-recv-size': 'single receive size'
  'x25519': `
x25519 — X25519 elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman key exchange (RFC 7748)

Implements Montgomery ladder scalar multiplication on Curve25519.
Uses a 10-limb representation (alternating 26/25 bits) for GF(2^255-19) field elements.

usage:
# std/crypto/x25519
// generate a key pair
priv [32]byte
pub [32]byte = x25519-scalarmult-base(priv)
// shared key
shared [32]byte = x25519-scalarmult(priv, peer-pub)
`
  'x25519.fe-add': `
Field element addition h = f + g
h: result summed limb by limb (no carry propagation; equivalence is guaranteed by later mul/serialization)
f: left addend
g: right addend
`
  'x25519.fe-add-v': `
Return a + b
a: left addend
b: right addend
h: newly created sum field element
`
  'x25519.fe-copy': `
Copy a field element
h: destination field element (filled in place with f's 10 limbs)
f: source field element
`
  'x25519.fe-cswap': `
Conditional swap (used by the Montgomery ladder)
mask: 0 = no swap, 1 = swap
a: first field element (participates in the swap in place)
b: second field element (participates in the swap in place)
mask: 0 = no swap, 1 = swap (expanded internally to an all-ones mask to stay constant-time)
`
  'x25519.fe-frombytes': `
Load a field element from 32 bytes (little-endian)
ref10 style: limbs are loaded unmasked via shifts, then carry propagation runs
Carry propagation uses the rounding constant (1<<(bits-1)), yielding a "half-normalized" form (limbs may be small negatives)
This form matches what fe-tobytes expects, guaranteeing a correct round-trip
h: field element after loading (10 limbs, half-normalized carries propagated)
s: 32-byte little-endian encoding (the top bit255 is masked off)
`
  'x25519.fe-frombytes-v': `
Return a field element loaded from 32 bytes (little-endian)
s: 32-byte little-endian encoding (bit255 ignored)
h: newly created field element
`
  'x25519.fe-invert': `
Compute a^(-1) mod p
Uses a ref10 addition chain to compute a^(p-2) = a^(2^255-21)
Addition chain fully aligned with libsodium fe25519_invert
out: field element to write the result into (10 limbs)
a: field element to invert (must not be zero)
`
  'x25519.fe-invert-v': `
Return a^(-1) mod p
a: field element to invert
h: newly created inverse (a^(p-2))
`
  'x25519.fe-mul': `
Field element multiplication h = f * g mod p
Uses ref10 cross-term expansion
Note: an array t[] stores intermediate results to avoid compiler optimization issues
h: product field element (f × g mod p)
f: factor (10 limbs)
g: factor (10 limbs)
`
  'x25519.fe-mul-v': `
Return a * b mod p
a: factor
b: factor
h: newly created product field element (a × b mod p)
`
  'x25519.fe-mul121665': `
Multiply a field element by the constant 121665 (= (A-2)/4, A=486662)
In the Montgomery ladder doubling formula z2 = E*(AA + a24*E), a24 must be 121665,
not 121666. 121666 makes ladder doubling wrong (all-zero/incorrect output for some scalars).
Stores intermediate results in an array to avoid compiler optimization issues
h: field element after multiplying by 121665
f: field element to multiply
`
  'x25519.fe-one': `
The one element
h: field element written as the identity (limb0=1, the rest 0)
`
  'x25519.fe-one-v': `
Return the one field element
h: newly created identity field element (length 10)
`
  'x25519.fe-sq': `
Field element squaring h = f^2 mod p
Delegates directly to fe-mul
h: square result (f² mod p)
f: field element to square
`
  'x25519.fe-sq-v': `
Return a^2 mod p
a: field element to square
h: newly created square result
`
  'x25519.fe-sub': `
Field element subtraction h = f - g
h: result subtracted limb by limb (limbs may be negative; later normalization converts them back to a positive representation)
f: minuend
g: subtrahend
`
  'x25519.fe-sub-v': `
Return a - b
a: minuend
b: subtrahend
h: newly created difference field element
`
  'x25519.fe-tobytes': `
Serialize a field element to 32 bytes (little-endian)
Carry propagation via floor division without rounding constants
Limbs are first normalized to [0, 2^bits-1], then a +19 test checks whether the value is >= p
s: 32-byte buffer receiving the serialized result
h: field element to serialize (normalized to [0,p) before output)
`
  'x25519.fe-tobytes-v': `
Serialize a field element to 32 bytes (little-endian) and return it
[32]byte is passed by value, so calling fe-tobytes(s, h) directly would not write back into the return slot;
hence the value is written to a local array first, then assigned wholesale to the return value s.
h: field element to serialize
s: newly created 32-byte little-endian encoding
`
  'x25519.fe-zero': `
The zero element
h: field element written as zero (all 10 limbs set to 0)
`
  'x25519.fe-zero-v': `
Return the zero field element
h: newly created zero field element (length 10)
`
  'x25519.mask-26': 'field element limb mask'
  'x25519.num-add': `
i64 addition
a: left operand
b: right operand
`
  'x25519.num-floor': `
floor(x / 2^bits), also correct for negative numbers
First add 2^40 to lift the dividend to non-negative, then shift right —— this way the result is the same whether >> is a logical or arithmetic right shift
(limb absolute values are far below 2^40), finally subtract (2^40 >> bits) back.
x: dividend
bits: shift amount (this file only uses 25 or 26)
`
  'x25519.num-sub': `
i64 subtraction
a: left operand
b: right operand
`
  'x25519.x25519-a24': 'Curve25519 constants'
  'x25519.x25519-gen-keypair': `
Generate a random key pair
seed: random seed
returns priv: private key
returns pub: public key
seed: rand seed (determines the private key; real scenarios must inject system entropy)
priv: private key (already clamped: bit0-2 cleared, bit254 set, bit255 cleared)
pub: corresponding public key (priv × basepoint)
`
  'x25519.x25519-get-basepoint': `
Return the basepoint u coordinate (9, 0, 0, ..., 0)
base: 32-byte little-endian encoding of the basepoint u=9
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult': `
X25519 scalar multiplication
scalar: scalar (private key)
point: point coordinate (public key)
returns out: the point-multiplication result (shared secret), little-endian field-element bytes
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-base': `
Compute scalar * basepoint
scalar: scalar (private key)
returns out: public key
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-base-bytes': `
Compute scalar * basepoint ([]byte version)
scalar: scalar (private key, length 32)
returns out: public key (length 32)
`
  'x25519.x25519-scalarmult-bytes': `
X25519 scalar multiplication ([]byte version)
scalar: scalar (private key, length 32)
point: point coordinate (public key, length 32)
returns out: result (length 32)
`
  'x509': `
x509 — X.509 certificate DER parsing utilities

Provides basic parsing of DER-encoded X.509 certificates:
- low-level DER TLV (Tag-Length-Value) parsing
- certificate structure navigation (TBSCertificate, serial number, public key)
- RSA public exponent extraction
- SHA-256 certificate fingerprint computation

usage:
cert []byte

// compute the certificate fingerprint
h0, h1, h2, h3, h4, h5, h6, h7 = x509-fingerprint(cert)

// extract the RSA public exponent
e = x509-rsa-e(cert)
`
  'x509.alg-id-rsa-encryption': `
AlgorithmIdentifier for rsaEncryption (1.2.840.113549.1.1.1)
returns out: SEQUENCE { OID rsaEncryption, NULL }
`
  'x509.alg-id-sha256-with-rsa': `
AlgorithmIdentifier for sha256WithRSAEncryption (1.2.840.113549.1.1.11)
returns out: SEQUENCE { OID sha256WithRSAEncryption, NULL }
`
  'x509.der-enc-bitstring': `
Wrap as a BIT STRING
data: byte data
returns out: DER BIT STRING
data: bit string content (byte aligned; the function prepends the unused-bits-count byte 0 automatically)
out: DER BIT STRING with tag=0x03 (content one byte longer than data)
`
  'x509.der-enc-explicit': `
EXPLICIT context tag [n]
tag-num: tag number (0, 1, 2, ...); the low 5 bits are combined with 0xA0 to form the tag
content: inner DER encoding to wrap in this context tag
returns out: DER [n] EXPLICIT (constructed tag + length + content)
`
  'x509.der-enc-integer-bytes': `
Encode a big-endian byte array as a DER INTEGER
data: big-endian bytes (positive integer)
returns out: DER INTEGER (with tag+length)
`
  'x509.der-enc-integer-i64': `
Encode an i64 as a DER INTEGER
val: integer value
returns out: DER INTEGER
`
  'x509.der-enc-len': `
Encode a DER length
val: length value
returns out: DER length bytes
`
  'x509.der-enc-null': `
Encode DER NULL (content always empty)
out: the two bytes 05 00
`
  'x509.der-enc-octetstring': `
Wrap as an OCTET STRING
data: raw bytes to embed (algorithm parameters, digests, etc.)
out: DER OCTET STRING with tag=0x04
`
  'x509.der-enc-oid-raw': `
Encode from raw OID content bytes (without tag+length)
raw: OID content bytes (e.g. rsaEncryption = [0x2A, 0x86, 0x48, ...])
returns out: DER OBJECT IDENTIFIER (with tag+length)
`
  'x509.der-enc-printablestring': `
Encode a DER PrintableString (taking the string's bytes verbatim as content)
s: text to encode (the caller must guarantee printable ASCII within the PrintableString character set)
out: DER TLV with tag=0x13
`
  'x509.der-enc-sequence': `
Wrap as a SEQUENCE
content: DER encodings of the child nodes concatenated in order (each already with its tag+length)
out: DER SEQUENCE with tag=0x30
`
  'x509.der-enc-set': `
Wrap as a SET
content: DER encodings of the member nodes concatenated
out: DER SET with tag=0x31
`
  'x509.der-enc-tlv': `
Wrap a TLV
tag: tag byte (e.g. 0x02 INTEGER, 0x17 UTCTime)
content: content bytes to wrap (may be empty; the length field is still written when length is 0)
returns out: complete TLV (tag + DER length + content)
`
  'x509.der-enc-utctime': `
Encode a DER UTCTime
s: time text in the form YYMMDDHHMMSSZ (e.g. "240101000000Z")
out: DER TLV with tag=0x17
`
  'x509.der-len': `
Decode a DER length
data: DER-encoded data
pos: offset where the length bytes start (1 byte for the short form; in the long form the low 7 bits of the first byte say how many following bytes encode the length)
len: output length value
adv: number of bytes consumed by the length field itself (1 for the short form, 1 + number-of-length-bytes for the long form; callers skip to the value with pos+adv)
`
  'x509.der-skip': `
Skip one DER TLV element
data: DER encoded data
pos: current position (pointing at the tag byte)
next: output position of the next element
`
  'x509.der-tag': `
Read the DER tag byte
data: DER encoded data, pos: position
tag: output tag value
pos: offset of the tag byte (only data[pos] is read, no advance; the caller moves past it)
`
  'x509.rsa-pkcs1-sign-sha256': `
Sign data with RSA PKCS#1 v1.5
data: data to sign
n: modulus (big-endian)
d: private exponent (big-endian)
returns sig: signature (modulus length)
`
  'x509.sha256-compress': `
SHA-256 single-block compression
Runs 64 rounds over the 16 32-bit message words and adds the result back onto the input state.
w0..w15 i64: the 16 32-bit big-endian words of the current 512-bit block (only the low 32 bits of each word are used)
hin0..hin7 i64: the 8 32-bit hash state words entering this block (initial values of a..h)
h0..h7 i64: the 8 32-bit state words after compression (hin + working variables, already & 0xFFFFFFFF)
`
  'x509.x509-build-name-cn': `
Build a Name (CN only)
cn: commonName
returns out: RDNSequence
`
  'x509.x509-build-rsa-public-key': `
Build the RSAPublicKey DER
n: modulus (big-endian, without a leading 0x00)
e: public exponent
returns out: SEQUENCE { INTEGER n, INTEGER e }
`
  'x509.x509-build-self-signed-cert': `
Build a self-signed X.509 certificate
n: RSA modulus (big-endian)
e: public exponent
d: RSA private exponent (big-endian)
cn: Common Name
returns cert: the complete DER-encoded certificate
`
  'x509.x509-build-spki': `
Build the SubjectPublicKeyInfo
n: modulus
e: public exponent
returns out: SEQUENCE { AlgorithmIdentifier, BIT STRING RSAPublicKey }
`
  'x509.x509-build-tbs': `
Build the TBSCertificate
n: RSA modulus
e: public exponent
cn: certificate CN
not-before: start time
not-after: end time
returns out: TBSCertificate DER
`
  'x509.x509-build-validity': `
Build the Validity
not-before: UTCTime string (e.g. "240101000000Z")
not-after: UTCTime string
returns out: SEQUENCE { UTCTime, UTCTime }
`
  'x509.x509-bytes-to-bn': `
big-endian byte array → []i64 (little-endian base 2^32)
Local copy to avoid a circular dependency with net/tls.no
data: big-endian bytes
returns out: big integer (each element a 32-bit limb)
`
  'x509.x509-fingerprint': `
x509-fingerprint: compute the SHA-256 fingerprint of an X.509 certificate
That is the SHA-256 hash of the certificate's TBSCertificate substructure (not of the whole DER).
First locates the TBSCertificate bytes with x509-tbs-range, feeds them to sha256.sha256,
then combines the 32-byte hash into 8 big-endian 32-bit words for the return.
data: the certificate's full DER bytes
h0: highest 32-bit word of the fingerprint (big-endian, 0 when the TBSCertificate cannot be located)
h1: the 2nd 32-bit word in descending order
h2: the 3rd 32-bit word in descending order
h3: the 4th 32-bit word in descending order
h4: the 5th 32-bit word in descending order
h5: the 6th 32-bit word in descending order
h6: the 7th 32-bit word in descending order
h7: the lowest 32-bit word (all zeros mean the certificate has no parsable TBSCertificate)
`
  'x509.x509-rsa-e': `
Extract the RSA public exponent (usually 65537)
data: DER encoded certificate
e: output exponent value (0 = not RSA or parse failure)
`
  'x509.x509-rsa-n': `
Extract the RSA public modulus (big-endian byte array)
data: DER encoded certificate
returns out: modulus bytes (big-endian, without a leading 0x00)
`
  'x509.x509-serial': `
Extract the certificate serial number (when it fits in an i64)
data: DER encoded certificate
serial: output serial number (0 = cannot extract)
`
  'x509.x509-tbs-range': `
Find the byte range of the TBSCertificate
data: DER encoded certificate
start: output TBS start position (including the SEQUENCE tag)
end: output TBS end position (exclusive, i.e. the start of the next element)
`
  'xz': `
xz — XZ / LZMA compression and decompression (pure Nolang implementation)

Implements the LZMA2 decompression algorithm, supporting:
- .xz format decompression (XZ container + LZMA2 stream)
- .lzma format decompression (legacy LZMA alone format)

usage:
data = fs.read-file('example.xz')    ; read the xz file
decompressed = xz.decompress(data)    ; decompress xz format

data = fs.read-file('example.lzma')  ; read the lzma file
decompressed = lzma-decompress(data)  ; decompress legacy lzma format

algorithm reference:
- LZMA specification: https://www.7-zip.org/sdk.html (LZMA SDK)
- XZ format: https://tukaani.org/xz/xz-file-format.txt
`
  'xz.compress': `
Compresses data in xz format (basic implementation)
data: raw data ([]byte)
out: xz-compressed data ([]byte)
note: currently uses a basic implementation with stored blocks;
full LZMA2 compression is left for a later implementation.
`
  'xz.decompress': `
.xz format:
- Stream Header: 6 bytes magic + 2 bytes stream flags
- Blocks: Block Header + Compressed Data + Block Padding + Check
- Index
- Stream Footer: 12 bytes
`
  'xz.lzma-decoder': 'lzma-decoder: LZMA decoder'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.decode': `
LZMA decoding main loop
data: compressed data, start-pos: data start position
unpacked-size: expected decompressed size (-1 = unknown)
start-pos: offset in data where LZMA stream decoding begins
`
  'xz.lzma-decoder.decode-distance': `
Decode a match distance
length: match length (used to pick the distance encoding)
dist: match distance (byte offset back from the current write position)
`
  'xz.lzma-decoder.decode-length': `
Decode a match length
probs: probability array, returns the length
length: decoded match length (combining selector and low/aligned bits)
`
  'xz.lzma-decoder.decode-literal': 'Decode a literal'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.init-probs': 'Initialize all probabilities to 0x400 (even chance)'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.update-state-literal': 'Update the state (after a literal)'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.update-state-match': 'Update the state (after a match)'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.update-state-rep': 'Update the state (after a repeat match)'
  'xz.lzma-decoder.update-state-shortrep': 'Update the state (after a short repeat)'
  'xz.lzma-decompress': `
.lzma format:
- 1 byte: properties (lc, lp, pb encoding)
- 4 bytes: dict size (little-endian)
- 8 bytes: unpacked size (little-endian, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF = unknown)
- compressed data
`
  'xz.lzma-eof-sym': 'LZMA decoding constant'
  'xz.lzma-num-states': 'LZMA probability model size'
  'xz.lzma-rd': 'lzma-range-decoder: LZMA arithmetic range decoder'
  'xz.lzma-rd.decode-bit': `
Decode one bit
prob: probability value (0-0x7FFF), returns the bit and the updated prob
bit: decoded bit (0 or 1)
new-prob: adaptively updated probability value (for the next decode)
`
  'xz.lzma-rd.decode-bit-tree': `
Decode a bit tree
num-bits: tree depth, probs: probability array, returns the symbol
probs: probability array of the bit tree nodes
sym: symbol value decoded by walking the tree bit by bit to the leaf
`
  'xz.lzma-rd.decode-direct-bits': `
Decode n direct bits
num-bits: number of direct bits to decode (bypassing the probability model)
res: raw integer value assembled from num-bits bits
`
  'xz.lzma-rd.decode-reverse-bit-tree': `
Decode a reverse bit tree
probs: probability array of the bit tree nodes
num-bits: tree depth (bits of the decoded symbol)
sym: symbol value decoded walking the tree in reverse (low bit to high bit)
`
  'xz.lzma-rd.init': `
Initialize the range decoder
data: LZMA compressed byte stream to decode
start-pos: byte offset in data where decoding starts
`
  'xz.lzma-rd.normalize': 'Range decoder normalization'
  'xz.lzma2-decompress-stream': `
Decompress an LZMA2 stream
LZMA2 uses control bytes to split chunks:
0x00 = end
0x01 = uncompressed with dictionary reset (with 4-byte dictionary id)
0x02 = uncompressed without dictionary reset
0x80-0xBF = LZMA compressed (possibly with dictionary reset)
out is a named result parameter (out-parameter syntax sugar): the call site binds the receiving variable on the LHS.
The body accumulates into a local res first and delivers out = res at the end (the same pattern as
inflate-decompress in gzip.no); declaring out as an ordinary input parameter would make it a read-only
reference, so pushed results could never reach the caller.
data: the LZMA2 stream including control bytes
start-pos: starting offset of the stream
out: decompressed plaintext byte stream (named output parameter, delivered once at the end)
`
  'xz.xz-magic': 'XZ constants'
  'yaml': `
yaml — YAML 1.2 parsing and generation

Supports the full node model of the YAML 1.2 core schema:
- block mapping / block sequence (indentation nesting, mapping in sequence, sequence in mapping)
- flow collections [a, b] / {k: v} (may span lines)
- scalars: plain, single-quoted, double-quoted (with escapes), block scalars | and > (with chomping / indentation indicators)
- comments, blank lines, document markers --- / ... , multi-document, %YAML / %TAG directives
- anchors &a / aliases *a, explicit tags !!str / !<...>, merge key <<
- explicit key ? key / : value
- core schema type inference: null / bool / int (decimal, 0x, 0o, _ separator) / float (.inf/.nan/exponent)

usage:
doc ?yaml = yaml.parse(source)
doc: {
ok -> {
host, found = it.find-str('server.host')
port, found = it.find-i64('server.port')
}
}

note:
- Aliases are resolved at parse time into new nodes sharing the same subtree (read-only), safe for repeated reads;
self-referential anchors are not expanded infinitely; serialization truncates at a depth limit.
- When keys are scalars they can be accessed directly via dot paths; for non-scalar keys use key(i) / value(i) to iterate.
`
  'yaml.anchor': `
The anchor name of the current node
a: the node's anchor name (empty = none)
`
  'yaml.apply-tag': `
determines the node type by an explicit tag
tag: normalized tag name
kind: node kind inferred from content
k: final kind after tag override (returns the original kind when the tag is unrecognized)
`
  'yaml.at': `
Get a sequence element / the i-th value of a mapping
i: sequence index / the i-th key/value pair of a mapping (0-based)
out: the corresponding value node (nil=out of range or type mismatch)
`
  'yaml.bool': `
Get the boolean value
val: the boolean value (nil=not a boolean node)
`
  'yaml.doc': `
Get the i-th document
note: child node views are returned as ?yaml for the caller to unwrap with match.
i: document ordinal (0-based)
out: handle of document i (nil=out of range)
`
  'yaml.doc-count': `
The number of documents
n: number of documents contained in this document set
`
  'yaml.emit': `
Convert a node to block-style YAML (without trailing newline)
node-pool: node pool
idx: index of the node to output
indent: number of spaces per indentation level
depth: current nesting depth
out: block-style YAML text (no trailing newline)
`
  'yaml.error': `
This document's parse error (always an empty string when parsing succeeded)
note: on parse failure yaml.parse returns err, so no yaml value is obtained;
For the failure reason use yaml.error-of(source).
msg: error message (always empty when parsing succeeds)
`
  'yaml.error-of': `
parses the source text and returns the error message (empty string on success)
source: YAML source text
msg: parse error message (empty string=success)
`
  'yaml.f-is-inf': `
does not compare against a literal: Nolang numeric literals do not support exponents (`1e308` is split into `1` + `e308`).
infinity is judged as "nonzero and unchanged after halving"; `x != x` is used first to exclude NaN.
x: f64 value to test
yes: x is positive/negative infinity (nonzero and unchanged after halving)
`
  'yaml.f-is-nan': `
Whether a float is NaN (NaN != NaN)
Does not use `float.is-nan`: receiver-style calls within the module are not resolved by the MIR backend.
x: the f64 value to check
yes: x is NaN (NaN != NaN criterion)
`
  'yaml.f64': `
Get the float value
val: the float value (nil=not a float node)
`
  'yaml.find': `
Look up by dotted path (sequences use numeric indices)
path: dot-separated path, e.g. 'a.b.0' (numeric segments index sequences)
out: the node hit by the path (nil=path does not exist)
`
  'yaml.find-bool': `
gets a boolean by path
path: dot-separated path
out: boolean value at the path end (nil=absent or not a boolean)
`
  'yaml.find-f64': `
gets a float by path
path: dot-separated path
out: float value at the path end (nil=absent or not a float)
`
  'yaml.find-i64': `
gets an integer by path
path: dot-separated path
out: integer value at the path end (nil=absent or not an integer)
`
  'yaml.find-str': `
Reads a string at a path
path: dot-separated path
out: string value at the path end (nil=absent or not a string)
`
  'yaml.get': `
Get a mapping value by scalar key
key: the scalar key text to look up
out: the corresponding value node (nil=key absent or not a mapping)
`
  'yaml.get-bool': `
Get a boolean by scalar key
key: the scalar key text to look up
out: boolean of the corresponding value (nil=key absent or not a boolean value)
`
  'yaml.get-f64': `
Get a float by scalar key
key: the scalar key text to look up
out: float of the corresponding value (nil=key absent or not a float value)
`
  'yaml.get-i64': `
Get an integer by scalar key
key: the scalar key text to look up
out: integer of the corresponding value (nil=key absent or not an integer value)
`
  'yaml.get-str': `
Get a string by scalar key
key: the scalar key text to look up
out: string form of the corresponding value (nil=key absent or not a string value)
`
  'yaml.hex-char': `
Convert a single hex digit (0-15) to a character
v: value of the hex digit, 0-15
ch: the corresponding hex character ('0'-'f')
`
  'yaml.hex-digit': `
Converts a hex character to its value; returns -1 for non-hex
c: ASCII code of the character
d: value 0-15 represented by the hex digit (-1=invalid hex character)
`
  'yaml.i64': `
Get the integer value
val: the integer value (nil=not an integer node)
`
  'yaml.inf': `
Positive infinity
v: the positive infinity value
`
  'yaml.is-bool': `
Whether it is a boolean
yes: the node is a boolean (true/false and the YAML alias spellings)
`
  'yaml.is-int': `
Whether it is an integer
yes: the node is an integer
`
  'yaml.is-map': `
Whether it is a mapping
yes: the node is a mapping
`
  'yaml.is-nan': `
yaml.is-float: whether it is a float
Whether the current node is .nan
yes: the node is .nan
`
  'yaml.is-null': `
Whether it is null
yes: the node is null
`
  'yaml.is-num': `
Whether it is a number
yes: the node is an integer or a float
`
  'yaml.is-seq': `
Whether it is a sequence
yes: the node is a sequence
`
  'yaml.is-str': `
Whether it is a string
yes: the node is a string
`
  'yaml.json-escape': `
JSON string escaping
s: original string
out: escaped JSON string (including leading and trailing double quotes)
`
  'yaml.json-of': `
node to JSON
node-pool: node pool
idx: index of the node to convert
depth: remaining recursion depth (guards against alias cycles)
out: JSON text corresponding to the node
`
  'yaml.key': `
Get the text of the i-th key of a mapping
i: key/value pair ordinal (0-based)
out: scalar text of the i-th key (nil=out of range or not a mapping)
`
  'yaml.key-eq': `
whether two key nodes are equivalent (compare scalar text)
a: key node a
b: key node b
yes: the two nodes' scalar text is equal
`
  'yaml.key-node': `
Get the i-th key node of a mapping
i: key/value pair ordinal (0-based)
out: the i-th key node (nil=out of range or not a mapping)
`
  'yaml.key-text-eq': `
Whether scalar key text equals a given string
nd: node to compare (uses its scalar text)
key: target text
yes: nd's scalar text equals key
`
  'yaml.kind': `
The type of the current node
k: the YAML-KIND-* kind constant of that node
`
  'yaml.len': `
Number of sequence elements / mapping entries
n: element count of a sequence or pair count of a mapping (0 for other kinds)
`
  'yaml.line-of': `
The starting line of the current node
n: the line number where the node starts
`
  'yaml.nan': `
must go through runtime division: compile-time constant folding would compute `0.0 / 0.0` directly as 0.
routing through a local variable avoids folding and yields a real NaN (`x != x` is true).
v: NaN value (produced by runtime 0.0/0.0, not constant-folded)
`
  'yaml.node': `
node: tree node
kind   node type (YAML-KIND-*)
style  scalar/collection style (YAML-STYLE-*)
tag    explicit tag text ('' when no tag)
anchor anchor name ('' when no anchor)
text   scalar text (quotes unescaped); empty for collections
ival/fval/bval parsed scalar values
keys   mapping key node indices; kids sequence elements / mapping value node indices
`
  'yaml.node-count': `
total number of nodes
n: total number of nodes in the node pool
`
  'yaml.node-is-nan': '`.nan`\'s fval is filled with a real NaN by nan(), so it is judged by value (NaN != NaN).\nNode raw-text check as fallback: paths such as tag rewriting may change only text without recomputing fval.\nnd: node to check\nyes: the node is .nan'
  'yaml.node-pool': 'node-pool: node pool'
  'yaml.parse': `
parses YAML text and returns the first document
Returns ok(yaml) or err(message)
source: YAML source text
out: handle of the first document (err=parse failure message)
`
  'yaml.parse-all': `
parses all documents
source: YAML source text containing multiple documents
out: document-set handle (access each document via .doc(i))
`
  'yaml.parse-radix': `
Parses a radix-based integer (ignoring _)
t: text containing the number
start: offset where the number starts
radix: base (2/8/16)
kind: success=YAML-KIND-INT, failure=YAML-KIND-STR
v: converted integer value
`
  'yaml.parser': 'parser: parser state (the method receiver is a reference and can be advanced in place)'
  'yaml.parser.advance': 'advance one byte'
  'yaml.parser.anchor-id': `
looks up an anchor, returns -1 if not found
name: anchor name
idx: corresponding node index (-1=not registered)
`
  'yaml.parser.apply-tag-at': 'Nodes are first created by inference via the core schema, and tags are applied afterwards, so a "recompute" step is needed:\nafter a tag rewrites kind, ival/fval/bval must be re-parsed according to the tag\'s requirements, otherwise\n`!!int "42"` would leave kind=INT with ival=0 (`yaml.i64` reads 0 instead of 42).\nCollection nodes (seq/map) keep only the raw tag text and do not change kind — `!!seq` / `!!map` do not alter the\nparse structure, and applying `!!str` to a collection is already ill-formed in YAML.\nidx: node pool index'
  'yaml.parser.at': `
Byte at offset k from the current position
k: offset from the current position (out of range treated as 0)
c: byte value at pos+k
`
  'yaml.parser.at-doc-marker': `
whether the current position is --- (1) or ... (2)
which: 1='---', 2='...', 0=not a document marker
`
  'yaml.parser.at-eol': `
whether at end of line (including EOF)
yes: the current byte is \n or the end of input has been reached
`
  'yaml.parser.byte-at': `
Gets src[i], returns 0 when out of range
i: target index
c: byte value at that position (0 when out of range)
`
  'yaml.parser.cur': `
Current byte
c: byte pointed to by current pos (0 at EOF)
`
  'yaml.parser.eof': `
Whether the end of input is reached
yes: pos has reached the end of input
`
  'yaml.parser.fail': `
record a parse error
msg: error description (stored into the parser error message)
`
  'yaml.parser.fail-at': `
Records a parse error with position
msg: error description (current position info appended automatically)
`
  'yaml.parser.indent-at': `
Skip leading spaces before counting: call sites almost always follow skip-blank-lines() closely,
where pos sits at the start of the line (column 0); a direct subtraction always yields 0, causing nested blocks to be judged as "not indented".
After skipping, pos sits at the first non-space character; the later is-seq-dash / looks-like-map-entry /
at-doc-marker also need this position.
ind: number of leading spaces before the first non-space character of the current line
`
  'yaml.parser.is-seq-dash': `
whether the current position is a sequence entry indicator '- '
yes: the current position is a sequence entry indicator ('- ' or a '-' at end of line)
`
  'yaml.parser.line-end-of': `
Return the end-of-line offset of the line containing i (pointing at the newline or n)
i: any byte offset within the target line
le: end-of-line offset (points at the newline; equals the input length when there is no newline)
`
  'yaml.parser.line-start-of': `
returns the start offset of the line containing i
i: any byte offset within the target line
ls: byte offset of the start of the line containing i
