yes: can be output verbatim as a plain scalar (no quoting needed)
`
  'yaml.quote': `
output a double-quoted YAML scalar
s: original text
out: YAML scalar text after double-quoting and escaping
`
  'yaml.resolve-plain': `
infers the plain scalar type according to the core schema
text: raw text of the plain scalar
kind: inferred node kind (null/bool/int/float/str, etc.)
ival: integer value when kind=INT
fval: float value when kind=FLOAT
bval: boolean value when kind=BOOL
`
  'yaml.scalar-repr': `
YAML representation of a scalar
nd: scalar node
out: YAML representation text chosen by kind and style
`
  'yaml.scalar-text': `
The scalar text of the current node (containers return ok=false)
out: scalar text (nil=container node)
`
  'yaml.scan-number': `
Tries to parse the text as an integer or a float
returns kind = YAML-KIND-INT / YAML-KIND-FLOAT / YAML-KIND-STR (not a number)
t: scalar text
kind: recognized number type (INT/FLOAT/STR)
ival: integer value when kind=INT
fval: float value when kind=FLOAT
`
  'yaml.source-of': `
The original input text
out: the original input text at parse time
`
  'yaml.spaces': `
generates n spaces
n: number of spaces
out: string of n spaces
`
  'yaml.str': `
Get the string value (numbers/booleans return their raw text)
val: the scalar raw text (nil=container node)
`
  'yaml.str-at': `
why this helper is needed: `arr[i]` (where arr is []str) returns option<str> by default,
and the MIR backend produces invalid IR when option<str> is used as a call argument / method receiver
(%option_str does not match %str-long). String concatenation auto-unwraps option,
so here we use `out = out - a[i]` to fetch the element, which both unwraps and satisfies the `no vet` bounds check.
a: target string array
i: element index
out: value of the i-th element (returns empty string when out of bounds)
`
  'yaml.stringify': `
Alias for to-yaml
out: same output as to-yaml
`
  'yaml.strip-bom': `
Strip a leading UTF-8 BOM
s: text that may carry a BOM
out: text with the leading EF BB BF removed
`
  'yaml.style': `
The style of the current node
s: that node's YAML-STYLE-* style constant
`
  'yaml.tag': `
The explicit tag of the current node
t: the node's explicit tag (empty string=none)
`
  'yaml.tag-name': `
Normalize a tag name (!!str / tag:yaml.org,2002:str → str)
tag: raw tag (forms such as !!str / tag:yaml.org,2002:str)
name: normalized short tag name (str/int/seq...)
`
  'yaml.text': `
The scalar raw text of the current node
t: scalar raw text (empty string for container nodes)
`
  'yaml.to-f64': `
text to f64 (returns nil to indicate failure)
t: numeric text
v: conversion result (nil=cannot be parsed as f64)
`
  'yaml.to-i64': `
Text to i64, returns -1 on failure
s: integer text
v: converted value (-1=conversion failed)
`
  'yaml.to-json': `
Convert the current document to JSON
out: JSON text of the current document's root node
`
  'yaml.to-json-all': `
Convert all documents into a JSON array
out: JSON array text of all documents
`
  'yaml.to-yaml': `
Re-output the current document as YAML
out: YAML text of the current document re-emitted
`
  'yaml.unescape-double': `
Handles double-quoted escape sequences
body: string content inside the double quotes (including escape sequences)
out: unescaped text (nil=contains an invalid escape)
`
  'yaml.valid': `
Whether this document parsed successfully (getting a yaml value means success)
yes: the handle carries a successfully parsed document
`
  'yaml.valid-of': `
Whether the source text can be parsed (without building a document)
source: the YAML source text to validate
yes: the source text can be parsed
`
  'yaml.value': `
Get the value node of the i-th key of a mapping
i: key/value pair ordinal (0-based)
out: value node of the i-th key pair (nil=out of range or not a mapping)
`
  'yaml.yaml': 'yaml: parsed document (may contain multiple documents)'
  'yaml.yaml-chomp-clip': 'chomping mode'
  'yaml.yaml-kind-null': 'node type'
  'yaml.yaml-max-depth': 'maximum recursion depth for serialization (guards against self-referential anchors)'
  'yaml.yaml-style-block': 'collection style'
  'yaml.yaml-style-plain': 'scalar style'
  'yaml.yaml.is-float': `
Determines whether it is a float
yes: the node is a float
`
  'zip': `
zip — ZIP archive parsing

Reads the ZIP file format, lists entries, and extracts contents.
Supports extraction of stored (method 0) and deflate (method 8) entries.

usage:
archive = zip{
data: raw-bytes
}
n = archive.count()
i <- [0..n): {
e = archive.entry(i)
name = e.name()
sz = e.size()
content = e.extract()
}
`
  'zip.entry': 'entry: ZIP entry (points to a central directory entry in archive.data)'
  'zip.entry.compressed-size': `
Reads the compressed size of an entry
csz: number of bytes after compression
`
  'zip.entry.extract': `
extracts entry contents (stored and deflate)
out: decompressed raw bytes (stored copies directly, deflate auto-inflates)
`
  'zip.entry.method': `
reads the entry's compression method (0=stored, 8=deflate)
method: compression method (0=stored, 8=deflate)
`
  'zip.entry.name': `
reads the entry filename
name: the entry's filename
`
  'zip.entry.size': `
Reads the entry's original size
sz: original byte count before compression
`
  'zip.u16le': `
Read a 2-byte little-endian unsigned integer
data: byte array to read from
off: start position (0-based)
v: the 2-byte little-endian unsigned integer (0 ~ 65535)
`
  'zip.u32le': `
Read a 4-byte little-endian unsigned integer
data: ZIP file byte array
off: start position (0-based)
v: the 4-byte little-endian unsigned integer (0 ~ 2^32-1)
`
  'zip.writer.add-dir': `
Adds a directory entry
name: directory name (a trailing / is added automatically if missing)
`
  'zip.writer.add-file': `
adds a file to the archive (CRC32 computed automatically)
name: path name stored in the archive
content: file content (stored verbatim with the stored method, CRC32 computed automatically)
`
  'zip.writer.cd-put': `
Append a byte to cd-data
b: byte value to append (low 8 bits written to the central directory buffer)
`
  'zip.writer.cd-write-u16': `
Writes a 2-byte little-endian integer to the central directory
v: integer to write (low 16 bits appended to the central directory buffer in little-endian order)
`
  'zip.writer.cd-write-u32': `
Writes a 4-byte little-endian integer into the central directory
v: the integer to write (the low 32 bits appended to the central directory buffer in little-endian order)
`
  'zip.writer.finalize': `
Write the central directory and EOCD, returning the complete archive data
out: complete ZIP archive bytes (local data + central directory + EOCD)
`
  'zip.writer.put': `
append one byte to data (supports push via a local variable)
b: byte value to append (low 8 bits written into the local data area)
`
  'zip.writer.write-cd-entry': `
Writes a central directory entry
name: file name
offset: starting offset of this entry's local file header
size: uncompressed size
compressed-size: compressed size
crc: CRC32 checksum of the content
`
  'zip.writer.write-eocd': `
Writes the end record (End of Central Directory)
cd-offset: starting offset of the central directory
cd-size: total byte count of the central directory
`
  'zip.writer.write-local-file': `
Writes the local file header and content
name: file name (written into the header and data area)
content: file content (uncompressed, written as-is stored)
crc: CRC32 checksum of the content
`
  'zip.writer.write-u16': `
writes a 2-byte little-endian integer
v: integer to write (low 16 bits appended in little-endian order)
`
  'zip.writer.write-u32': `
Write a 4-byte little-endian integer
v: integer to write (low 32 bits appended in little-endian order)
`
  'zip.zip': 'zip: read-only view of a ZIP archive'
  'zip.zip.compressed-size': `
Compressed size
idx: entry ordinal
csz: compressed byte count (same as the original when stored)
`
  'zip.zip.count': `
Return the number of entries in the ZIP
count: total entries (0 when the archive is corrupt)
`
  'zip.zip.entry': `
Get the idx-th entry
idx: entry ordinal
e: entry handle bound to the entry's offset (fields read via its methods)
`
  'zip.zip.entry-off': `
Offset of the idx-th central directory entry
idx: entry ordinal (0 ~ total-1)
off: absolute offset of that central directory entry within the file data
`
  'zip.zip.extract': `
Extract the idx-th entry's content (stored and deflate)
idx: entry ordinal
out: decompressed raw bytes (stored copied directly, deflate inflated automatically)
`
  'zip.zip.find-eocd': `
Search backward from the end for the EOCD signature
off: offset of the EOCD signature (-1 when not found)
`
  'zip.zip.get-cd-off': `
Read the central directory offset (single return value, avoiding a multi-value compiler bug)
cd-off: starting offset of the central directory (0 when there is no EOCD)
`
  'zip.zip.get-total': `
Read the entry count (single return value)
total: total entries in the archive (0 when there is no EOCD)
`
  'zip.zip.method': `
Compression method (0=stored, 8=deflate)
idx: entry ordinal
method: compression method (0=stored uncompressed, 8=deflate)
`
  'zip.zip.name': `
File name of the idx-th entry
idx: entry ordinal
name: file name (may contain a / separated relative path)
`
  'zip.zip.size': `
Original size
idx: entry ordinal
sz: original byte count before compression
`
  'zlib': `
zlib — RFC 1950 zlib compression and decompression (pure Nolang implementation)

Git stores every loose object and every pack-object payload as a zlib
stream: a 2-byte header, raw DEFLATE data, and a 4-byte big-endian
Adler-32 checksum of the uncompressed data.

supports:
- zlib compression: builds a valid zlib stream using stored blocks (BTYPE=00)
- zlib decompression: supports stored blocks and full DEFLATE via gzip.inflate-decompress

usage:
data = fs.read-file('blob')          ; read as []byte
compressed = compress(data)     ; compress to zlib format
decompressed, ok = decompress(compressed)  ; decompress zlib format

algorithm reference: RFC 1950 (zlib), RFC 1951 (DEFLATE)
`
  'zlib.adler-32': `
Compute the Adler-32 checksum
data: input data ([]byte)
n: number of bytes actually included in the computation (i64)
returns sum: 32-bit checksum (i64), b in the high half and a in the low half, comparable with RFC 1950 implementations
`
  'zlib.compress': `
Build a zlib stream using stored blocks (BTYPE=00):
- 2-byte zlib header (78 9c)
- DEFLATE stored blocks (each block up to 65535 bytes)
- 4-byte big-endian Adler-32 checksum
out: complete zlib stream (2-byte header + stored blocks + 4-byte Adler-32)
`
  'zlib.consumed-stored': `
Compute the number of consumed bytes of a stored-block zlib stream
data: zlib stream starting at offset 0 ([]byte)
returns consumed: total consumed bytes (including header and Adler-32), 0 means a non-stored block
Accumulate per block: 5-byte block header + data length + 4-byte checksum; stop at BFINAL or a non-stored block
`
  'zlib.decompress': `
Supports all DEFLATE block types (stored, fixed Huffman, dynamic Huffman)
Decompresses via gzip.inflate-decompress.
First tries stored-block scanning (fast path); on failure uses inflate decompression.
out: decompressed data (ok); nil when length < 6, the header is invalid, or decompression fails
`
  'zlib.decompress-stored': `
decompresses only stored-block zlib streams
data: zlib stream containing only BTYPE=00 blocks ([]byte)
returns out: decompressed data (some), nil for non-stored blocks or failure
out: bytes decoded only from BTYPE=00 blocks; nil if another block type or a mismatched trailer is encountered
`
  'zstd': `
zstd — Zstandard compression and decompression (pure Nolang implementation)

implements the Zstandard (zstd) decompression algorithm, including:
- FSE (Finite State Entropy) decoding
- Huffman decoding
- LZ77 sequence decoding
- Zstandard frame format parsing

usage:
data = fs.read-file('example.zst')   ; read zst file
decompressed = decompress(data)  ; decompress zstd format

algorithm references:
- Zstandard format: https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc8878
- FSE: https://github.com/Cyan4973/FiniteStateEntropy
`
  'zstd.bits-for-count': `
Compute the number of bits needed for count
count > 0: returns the significant-bit count of count (floor(log2(count)) + 1)
count <= 0: returns 0
count: frequency of a symbol in the normalized FSE table
n: minimum bits required for count (callers use tableLog - n to get the extra bits of that state)
`
  'zstd.compress': `
compresses data using the zstd format (basic implementation)
data: raw data ([]byte)
out: zstd-compressed data ([]byte)
note: currently a basic implementation using raw blocks;
full FSE + Huffman + LZ77 compression is left for a later implementation.
`
  'zstd.decompress': `
Decompresses Zstandard-format data
data: zstd compressed data ([]byte)
out: decompressed data ([]byte)
`
  'zstd.fse-table': 'fse-table: FSE decode table'
  'zstd.fse-table.build': `
Build the FSE table from normalized counts
norm-count: normalized count of each symbol
max-symbol: maximum symbol value
table-log: log2 of the table size
`
  'zstd.fse-table.decode': `
Decode one FSE symbol
returns the symbol and the new state
br: bit reader used to fetch the extra bits for the state's transition to the next generation
state: current FSE decoding state (row index into the symbol table)
sym: symbol corresponding to the current state
new-state: state after transitioning to the next generation (next-state[state] plus the extra bits read)
`
  'zstd.zs-bit-reader.align-to-byte': 'Align to the next byte boundary'
  'zstd.zs-bit-reader.init': `
initializes
data: compressed byte stream to decode (read-only, not modified)
pos: byte offset to start reading from (the decoder enters the bit stream here)
`
  'zstd.zs-bit-reader.read-bit': `
Read one bit (LSB-first)
bit: the bit read (0 or 1); 0 when the data is exhausted
`
  'zstd.zs-bit-reader.read-bits': `
Read n bits (LSB-first)
n: number of bits to read (assembled into the integer LSB first, bit by bit)
v: integer assembled from the n bits read
`
  'zstd.zs-bit-reader.read-varint': `
Read a variable-length integer (LSB-first, 7 bits per group)
v: decoded integer value (low 7 bits of each byte significant, high bit 1 means more bytes follow)
`
  'zstd.zs-frame': 'zs-frame: Zstandard frame decode state'
  'zstd.zs-frame.decode-block': `
Decode one block (returns last-block: some = success carrying whether it is the final block, nil = failure)
last-block: tri-state result — ok(true) final block, ok(false) intermediate block both decoded successfully; nil means a truncated block header or a reserved block type
`
  'zstd.zs-frame.decode-compressed-block': `
Decode a compressed block
block-size: compressed block size
ok: true when the compressed block's literals and sequences sections parse and expand successfully (decoded bytes appended to out)
`
  'zstd.zs-frame.read-frame-header': `
Read the frame header
ok: true when the magic number validates and the header fields parse completely (pos then advanced to the first block's start)
`
  'zstd.zs-huffman': 'zs-huffman: zstd Huffman decode table'
  'zstd.zs-huffman.build': `
Build the Huffman decoding table
lengths: Huffman code length table per symbol (lengths determine the code distribution weights)
max-symbol: upper bound of symbol values (end of the lengths traversal; 255 for zstd byte symbols)
`
  'zstd.zs-huffman.decode': `
Decode one symbol
br: bit reader supplying the code bits (the codeword assembled bit by bit from the MSB end)
sym: decoded symbol value; 0 when no code matches within the maximum code length
`
  'zstd.zs-magic': 'Zstandard constants'
}
